        <p>r</p>
        <p>SUNDAY CARS. Sir,—l am sure many of us feel obliged to the deputation which waited upon the Tramway Committee in providing us at this time of the year with ji "Religious Tramway Comedy." It was so seasonable! Personally, lam somewhat astonished at the modesty of their request in Only requiring the running of the trams, with .the provision of tramway drivers and 'con- ductors on Sunday morning. Why did they not ask to be furnished with velvet seats, rugs, and footwarmers. in addition? Surely such zeal in their desire to pursue a heavenly course should not go without some earthly reward. E. ENGLAND. 1, Mill-street, December 27th, 1913.</p>
        <p>• Sir,—lt was with much regret that I learnt of the Tramways Committee's decision to rescind their resolution to give their drivers and conductors a full day's rest every alternate gundaY, and, with many others of the rate- payers of Warrington, I sincerely hope that the committee will yet see its way to do what is right in the matter. I can scarcely think that the reason given, that the trams are necessary to take people to L place of worship, can be logical; since the aim 'Of such worshippers surely should be to reduce Sunday labour to a minimum, and not to com- pel a body of men to neglect the Fourth Com- mandment, and effectually prevent them from attending Divine Service, in order that they themselves might attend with greater ease and comfort, though undoubtedly with far less bene- ficial results, since it is at th 6 expense of others. (REV). W. 11. COCK. Palmyra-square, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE AND THE SUPPLYING OF REPORTS.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE AND THE SUPPLY- ING OF REPORTS.</p>
        <p>-- • Sir,—May I thank Mr. Gray for his belated disclaimer of his insinuation that " the National Service League, or any other organise, tion in this country, paid for the cost of cabling out Lord Roberts's speech"? All of us who know Mr. Gray's earnestness expected him to withdraw from the untenable position implied in his original letter. Even his informant, the allooymous Quaker official, apparently throws him over, for his statement, as quoted by Mr. GIaY, says nothing of "cabling," which would Cost many pounds, but only speaks of "sending" _:;!ports, which would be covered by a penny taimP• Now Mr. Gray has withdrawn his sug- gestion against the NatiOnal Service League, I ladl- accept his explanation. B. S. TOWNROE. Newton-le-Willows, December 31st, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ALCOHOL PRIDE AND AMBITION.</head>
        <p>ALCOHOL PRIDE AND AMBITION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>Sit, —I thank Mr. Leslie for his kindly refer- ence to my letters, and with your permission I will state very broadly' the general principles which -underly my attitude towards the liquor -question. A • r place alcoholic liquor in the same category air pride, ambition, the desire for wealth, and the love of dress, all good things and beneficial 'when rightfully used; fully admitting that all . , are capable ef producing much misery when i abused, just as marriage may be the greatest , blessing or curse that affecti a man or woman's life. \ All the things I have Mentioned are con- tfolled by law and circumstance to a certain degree, but there is a large margin of individual free-will and choice, and I would have that margin of choice remain. Ido not believe in " virtue" that is the result of compulsion; it is merely the meanest form of slavery. Sobriety, not in the one thing alone, but in all things as the result of free choice, the product of self- respect and personal responsibility, ig the only sobriety worth haying. Under present conditions the individual may use or abstain as he pleases, and I for one deny ! the right of Mr. Leslie or any number associated with him to convel total abstinence, for that is really the claim made by the prohibitionist. Mr. Leslie admits you cannot cure the ake drunkard, but he pleads he should be allowed under Local Veto or Local Prohibition to it as difficult for the " casual " (I think the proper word is moderate or reasonable) drinker to please himself, hnd gratify his taste, if he so desires. I think that is an [ unreasonable and, if Mr. Leslie will forgive me saying so, an insolent demand. ' Surely he will see that he is arrogating to himself the claim to set up a standard of per [,,, ; sonal taste and conduct that he has no shadow kt: of right to do. The attitude" is one of "What I ' think is right and proper must be right and proper for you." This is the end , of' all per- sonal liberty, and I believe with John Stuart :,. Mill that : "The limitation in number of beer and spirit I houses, for' the express purpose of rendering them more difficult of access, and diminishing the occasions of temptation, not only exposek. ki; all to an inconvenience because there are some '. by whom the facility would be abused, but is</p>
        <p>The effect of Eyestrain on the Nervous System and consequently on the General Health, is serious.</p>
        <p>necessitates the greatest care in the selection of Spectacles to give relief, and the Importance of Sight-testing cannot be over-es- timated.</p>
        <p>Our Optical Experience extends over 25 years and we have every confidence in recommending our system of sight-testing,</p>
        <p>72, Sankey Street</p>
        <p>(next to Winmarleigh Cafe),</p>
        <p>December 23rd, 1913.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN SCIENCE TEACHING. Sir,—There is a newness and simplicity about Christian Science teaching that one cannot help being drawn towards It. Its simplicity, is in its vocabulary. Such compound words as " all- power," " all-presence," " all-science," and what ought to be compound words, " allness of spirit," "divine mind," "mortal mind," "carnal mind," are so self-explanatory. Its newness is in its teaching. Mr. Harwood tells us that " Christian Science is just the teaching of Chlist Jesus understood and demonstrated." Which teaching, Luke xiv. 26, or I. John iii. 15? All the teaching, or just which suits?</p>
        <p>The Bible contains so many contradictions, and Christians know so little of its contents, that one is not so prone to believe that Chris- tian Scientists can make it so beautifully simple to understand as your correspondent would have us believe._</p>
        <p>- If Mr. Harwood has something to offer, I am prepared to read it, but let him rid his- letter of Arimmiygs, please.</p>
        <p>IPSE DIXIT.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS AND STANDARD</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS AND STANDARD</p>
        <p>WAGES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, "Freer Lance," seems to find food for amusement in the above subject. He describes my figures about the rents, &amp;c., as "lumping" it. Yet his inability to give us any different figures proves that the "lumps" ate hard facts.". He also accuses me of wandering from the above subjects, yet immediately after he quotes a statement which appeared- soine months ago, under the heading " Motor cars and their Dangers." This quota- tion he "twists" to suit his own purpose. The word "certificate" never appeared in the original. There is a vast difference in the meaning of the word "licence" (which Was the original quotation) and "certificate." The former is simply a duty payable to the revenue authorities for " permission " to drive a car. The latter is proof that you are capable of driv- ing a car, and can only be got by learning first and demonstrating afterwards. He also suffers under the illusion that I am up against anyone earning an extra shilling. Nothing of the sort. If he had taken the trouble to digest my letter, before rushing into print, he would easily have seen that I simply claimed that a tradesman who infringed on another man's trade (even if it was only a spare time(?) occupation) could not be a real . .</p>
        <p>trade unionist. I would advise "Freer Lance," when he quotes anything, to give the correct quotation, as one word can alter the whole meaning; also (if he honestly believes in his own views) to adopt a distinctive nom-de-pluine, so that your readers may really understand the points a. issue.</p>
        <p>FREE LANCE. TheiWall, December 31, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>• UNITED IRISH LEAGUERS WAVING LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>• UNITED IRISH LEAGUERS WAVING LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the recent meeting addressed by Mr. P. Kelly, at the United Irish League Rooms (wherever they are), the Chairman is reported to have said "The Councillors (County) in Ireland were returned by ballot, and if Mr. Smith's state- ment was true that there were only 16 Pro- testant County Councillors in Ireland, there was an overwhelming majority in favour of Home Rule." As member of the United Irish League, Mr. Kelly would know full well that the ballot in Ireland is subject to wide interference, as the Louth election proved, that the County Coun- cils elections are run on lines that absolutely, exact from voters allegiance to the League's orders. Mr. Redmond himself says: "We have in Ireland an organisation which is practically a governmento the Dublincou n,t r3a-. greTathereofficeismainn- tig Joseph by the real Chief Secretary for Ireland, _e n Devlin, the member for Belfast." vigorously does this League operate that in spite of Mr. Redmond's promise in the House of Commons in 1898 when County Coun- cils were given to Ireland, t repre- sentation to Protestants, thereogive fair are outside Ulster only, 15 Protestant total of 703. Councillors out of a The network of League branches throughout three-Parts of Ireland produces a social la which saps the free will and liberty moafrlte, country population. The mot strenuous coer- cion is resorted to for the purpose of exacting subscriptions to the League, in spite of which had it not been for the support from America, Mr. Redmond says the representation in Par- liament of the Nationalists could not have lasted six months. What are the facts about appointments? It must be remembered that County Councils have only been in existence since 1898, when the power of making appointments was handed over to them, having previously appertained to Grand Juries. Those who held appointments at that date have retained their posts, and when vacancies occur 'their successors are appointed by the Councils. Now what are the facts since 1898? Out of a list of 20 Councils lying before me, 14 have made no Protestant appointments since 1898. Of the rest, Cavan, two veterinary surgeons and an assistant secretary are Pro- testants; Clare, one assistant road surveyor; Galway, one Protestant veterinary surgeon (no other applicant); Kildare, technical instructors nominated by State Department; Limerick, a mechanical engineer; Wicklow, a technical in- structor recommended by the Board of Agricul- ture. It will be seen that of Protestants appointed in this list, the bulk were specialists, where there .was no competition. Of the 33 salaried officers in the Cork Cor- poration two only are Protestants, while in the Cork County Councils no Protestants have been appointed since 1898, although the Protestants number about 10 to 12 per cent. of the popula- tion. These facts are quite sufficient, I should imagine, to show that Nationalists are as intol- erant in respect of officials, as they are in regard to County Council representation. G. W. S. JARRETT, The Primrose League Prov. Secretary for Lancashire, Cheshire and North Wales. Hale, Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 take up my pen again with a very ;tumble, sort of feeling that 1, so ignorant, have dared to enter into a discussion with one so richly endowed with knowledge. I merely asked a few questions with becoming deference to Mr. Smith's superior intelligence, and very diffi- dently made a few suggestions, which I thought might enable him to steer a straight course, as his appeared to have become somewhat tortuous. In response, I get part of a reply, some abuse, and plenty of insinuation, and it makes me wonder whether I was quite wise in joining issue with a mind so intelligent, or whether his mind has unfortunately altered the size of its receptacle. Mr. Smith's method of correspondence rather suggests a modern Don Quixote. He tilts here and slashes there, evidently in the belief that he is performing prodigies of valour, but all the time he is creating a wilderness of his own words, in which it is almost impossible to follow him' He appears also to be lacking in a sense I of humour; not that I would suggest for one Moment that the subject he is in such deadly earnest about is one which lends itself to humour, but he taunts me with having written a lengthy letter, which, after all, only occupied a little more than half a column, and was largely made up in re-stating various aspects of his previous letters, and then writes a letter himself three times as long. In regard to the charge of five per cent. on £lB for paving, my suggestion was reasonable enough, and the only thing at fault was his loose way of presenting his case, which led me to take his figures as an inclusive charge. But why, instead of wasting his time writing about that of which he knows nothing, and making silly guesses at my thoughts, of which, by the way, I have not yet made him a confidant, did he not meet the charge a second time repeated, that seeing that this item of .118 becomes a permanent asset in the value of the propertY, it is a fair proposition that the tenant be charged with only half the amount? Appar- ently he was too busy working in his idea of " something for nothing," whMh is decidedil attractive, and had not occurred' to me before, but, singularly enough, the only way I can iiinagine which. offers any possibility of obtain- ing it, is by putting' is. 4d. a week on the rent to collect .61 Bs. 6d. a year. What I cannot understand, however, is why he should impute this idea to me. He is utterly wrong in doing so, and the credit must lie' at his own door. . Aupther- feature of Mr. Smith's style of cor- respondence is that he is prone to misquoting to support his nebulous way of argument. It is, I believe, an old trick of the wily corre- spondent, but may I suggest that Mr. Smith leave his tricks at home for a while, and, if he is out to convert the unrepentant, that he deal with the matter in a manner worthy of one presumably possessed of so much intelligence, without a lot of useless rhetoric thrown in by way of packing? For instance, I never made , use of the statement, "I am interested in the, . rating and the assessment of house property.' My interest in rating is more comprehensive than that, and is from a more detached point of view than Mr. Smith's can possibly be. Hence my reference to Mr. LlOyd George, who has seen the folly of the present system and is taking</p>
        <p>steps accordingly. Neither did I make the statement, "If MT. Smith can double the output by working night and day there are lots of firms who would be glad to engage him," &amp;c. I mentioned "treble the output," his statement in a previous letter, not "double the output," and I asked him where and how output was being trebled in this '.manner, but instead of answering he Nicks down to the word "double," j and writes a soliloquy on ability and influence, which seems to say that his abilities have been oadly missed. He may be right in this, but if he can show how running night'and day will even double output, he need not fear that lack of influence will stand in the way if he should apply for a job. But first he will have to show his credentials, and I am afraid they will Wive to be more to the point than his letters on "Increasing Rents." However, rather than answer questions based on his own remarks, he finds it easier to cate- chise your readers with ridiculous questions as to whether manufacturers run night and day in order that the worker may take eight hours to do what can be done in four. No one sug- gested they did, and only Mr. Smith would think of anything so funny. It is just about on a par with his statement that running night and day trebles output, and before chiding me with ignorance he should be sure that his own state- ments bear the stamp of truth. Then there is another matter Mr. Smith has overlooked. In referring to his idea that his tenants should ask their employers to pay them more wages, so that they can pay him what he chooses to demand in rent, I reminded him of his statement that £70,000 worth of PrOPertY had been "grabbed," and asked him if the manufacturers had done the grabbing. I sug- gested that if they had not, he might make some endeavour to persuade those who had to foot , his bill, seeing that he made the statement by .way of complaint. But, no! He has evidently found an easier way, and will not be bothered. In passing, may I say that I am not so fortu- nate as Mr. Smith to receive balance sheets, and cannot therefore oblige him with one, but I venture the statement that many manufac- turers honestly consider their works over-rated, and can give reasons just as potent as those of Mr. Smith for doing so. Neither do I own property and land. If ever I do, then will be' the time to talk about protests from me, but seeing that no one is compelled to be either a property owner or builder, I shall certainly make an •effort not to whine about the manu- facturer. And, in conclusion, I must explain that I did not use the Chancellor's name as a taunt to the building section of society. I merely presumed to suggest it as a ray of hope to the depressed spirits of Mr. Smith, and did not realise that in doing so I was addressing the whole of the building trade. Here, alas! is another display of ignorance by A. HORNBY RATHBONE. Latchford Without, December 31st, 1913.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Smith is so evidently in earnest it seems a pity he should be put to so much trouble and expense in such a poor cause. It would also have been much better if he had called in his accountant's certificate earlier on, for it explains, in spite of the mix up of rateable and unrateable charges, hidden behind as it were, much that Mr. Smith intended to convey to your readers. So well did he mix up his figures and charges that I confess I failed to grasp his meaning. However, so far, he and I have arrived at the common conclusion, that :while it is possible to recover increases of rates s Without unduly robbing the tenant, when the tenant. palpable, landlord's pi:ivate account for paving, &amp;c., it can only be recovered at a cost of 50 per cent. Just sooo,mmbuittwhentpxv thehy injusticeaot t try is o some other method of recovering the tenant's share? Has any other method ever been tried at all? •</p>
        <p>mlt n th been authoritiesw hi spperreefdertothminegtshat as thue A they ssaerses-. Perhaps so. Certainly their share in the plun- der must be ft considerable item, even if it is not creditable, and may be the real explana- tion why this gross scandal is allowed to be perpetrated on the poorest class of ratepayers. I have protested all along against tenants being I expected to pay the whole of the paving charges, and have suggested that half the amount only should be saddled on the tenant; but above all, I do protest most strongly against these charges being made a permanent charge on the rent. Now that we know that 18s., or what Mr. Smith chooses to allude to as 5 per cent. on the paving charges, to be recovered through the rent, means something like 455. per, annum to be paid by the tenant, one need not wonder at rents being so high. Mr. Smith calls it an</p>
        <p>A STRIKING EXAMPLE of genuine value is afforded by the fact that Steedman's Powders have had a steadily increasing sale for nearly 100 years, and still stand unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no poison, Observe the EE in Steadman.</p>
        <p>anomaly in rating. I don't. I have another name for it, but must contain myself at this -period of the year. During the last 30 years I must have paid these charges two or three times over. This fact alone should convince every- body that to force me still to go' on paying is nothing but sheer robbery. Take the 5 per cent. charge off and my economic rent would be ss. 6d. per week. If I am compelled to pay 6s. 3d. then I am rack-rented. I have main- tained this position in this correspondence from the very first. _ . .</p>
        <p>There is always a remedy for an injustice even when vested interests are eoncerned, and in case Mr. Smith should ask for one, I suggest that in the first place it would be necessary to fix the total amount of each tenant's share. Then the amount to be payable weekly for a period of seven years should be settled, and the whole of such particulars should be entered in the rent-book, the money to be paid with the rent. with this difference, of course, that it should not appear -nor be returned as part of the gross rental, so escaping rateable charges. To use Mr. Smith's way of putting it, it should be known to all landlords and tenants that private charges for paving are not rateable except when paid through the gross rental. I am all the more anxious to champion fair rent ii,..these normal times, because I can fore- see at no distant date our sanitary authority being compelled to adopt a more up-to-date sewage system, which may cost anything up to a quarter of a million. Then we shall not only see house values go up,,we shall also know Imore about what that .seeming paradox of empty houses and the horrors of overcrowding means. December 31, 1913.</p>
        <p>FAIR RENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</head>
        <p>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</p>
        <p>MENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Reginald Barker, in his lengthy letter, has travelled beyond the points contro te - up his points. by me, but it will be a pleasure.to take veSrurdely Mr. Barker has been unfortunate in quoting Lord Halifax and the Oxford Movement present-day leaders ,as agreeing with him in supporting the Disestablishment of the Church.</p>
        <p>Whatever opinions Lord Halifax may at one time have had, both his Lordship and the Secre- tary of the English Church Union—the society which to-day expresses the views and carries on the work of the Oxford Movement—are utterly opposed to Dieestablishment. Your</p>
        <p>correSpondent can prove this by direct communi- cation with Lord Halifax, since he is evidently ignorant of the fact, but in brief I will state the reasons for his champion's change of view. His Lordship has read and understood Clause 3 of the Welsh Bill, which re-imposes the whole present ecclesiastical law on the Disestablished Church, and then says it is made free. He knows flow, that this talk of freedom is a sham, and that the Bill is a piece of the grossest erastianism—against which Mr. Barker, in the course of his long letter, himself speaks so strongly- Lord Halifax also sees that the new rules for doctrine, Ac., are to be made by the bishops, clergy and laymen of the Church, but that Mr. McKenna refuses to insert in the Bill any definition of the word " layman," and so leaves it _open to Nonconformists to say they are laymen and to have a finger in the pie. Also that no new rules can be made unless approved by the State--which means by Mr. McKenna, as Home Secretary. All this, and more, is quite unacceptable, as now understood, by extreme High Churchmen of Lord Halifax's stamp, of which, I assume, Mr. Barker is one. It appears to be quite evident that Mr. Barker really does not understand the meaning of the word "alien." That the Church of Wales cannot be an alien Church, as he evidently con- tends, but is the visible representative of the ancient British or Celtic Church, which was driven into Wales by the ASigles and Saxons, a Lymm Tory may not be able to convince him, but he ought to be convinced by such an eminent Liberal Statesman and men of letters as Mr. Bryce, who stated in the House of Com- mons, that undoubtedly " the Church in Wales is the legitimate historical euccession to the Church of St. David." The fact that it has entered into full communion with the Chnrch of England (which is in the same way the true historical succession of the Church •of , the Angles) does not make it alien any more than the fact that Nonconformists have entered into &amp;Tree Church Council touches their continuity. As regards Nonconformists paying the Church they do not attend or believe in, it to easy' to apply the " quoque tithes to argument. As an instance or two, the great tithes at West Ayton. Yorkshire, go to the Unitarians, and Churchmen have to Pay them. The Church- ' man who holds the farm of Pencyncau, in the parishreceives a visit every Sundayf L omorning u fromli i 1 a deacon of the neigh- bouring Trinity Chapel, belonging to the Welsh Calvinistic Methodists, and is " compelled " to pay one guinea each week towards the payment of the Nonconformist preacher for the Sunday. If there is anything unjust in principle of tithes Nonconformists, surely 'the same may be said of tithes payable by Churchmen. layable' by leltakasegruekryrcaehln'and those associated with f one is taken away, so should the other la• sten away. endowments were as sliven to titer. CBltsurrnr for the saying of Masses, why not hand the endow- conditions to the Roman nt:cser. htw ih theygiccis vhwewonauhr leeednna ri uatwtaiyi e from d Church,Thie pOcsoeutnoedilos: buwtthoalr redlastuicehu funds T take, Mr. B '.loiluthdeo nCootu 'propose the County Councils with the Pope of Rome? They will not carryout the conditions for which lir. Barker. says the money was given. Not have the Welsh Libraries any relationship with prayers for the dead! But before Mr. Barker can be sure that such were the conditions upon which endowments were given to the Church Mr. Lloyd George at- tempted equoted two cases in fact, but immediately pointed out and proved that to have been so given was bought qbeufootree a thseinygelear example? 1662, will he be good enough tq Anne's Bounty in the 18th century!ifr .Barker b y Queen ueen the thewalsaland d this—be di l says that Henry VIII. endowed the Church after the Reformation. But the death: he himself was, in fact, a Roman Catholic until his death. Nor did this "saintly monarch" establist a Church, as Reformation rmvatiiioin,: not completed until long after as Mr. Barker tells us. If he did either the one or the other, will your correspondent tell us how? for so momentous a course as the estab- lishment and the endowing of a Church can easily be confirmed to sorae Act of Parliament. To make assertions of such a nature without chapter and verse, and to build much argumen- tative matter thereupon, scarcely carries any conviction. What Henry VIII. did was to suppress and rob the religious houses, which were outside the Church, and indeed robbers of the funds of the Church. He would have done justly had lie restored these funds to their rightful owners, instead of which he gave much of the money to favourites and courtiers—no di übt for services rendered. The Church has never re- ceived back these lost revenues, much less been endowed by anything Henry VIII. did. Henry VIII. was astute enough to use the Reformation movement which was then spreading over Christendom for his own purposes and for reasons of State, and so helped the movement itself in England. To that extent good was done. In our own day we know something of alliances for political reasons between religion- ists the very poles apart, but in this latter day case he would be a clever man who could prove (not merely assert) that good would result. There are some reasons given in the last paragraph of Mr. Barker's letter intentionally in favour of Disestablishment which are argu- ments rather for Church Reform, with some of which I cordially agree, but which I find it difficult to realise as coming from a High Churchman of Lord Halifax's stamp! Over such arguments, will he let me fall into his arms, and he into mine, seeing I am an Evangelical? I want Church Reform, but I cannot see how the way to it is to disestablish the Church, and I think I know what is exactly meant, historically, by "Establishment." The, fact is, I have asked very many (who probably know much more of the matter than either of your correspondents) in what way Disestablish- ment would really help Church Reform, and no one can tell me. If the present Bill is exam- ined there is no indication whatever of it. The statement is frequently made, but cannot be proved. All such statements rest on assump- tion, and assumptions are not arguments. C. STIDSTON-BROADBENT. Lymm, December 30th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SACRIFICERS</head>
        <p>THE SACRIFICERS</p>
        <p>it sad as they ..en with passionate negation xlines, pro tem., the sweet temptati whilst Derby, with heroic bluff, Toys with, although he hates, the Stuff.</p>
        <p>Reproduced by the permi,</p>
        <p>them, it would also be ruinous to the interests of their party. In any event our duty is clear, and that duty is to endeavour by every means in our power to compel them to submit their proposals to the judgment of the country. Sir Edward Carson writes: I believe that 1914 will witness the failure of the conspiracy be- tween the Government and the Nationalist party to pass Home Rule against the wishes of the electors. In any event, I know that we shall have the British people behind us in our determination not to give up our position as free citizens of a United Kingdom. Mr. Austen Chamberlain says: -Vo graver crisis ever confronted the nation. If the policy be not defeated, the new year will be stained with the blood of our countrymen shed in civil strife, and the lasting legacy of bitterness and estrangement will destroy the unity of our national life.</p>
        <p>Lord Selborne says: The Government are apparently about to embark upon a land policy which may be summed up as more, and still more, interference by the Government with the daily life of every man, every discouragement to man wishing to own his house or farm. Against this put the Unionist policy—the great- est possible reduction in the number of officials, commissioners, and inspectors, and assistance to everyone desiring to own a farm, small hold- ing, or house. Mr. F. E. Smith, after expressing the opinion ' that there is " unfortunately no great prob- ability of either peace or goodwill obtaining in the political world during the next few ' months" and "that the Unionist party must hold itself prepared for any eventuality," says, " like Macbeth, the Government hesitate before plunging the dagger of civil strife into the heart of what has been for centuries a united people. Our party left Ireland prosperous and contented. Eight years of Radical Government have undone the work of twenty years of Union- ist administration, aroused secular passions which were being rapidly forgotten, and pre- sented the United Kingdom with the choice</p>
