      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>UNIONIST GLOM.</head>
        <p>UNIONIST GLOM.</p>
        <p>Leaders' New Year Messages Speak of Bitterness and Civil Strife.</p>
        <p>The January issue of "Our Flag," the official Conservative and Unionist organ, published on Tuesday, contains the New Year's messages of the leaders to the party. The messages are mostly gloomy forebodings of disaster in Ulster, and, singularly Sir Edward Carson's seems the leak provocative of that result. Mr. Boma Law says nothing more helpful to peace than that it is the clear duty of the party to en- deavour by every means to compel the Govern- natnt to submit their proposals to the country. Lord -Lansdowne says: The New Year's number will see the light amidst anxieties greater than any which have threatened our country within the memory of living men. • We are in imminent peril of civil war, provoked by ; a Government which has wrecked the Constitu- tion and is now attempting to wreck the union. His Majesty's Ministers, who have declined our. challenge to submit the issue to the judgment of the electors, apparently realise' the danger, but can discover no means of averting it. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Boner Law says: The prospect before us as a nation in the coming year is gloomy. The Government persist in their declared policy which is to drive the people of Ulster out the Union against their will and without t sanction of the electors. Then they will be responsible for national disaster. I am, how- ever, convinced that ,as they approach nearer to the precipice the Government will shrink from taking the plunge, for to do so would not only be disastrous to the nation, but, and this is a consideration which is sure to influence</p>
        <p>of I ;he I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>:on of the" Westminster Gazette.'</head>
        <p>:on of the" Westminster Gazette.'</p>
        <p>between the violent coercion of the loyal and ' Protestant North, followed by a great upheaval of democratic sentiment in this country and the dismissal of the present Government from power. Mr. Joseph Chamberlain has told ns that we must fight to a finish, and I would say that the whole Unionist party are prepared to stand by his advice." Mr. Walter Long emphasises their duty as a party to stand shoulder to shoulder, "grimly resolved to do our utmost to save our country from civil war, and our people from overwhelm- ing disaster, and to be true to our fellow loyal- ists in all Ireland." Mr. Steel-Maitland gives it as the duty of Unionists to defeat Home Rule, and "to carry our positive and constructive reforms in in- dustry, in agriculture, and in housing." Lord Edmund Talbot gives it as his "New get Year wish," "that the people may be given an early opportunity of expressing their opinion of a Government which is afraid of consulting them, and which prefers the horrors of civil war to the risks of losing their own seats." Sir George Younger said that "Unionists in Scotland are in good heart and full of hope," and adds "Scotland clearly recognises her re- sponsibilities to her Irish brethren, and there seems little doubt of her answer to the Govern- ment's proposals, as they stand, if the, appeal she demands be made to her." The Duke of Marlborough, writing in the New Year number of the "Primrose League Gazette," says that the Irish question has entered upon a new phase, and that phase is revolutionary. The situation is dominated by the implacable hostility of Ulster to the Home Rule Bill, and by its determination to resist by force the operation of the possible Home Rule Act. The Duke denies that Unionists have taken up the Ulster question to divert attention from Mr. Lloyd George's land campaign. Their attitude to-day was that which they adopted when Mr. Gladstone first raised the Home Rule issue. Unionists would lend an attentive ear to definite proposals for an equitable settlement.</p>
      </div>
