        <p>PASSIK WEEK.</p>
        <p>COMING PILGRIMAGE TO ULSTER.</p>
        <p>We understand that a political trip to Ulster is being' organised upon lines similar to'those that governed the famous trip to Germany a few years ago. Then the trippers had to see Germany under tariffs; now they have .to see Ulster in arms ant ready to "go 10r' England, if England grants Home Rule. What is com- mon to both these excursions is that Conserva- tive organisers are going to act as hosts to a few Liberals who are to go and see: that there is no deceit about the thing. If Liberals can only be convinced that Ulster means fight and not bluff, then all may be welt—just as if the Liberals who went to Germany could convince their fellows that tariffs were the thing for England, England would be converted. There is something exceedingly pathetic about this admission that a leaven of Liberal testimony is necessary to make the Tory fables appear to be like truth. We do not know who the Liberals of Warrington are who have been favoured with these invitations, but may we express the hope that on this occasion they are real Liberals, and not professed Liberals in disguise, only waiting for an opportunity of declaring themselves Tories? If we have an honest, uncensored opinion from any Liberal returning from Ulster, we shall- be glad to publiSh it, and shall not fear the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS IN THE STREETS.</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS IN THE STREETS.</p>
        <p>..... It is a matter for keen regret that there has, of late, been so much drunkenness i n the streets of Warrington. Perhaps the festive season .and trte good times now being experi- enced and the Jorge wages earned are to some extent responsible; but whatever the reason, the fact remains that the town has not of late been quite so free from objectionable sights as it was till recently. We note from a record of the Watch Committee that the Citizens' Tem- perance League has called attention to this matter. They told the committee that they view</p>
        <p>with apprehension the amount of drunkenness on the streets of Workington, especially among young people, and in view of the danger to citizenship respectfully urge the Watch Com- mittee to instruct the police to exercise in- creased vigilance as to the public-houses where such intoxicants are obtained.</p>
        <p>Tills will be laid before • the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday, and may give rise to some discussion.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH ACCOUNTS. The District Auditor (Mr. F. Lambert Stevens), reporting Upon his audit of• the bor- ough accounts for the year ended March 31st, says that the total receipts amounted to £366679 and the expenditure to £362,197. There was a net balance in hand of £89,428. Loans and capitalised value of annuities amounted to £1,277,493—all fairly large figures, showing the size and the stability of .our muni- cipal constitution. Having spoken in apprecia- tive terms of the accurate manner in which the accounts of each department are kept, the official states:</p>
        <p>The only matter to which I think it neceisary to Specially call your attention is your practice of paying travelling and hotel expenses to mem- bers and officers attending conferences, or en- gaged on other business of the. Corporation in London, on the basis of a resolution passed in 1897, fixing the allowance at 30s. per day, and first-class railway fare. This, I consider, should be amended so as to be in agreement with the regulation made by the Lords of the Committee of the Privy Council on Education, under the School Board Conferences Act, 1897, that is to say, that the maximum allowances should be the actual travelling expenses, and Is. per diem when absent from home at night, and 109. 6d. per diem when not absent from home at night. This question has been referred to the General Purposes :Committee for consideration:</p>
        <p>'HOWARD PLACE. There are some queer entries on the agenda for Tuesday's Council . theetind reipecting Howard Place. First we, have a complaint, through one committee, of the nuisance caused there by noisy children, and the information that the matter has been referred to the Chief Constable. -</p>
        <p>Then the Sanitary Committee report that the Finance-Committee, to whom application has been made for a site for a public convenience at, the corner of Dial-street and .Fennel-street think that if they acquiesced "it might interfere with the development of that site." Whereupon the Sanitary Committee resolved to instruct the Borough Surveyor to Prepare a plan for the construction Of a convenience- on Howard Place for submis- sion at the next meeting of the committee. At the subsequent meeting the plan was submitted and .approved, with provisions as t a are- taker's room. -The friends of Howard Place, and -of all that is just and reasonable, should be in their places at the Council meeting to prevent what the'y ha've,'on more than one or two occasions, already_ decided shall not be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NEW TOWN HALL SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE NEW TOWN HALL SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall and Municipal Offices Sub- committee of the General Purposes Committee have reported in favour of leaving the existing Town Hall as it is,'and of, instructing the Sur- veyor to furnish particulars as to the existing office requirements, with a report as to the possibility of erecting a three-storey building to contain all departments of the Corporation, upon .the site behind Bank House and facing the eastern approach to the Town Ilan. •</p>
        <p>NEW YEAR THOUGHTS.</p>
        <p>" How shall 1 a habit break?" As you did that habit make, As you gathered, you must lose; As you yielded, now refuse. Thread by thread the strands we twist, Till they bind us neck and wrist; Thread by thread the patient hand Must untwine ere free we stand, As we builded, stone by stone, We must toil unhelped, alone, Till the wall is overthrown.</p>
        <p>But remember, as we try, Lighter every test goes by; Wading in, the stream grown deep Toward the centre's downward sweep; Backward turn, each step ashore Shallower is than that before.</p>
        <p>Ah, the precious years we waste Levelling what we raised in haste; Doing what must be undone Ere content or love be won ! First across the gulf we cast Kite-borne threads, till lines are passed, And habit builds the bridge at last! Tlie philosophy of the poetry of John Boyle O'Reilly is as clear, simple and effective as his style is musical, beautiful and impressive. This poem is a splendid illustration.</p>
        <p>PEVERIL FIRE GRAres never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>GOOD TRADE,</head>
        <p>GOOD TRADE,</p>
        <p>passage from ene __. Our year to another finds the country in a indst prosperous cOndition.- Perhapg nothing -reflefets this fact better than the returns of the burruptcles of. the year, and regarding these we' v‘-'4ll quote the authority of " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," which says ::----</p>
        <p>We said last year that the prosperity of 1912 was reflected in the figures we gave of the Official .Failures. But the results for 1913 are even more remarkable, as they seem to prove a progressive improvement in our Trade. The grand total for the United Kingdom for the last twelte months only comes up to 7,191, as compared with in tereached. tide 7,874 for the, year preceding. This is indeed _a record, and is by far the lowest that has yet been n hayprtohseptevriare; signs of a turn Some se ty ad but, at all events, our statistics show a steady and increas- ing falling-off in Official Failures since 1908, when the similar total came out at 10,196. It. is more especially in England and Wales that business appears to have been so flourishing, for here-the total of 6,540 'ln 1912 is found to have dropped to 6,000 in 1913. In the same way there are ,very good results for Scotland, the similar totals being 967 and 827 respectively; and, indeed, even Ireland shows a progressive decrease, though small, the comparative figures there being 367 and 364, Which, if compared with the 490 of 1910, i certainly an improvement.</p>
        <p>we do not claim any this happy state of things, lint we would ask political significance for our friends—and our oPponents—to remember, when the trade cycle, which is held to be , inevitable, , arrives, that this prosperity took place under Free Trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>ANOTHER PEER • RAISES FARM WAGES.*</head>
        <p>ANOTHER PEER • RAISES FARM WAGES.*</p>
        <p>The EARL of Pm:mom:, Who is the owner of a large estate 'in South Wilt Shire, is, it is understood, about to follow the example re- cently set by LORD ASHBY ST. LEDGERS. It was intimated by the HON. GEORGE HERBERT_ (brother of LORD PEMBROKE), at a tinionist meet- ing at Wilton, near Salisbury, on December [6th, that " he.and Lono PEmenoss would do all in their power in that part of the country fo raise wages considerably all round." Mr: HERBERT added: "It might he asked, Why have YOU only done this now?' My answer is that the cost, 'of living has gone up_so considerably in recent years that the wages of those employed on the land should bo, raised." The speaker further said: •</p>
        <p>I agree with Mr. LLOYD GEORGE that there should be a change in regard to the agricultural labourers' wages. ; I am per- fectly convinced that in the long run neither the farmer nor the landlord would be worse off through paying better wages, because they would get better work from their men, and the men would live happily and contentedly.</p>
        <p>The intimation is welcome as indicating a recognition amongst Unionists of the need for the change which Mr. Lr.oyu GEORGE has out- lined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>THEAT ES.</head>
        <p>THE THEAT ES.</p>
        <p>"ALADDIN." Gorgeous Spectacular - Pantomime.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT ATTRACTION.</p>
        <p>" Christmas comes but once a year and when it comes it brings "—pantomime. The festive season would not be half so complete, so gay and jolly if we had no pantomime, and the popularity of such a theatrical diversion in Warrington is evidenced by the crowded houses at the Royal Court Theatre, where, -on Boxing Day, Mr. Richard Nightingale's "Aladdin" commenced a run of eight nights and five matinees. ;Success attended the initial per- formance, but on Monday evening it was appar- ent that any slight hitches had been overcome, and everything went with a swing. The pan tomime is full of brightness, is. rich from spectacular point of view, much artistic tat( being displayed in the scenic effects and the groupings of the ballet, and the incidental musical items, both humorous and sentimental. are decidedly pleasing. The curtain rises and we see the Market Place, Pekin. There is plenty of colour, but so judiciously used as to make the setting one of brilliance, although in no way gaudy. Another example of Abe scenic artist's art is seen in "The Cave of Mystery," with its gruesome, weird and awe-inspiring effects, which are relieved, however, when the grand jewel and electric ballet makes its appear- ance. A pleasingly pretty scene is that of "The Gardens of the Willow Pattern Plate," where the floral effect, in the middle of the huge plate, by the ballet, is decidedly one of artistic beauty. As a. finale there is the gorgeous wedding feast and procession of lanterns in Aladdin's Palace. where the richness and beauty of the dresses and the daintiness of the ballet are seen to ad- vantage. • The producer has been discreet and wise in his choice of cast, for a more suitable " boy " for a production of this class it would be well- nigh impossible to secure than Gladys Mayius, a' delightfully pretty and dashing comedienne. Her happy-go-lucky manner is in no way forced and her whole actions and mannerisms are naturally captivating. Her ragtime numbers are most popular, notably "You made me love you," in which the "didn't want to do it" ex- . pression is -very 'amusing. In her sentimental item, "Some day." she was not nearly so suc- cessful, the tone was not rich enough and the expression too weak. Her dancing compares favourably with her comedy items, being re- ceived with tumultuous applause. Elsie Ked- wards makes a charming princess, whose sing- ing and dancing are highly commendable. Mil- dred Bryan characterises Pekoe. She is a viva- cious comedienne and in her serio-song "Only a faded rose" she is a decided success, her voice being well modulated and full toned. Pluche Prince handles the part of Caramel succes-- fully. whilst Lola Morris, the chic Geni of the Ring, renders her songs, including "My hero" ("Chocolate Soldier") in a delightful manner. There are a number of jovial, as well as eccentric, comedy merchants, Who collaborate, and with many new stories, gags and songs keep the audie- ^ in a state of boisterous merriment. Tht "o'—rtet" "Stars of the evening" is pro- bably ('a most laughable, united effort anal..is well r"--,eived by the audience. Tom Hulme as Aber -tzar, the African magician, is a host in himaelf. The spontaneity and richness of his he— aur causes roars of latighter. Mr. C. Young Fix, is a clever comedian as Widow Twankey. Weed, he is one of the best "dames" we have seen. His actions are most natural, and his humour is of an excellent quality. Mr. Sydney Dench is adinirable as the bibulous Gun Sling. his gags being witty and clever. Norman and Allan are eccentric in their ." turns," and are clever cross-talkers, minsters and dancers. As Flip and Flop, bailiffs and servants of the Emperor, they are placed exactly right. Joe Howard also assists in the comedy as Wishee Washee, and his facial expressions call for many laughs. Mr. Charles Adair plays the part of the Emperor in a dignified manner, and indeed each member of the company adds his or her quota to a successful production. Others in the company are Flo Allen litereanr,, Hammond as Nicee, Lilian M. Davies as wave of the Lamp. The ballet is a large one, and Madame W. Allan is deserving of praise for the taste -displayed in the, setting and-gremping of the chorus, which consists of: Avery Hoard, Winifred Hoard, Dorothy Hoard, Elsie Cooper, Winnie Hamniersley, Lena Hyman, May Hall, Elsie Wilson, Beatrice French, Marie French, Annie Gough, Emily Scott, Nancy Forsyth, Evelyn Forsyth; Daisy Stewart, Louie Leslie, Connie Grandy, Ester Jennings, May Bell, Louie Lindon, Dolly Mallin, Dorothy Marsden, Mar- jorie liVoodhall and Doris Cooper. Mr. 11. L. Warburton, the popular Court acting-manager, has made excellent arrange- ments, and under the leadership of Mr. Fred Eplett, an augmented orchestra renders delight- ful and appropriate music. There will be a matinee to-day and the two performances to-night.</p>
        <p>Wirani4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>"THE EVER OPEN DOOR." , One of Mr. Loader's Successful Aftiwych Productions.</head>
        <p>"THE EVER OPEN DOOR." , One of Mr. Loader's Successful Aftiwych Productions.</p>
        <p>HERE NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>For real intellectual pleasure one must go to the masters of art. Such being the case, one has only to mention that Mr. George R. Sims has written another drama to secure interest at once, and to set playgoers thinking of an enjoy- ment to come.</p>
        <p>Among the many playwrights before the public there is no more successful writer than the worthy " Dagonet." He has written a number of plays, and as evidence of his versatility we may mention they are not confined to one class, as they comprise burlesques, musical comedies, comic operas, and good domesi..ic dramas, and, strangely enough, most of them have been'suc- cessful. A feather in his cap is that he has</p>
        <p>never touched any unsavoury subjects, such as are found in too many of the so-called problem plays. It is as a writer of emotional drama that Mr. Sims is most popular. In his latest play he has given us a most moving and cleverly written piece, viz., "The Ever Open Door," in which he has collaborated with Mr. H. IT. Her- bert. It was produced during the present Lon- don season at the Aldwych Theatre, and met with instantaneous success, crowded houses being the rule at all performances; in fact, so keen' has been the competition for seats that</p>
        <p>extra matinees have had to be put on to cope with the rush.</p>
        <p>It is a strong, clever drama, full of splendid situations, broadly comic effects, and many scenes of sentiment, all of which go to delight the average.hcaltlty minded person. _ _</p>
        <p>- -- The story turns round the fact that Lady Dereharii, a beautiful and wealthy widow, is led to believe that during the delirium following child-birth she murdered her baby son. She had, however, done nothing of the kind, but the child had been taken from her and put into the hands of a horrible old woman, Mrs. Dundon. It is a play which readily grips attention and carries. the interest of the audience to th.s close, where a most interesting denouement awaits them.</p>
        <p>The locale of many of the scenes is round Westminster, and as there is no living writer who knows his London better than Mr. George R. Sims, who has been likened •to a second Charles Dickens, we can promise playgoers that all the scenes will be true to life.</p>
        <p>The scenery will be foundd-very fine, the West- minster scenes indeed being sketched from actual existing places. Produced by Mr. A. M. Loader, at the -Ald- wych Theatre, London, this tour is also under his direction, and he has gathered - -together Many well-known artistes, viz. Miss Daisy Jamieson, Miss Vida Flower, Miss Anna Cure, Miss Adah. Rothwell, Messrs. Frank BeresfOrd, Wilfred E. Payne, to., &amp;c. "The Ever Open Door" will be the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre next week, and would advise early application for seats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LOVE AND WAR AT THf, HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>LOVE AND WAR AT THf, HIPPO- DROME.</p>
        <p>Scenes of war and thoughts of battle are in- separable- from interesting stories; and. "-The Gentleman Ranker," a production by Leo Lilly and Company, which appears at the Hippo- drome this week, must, by sheer Merit,. Tank high in this class of story. It is an incident of the South African War, and is eriacted-to the accompaniment of rifle shots, the rousing roll of drums, and the resounding • notes of the bugle. Private Barton, a young good/for-nothing, Who has forged his father's name and in other ways disgraced the family, joins the Army as a ranker, in order to hide his shame.</p>
        <p>Private Barton redeems his name, and also the favour of Violet Lee, a Red Cross 'nurse and his' father's ward. As Hudson, Mr. Charles King adapts himelf admirably, acting the part faithfull7. Colonel Fairly finds AR excellent interpretation at the hands of Mr. A. B. Wentworth, and the nurse's part is daintily portrayed by Miss Dora Alexander. Mr. Geerge B. Topping is quite acceptable as " Soapy " Hudson. The illuminated tableau of the gentleman ranker bringing relief across the Modder River is very striking, and a grand finale is given when the bugle band and the- troops march through the ,Devil's I'ass. I'aul and Fred Wille, equilibrists, give a clever turn, a feature of which is the dancing movements performed by one of them, at the same time performing a -daring head to lipid balancing feat. Their dog is highly trained. Cyrus Dare has many quaint sayings of chil- dren, whom he admirably mimics, and his great vocal story, "The Stolen Child," is pathetic in. the extreme. A comedian with large experience in. the laughter market is Ben Albert, his parodies of ragtime songs being perhaps his best hit. Miss Devenport gave every satisfaction, and large houses had great pleasure in listening to her songs; whilst an extraerdinary exhibition of wooden shoe dancing was presented by that 'dancing demon,. Pat Lynch. Mdlle. Marjutti performs some striking evolutions in the original scena, From Demon to Fairyland." We would not be doing justice if we omitted to refer to the orchestra, under the conductor- ship of Mr. Herbert. Stone; the music at this house of entertainment is always a pleasing and satisfactory feature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>TO-MORROW'S /11110DROME CONCERT. • Visit of the South Lancs. Band, ,In.:the name of charity a tuilitary band concert will-b*NOven-in.the liippa4rome4•o.4itorrow-- -0 – night, When the famous Band of the 2nd</head>
        <p>TO-MORROW'S /11110DROME CONCERT. • Visit of the South Lancs. Band, ,In.:the name of charity a tuilitary band con- cert will-b*NOven-in.the liippa4rome4•o.4itorrow-- -0 - night, When the famous Band of the 2nd</p>
        <p>Battalion South Lancashire Regiment will be '1 in attendance. Most valuable assets to the . programme have been introduced by the aequisi- • - tion of Miss. Marie Houghton, a local soprano, ivho has won fame both with her connection with, the Warrington Musical Society's_ Opera • enterprises, and later as a student under Madame Albani ; and Senor Angel Blanco, a Spanish violinist, who has won eminence in this, as well as in his own country; • has also been engaged. Through the kindness of the manager, Mr. A. W. Turpin,. the whole of the proceeds will be handed over to the treasurer of the infirmary.</p>
