      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>Next week's programme promises to be of un- usual interest, for, in addition to the original pantomime- sketeh, " Fun in a China Shop," presented l:iy" the Howard and Collinson Trio, Mr. and Mrs. Gord6n Wilde, the famous society entertainers, are engaged. What is described as a " Spanish novelty perch act," will be given is sure to please.. Gordon Brown, by the Two Rodriquez, and Fannie Needham. or, will make an appearance, and, cob me ebdoi tenor, together with Mdlle. Quin's mysterious act, Jeavon .and Jena, and Cissie Lupin°, dancer, a good programme is assured.</p>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL. Much success has attended the efforts this week of the Royal Stook Company, who appear in the exciting melodrama "The Shadows ef Great City." As usual, Mr. Harry Val Vousden makes a decided hit when he presents the part of Toni Cooper, an honest sailor, who is wrongly imprisoned. A pair pf typical crooks are found in Mr. Digby Hayes and Mr. George Overs, who respectively appear in the roles of Abe Nathan, a pawnbroker, and George Benson, a "gentleman thief." Mr. Arkwright, the detective, finds a capable interpretation at the hands of Mr. C. B. Horrobin, whilst as the boisterous Biddy Roonan, Miss Lillian Murray is quite successful. The respective characters of Nelly and Annie Standish are pleasingly por- trayed by Misses Phyllis Ray and Beatrice Malvern,</p>
        <p>and Cross a, Mr. acting Is a* dr. A. anti ly ieiarge aapy " the 3 the grand id the</p>
