        <p>I</p>
        <p>Licensing Sessions. The borough magistrates decided on Monday that the annual licensing sessions should be held on February 6th.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market. Wheat, 4s. 4d. to 4s. 54d. per 70M.; oats, 29. 6d. to 2s. Bd. per 451 b.; wheat straw, 30s. per ton; oat straw. 30s. per ton; XX flour, 235. 6d. per 2401 b.; bran, is. 3d. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Carols. There was a large congregation at the Parish Church on Sunday evening, when the choir ren- dered a number of carols in an impressive man- ner. Air. F. •114. Darby was the organist, and the Rector expressed his best wishes to the con- gregation for the New Year:</p>
        <p>Promotion for Mr: L T. Williams. ' Those who remember the energy thrown into his work by Mr. I. T. Williams, While he was in the Warrington district, will not be surprised to learn that that gentleman has been appointed chief goods manager of the London and North- Western Railway Company.</p>
        <p>Christmas at Dutton Workhouse. Christmas at Dutton Workhouse, was spent in an enjoyable manner, and all were thank- ful for the extra fare. The men received gifts of tobacco. A Christmas tree, well laden with presents, was placed in the nursery, and the children made merry. In the evening the din- ing was the scene of a pleasing programme.</p>
        <p>Dramatic Society's 111-luck: "Sweet Lavender" Abandoned._ The Warrington Amateur Dramatic Society', who have been rehearsing- "Sweet Lavender" for the past two months, with the object of pre- senting that play at the Royal Court on Feb- ruary 4th, have been forced to abandon that intention for the present, owing to sickness arid bereavement in the families of several members taking part.</p>
        <p>Local Jockey's Generosity. Steve" Donoghue, the successful local jockey, does not forget the children at St. Rene- diet's School, where he was mice a scholar, for on Monday afternoon they were given a treat at. his expense. Accompanied by the Rev. Father Swarbrick and a number of teachers, the youngsters spent a most enjoyable time at th• Picturedrome, and afterwards partook of an excellent tea. The remainder of the evening was spent in a very pleasant manlier.</p>
        <p>Campanology. The bellringers of St. Paul's Church rang a true peal of 5,040 changes last Saturdag• in the following method, in three hours three min- utes :—Two peals, 720 changes. Canterbury Pleasure; two peals, 720 changes, College single, and three peals, 720 changes, plain Bob minor. The ringers stood as follows: 1. J. B. Miller; 2, Cl. Bebbington ; 3, J. J. Allender; 4, W. Martin; tenor, J. J. Wilson. Mi. J. '3. Wilson eon- duoted. The weight of the tenor bell is l 4 ewe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>The Royal Stock Company next week will pre- sent " Man's Eaelay," described as a "drama with a moral."</p>
