      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>Last week 26 births were registered-10 females and 16 males—whilst there were 27 deaths. For the corresponding period of the previous year 34 births were registered, 20 being males and 14 females. There were last week four deaths from pneumonia and three from cancer. In the fever hospital there are 75 Patients, the figures being: Scarlet fever, 46; enteric fever, 5; diphtheria. 3. There has been during this period .80 inches of rainfall.</p>
        <p>- St. Luke's Church. . Organised by the Young Men's Bible Class in connection with St. Luke's Church, a succeBs- . ful whist drive in which 140 persons took part. eld was h • , a night. The Bank Quay Schoolroom on Boxing ebool, e Proceeds were in aid of the Sunday an; a number of ladies rendered valu- able assistance. Whist prize-winners were: Ladies: 1, Miss C. Downing; 2, Miss Ashcroft; consolation, Mrs. Westwell. Gentlemen: 1, Air. Rylands; 2, Mr. Chalmers; consolation, Mr. T. Forster. Dancing was indulged in for ~ tn.° hours and a very pleasant evening was *P6nt.</p>
        <p>Dutton Children's Home. Santa Claus was busy at the Children's llama at Dutton Lodge on Christmas Eve, when he visited, all the bedrooms and filled the stockings withe:Sweets and toys. At ,the hour of 4 a.m. the children were brought from the land of dreams to gaze upon the articles, which so Plainly indicated that he had been at work. Extra delicacies were provided at the table for each meal, and the evening was concluded by the children and staff entering with zest into merry games. The decorations were very choice, each room being submitted to an artistic erlour arrangement, but perhaps the pick was to be found in the boys' day-room, on the recently erected stage, which represented a pine forest on the borders of a lake, and in the centre of the arrangement stood a heavily laden Christmas . tree of unusual size. .</p>
        <p>The'Ftealism of "The Ever Open Door." The scenery of this play is an exact replica of the streets of Westminster, in which the action of "The Ever Open Door" takes places. Mr. Loader and Mr. George R. Sims visited the slums, accompanied by the scenic artist. On one occasion when taking the sketch of Laundry Yard, the inhabitants of that charm- -ing locality resented their picturesque sur- roundings being exposed to the world in general, and, after making various remarks in the most unqualified and unparliamentary lan- guage, resorted to more drastic methods, and Pelted the knight of the brush with tealeaves and half a loaf of bread, which caused him to beat a hasty retreat. Fortunately for the play, the artist had finished his work. "The Ever Open Door" will be at the Royal Court Theatre next week.</p>
        <p>Corning Events. January 4.—Rev. B. Pei-civil completes 211 years' ministry at St. Peter's. Special sermon, ' 6.30. January 4.-2nd South Lancashire Band atl RiPnodrome. January 5 to January 7.—Warrington Free Church Council prayer meetings, 8. January 5 to January B.—Lymm Free Chunk _Council united services. January B.—Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, sermon, tea and meeting, 3.30, 5 and 7.30 7. January B.—Padgate Wesleyan entertainment, January 9.—Rev. U. Dunnico, Methodist School, Lymm, 8. January 11.—Warrington Free Church Coun- cil United Communion, St. John's Presbyterian Church, 8. January 14.—Mr. Lowe's auction sale, Butter- Market-street Salerooms, 2. January 15.—Mr. Lowe's auction sale, at 11, Wilson-Patten-street, 11. January 22.—Police Ball, Parr Hall, 8. February 26, 27 and 28.—St. John's Presby- terian Sale of Work, Cairo-street Schools.</p>
        <p>Gratitude for Timely Help. We did not miss our father with having such a kind gentleman as you," writes a child to Mr. Laws, the local police court missioner. A mother writes that she hopes those who gave the toys that made her children happy themselves have " heaps and heaps of happy Years." Many other letters framed will upon similar lines have been received by Mr. Laws and his colleague, Mr. G. H. wainwright, of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, welcome echoes of the gratitude felt and expressed by deserving poor People, who have been paid a kindly attention during Christmastide, the attention including the distribution of toys among the younger members of the poorest section of the commu- nity. This service was made possible by the generosity of a number of ladies and gentle- men, who entrusted the expenditure of some money they could spare to the mission. The work in which Mr. Laws and Mr. Wainwright are engaged is deserving of all praise, especially that part of their mission which aims at bring- ing married couples into a state of harmony by showing them the folly of quarrelling over Petty grievances or imaginary faults.</p>
        <p>OM</p>
