        <p>Die death occurred at Pendleton, Aaucues_ ter, on Tnursday, of tile Bev. T. Sinclair A.A., formerly vicar of St. Thomas' Church, cAockton heatn, from where he retired in 18a, owing to The funeral will take In at St. Thomas' Church on Tuesday at 12 noon. Accident at Itylands' • As the result of an -accluent at Messrs: Itylands' works, ..Edward Booth, 40, of so. Cnurch-street, received a fractured clavicle anu fractured ribs, lie was taken to the Intirmar.y on New Year's Eve, and is progressing satisfac- torily.</p>
        <p>nection treet Baptist Church was wall' the Golborne-s lletwistye. Tear,s Party.partyin eau The annuaßy l held on Thursday. A meeting and concert fol- Mr. Longshaw, church and school secretary, lowed, over which the Rev. h. Ogden presided. gave a hopeful account of the school life. NISH Of Professor G. Currie Martin. Those who had the privilege ,of hearing Pro- fessor Currie Martin give his series of lectures on " Inspiration' last year will be delighted to know that he is giving another series of lec- tures during each Monday in January. The lectures are of an educational nature, and are on "The Lessons of thp Great Religions." The first two are to be given in the Academy-street School on *January 'sth and 12th. The second two are to be given in the Stockton Heath School (Greenbank-street) on January 19th and 26th. 'the Generous Public. The response to, the appeal at the places of amusement in the town, for help towards the Infirmary Christmas treat, has been generous, as the following return shows :--Hippodrome, £2B 2s. 2d.; Royal Court Theatre, £l6 12s. 7d.; Picturedrome, 188. 10d.; New Picture House, £7 10s: 9d.; Andrews', £6 Bs. 7d.; Park Pictures, .R 4 178. 71d. ; Pavilion, £2 14s. 84d.; Theatre of Varieties, £2 118. ld.; a total of .46 168. 4d., against £74 12s. last year.</p>
        <p>Watchnight Services. , On New Year's Eve the usual watchnifht ser- vices were conducted at mod of the chapels and churches in the town, and on the whole were well attended, especially at St. Paul's. The following were the preachers at the chapel ser- vices, which were bright apd inspiring, the pastors giving appropriate addresses, and the congregations joining in singing special hymns Wycliffe Congregational, Rev. T. T. James; Gol- borne-street, Rev. 11. Ogden; St. John's Presby- terian Church, Rev. W. Reid; Bold-street, Rev. 11. Williamson; organist, Mr. T. B. Weaver; Legh-street Primitive Methodist, Rev. A. J. Chessum ; and Bank Quay Working Men's Mis- sion, Mr. T. Dale.</p>
