        <head>Bold-street Social: Sisterhood Gives a</head>
        <p>Bold-street Social: Sisterhood Gives a</p>
        <p>l'iano.</p>
        <p>Ihe most successful Re:v.-Year's gathering that has been held in connection with the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School for many years took place on New Year's night. Close upon 300 people attended, and the excellent programme of well-varied items, drawn up by Mr. W. Silcock, treasurer; Mr. F. Light, secretary, and a small committee met with the unanimous approval of the assembly. The Misses Mills and Dring, assisted by rperferseidslediments Miss the. teachers, served the ston Hugh and (iMiss Williamson Ell a Cotton presented a satisfactory report of the Sunday School work in the form f • of a dialogue. Among the outstanding features of the enter- tainment were the seleetions, songs, sketches,. &amp;e., given by Mr Alfred Dutton, the children of the primary department, Mr. W. Swaine, Miss Parkinson, Mr. 'Warburton, Miss Smith's class, Mr. Peake's class, Miss Chilton's clasti, miss Isaacs ' class Miss Cooper's class, the choir, and Miss Weaver (who accompanied). On behalf of the Sisterhood Committee the Rev. 11. Wil- liamson presented the new piano to the Trustees which was accepted by Mr. of the chapel, Hickman, who suitably responded. Mr. W. Cooper, the treasurer, was highly delighted with the result of the effort.</p>
        <p>WOOD'S COLLEGE. Notable List of Successes and Prizes. • At the last term examination, held on the 19th December, under the auspices of Sir Isaac Pitman and Sons, Ltd., and superintended by a disinterested local committee, the •following awards were gained by students of Wood'E College, Bold-street : Speed section: 120 words per minute, Mars • Gilbert, Ellen B. Corner; 90 words, Marjorie Gerrard, Elsie M. Titchett, Ethel M. Higgins, Emma Percival; 80 words, Eva Podmore; 5G words, Norah M. Tooner. Theory certificates: Josephine Kilduff, Eva Long, Dorothy Howard, lklab,et Pilkington,. Dora Bladen, Gertrude Hough, Jessie Al olyneux. Elementary certifi- cates: •Doris H. Travis, Doris Howard, May Mather&amp; In addition to the certificates the following special prizes have been awarded: Mary Gil- bert, bound copy of " Pitman's • Shorthand Weekly," January to . June, 1905; Marjorie Gerrard, "A Christmas Carol," in shorthand; Dorothy Howard, "The Haunted Man," in shorthand. . . .</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that Miss Mary Gil- bert, who had no previous knowledge of the subject, has in seven months gained a speed of 120 words per minute. This reflects great credit both on herself and Wood's College.</p>
