        <p>ANOTHER SOAP COMBINE? Reported Coming Bid for China Trade.</p>
        <p>DENIALS IN ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>It was 'announced in Shanghai on Wednesday tt luila et of gs iogaapn tai and d oaml libe n apt prod oo ndufcotis. itnheomuannauhfa.ead- been formed between the firms of Messrs. Lever Bros., Messrs. Brunner, Mond and Co., Messrs.l J. Crosfield and Sons, Messrs. Gossage, and the' Erasmic Company. It was reported that land had been acquired, where a factory, equipped with the latest scientific appliances, was to be ' established. The nominal capital of the com- panies is stated to be about £35,000,000, and is who made beAlinevejlEtxoambeinaerß”rirtseiplrieise-ecnotida-tive, inquiries in official quarters in Warrington, was assured that there was no truth whatever in the statement. The statement is repeated from Shanghai that "several firms" have formed a combine, which will have a market value of 60 millions, and that a factory site, valued at £100,000;h:is 1 een purchased. The Press Association says there is good reason to believe that a scheme for 'opening up China in the interests of the,soap industry is well in hand, but whether anything really definite has been concluded is hot at present known in Liverpool and district. Sir John Brunner denies the report of "a gigantic British combine," but admits it is true that there has been talk of putting up works in China. - Messrs. Lever Bros.' directors stated that they had nothing to add to what Sir John Brunner had already stated. They were unable</p>
      </div>
