      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. T. BIRTLES.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. T. BIRTLES.</p>
        <p>A Picturesque Personality and Useful Citizen.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>The whole town heard with the deepest regret of the death of Councillor Thomas Birtles, which took place at his residence, Brantwood, Fearnhead, about half-past two on Tuesday afternoon, at the age of 76 years. The deceased gentleman, who was well known and respected throughout this part of the country, died after a trying illness extending over a period of seven weeks. He made his last public appearance at the annual meeting of the Town Council on November 10th, when he appeared to be in his usual health,- but few knew that he was suffer- ing from an internal complaint which would so soon prove fatal. He was of a cheery disposi- tion, and the memory of his striking figure, with his long, flowing white beard, will always be cherished by those who knew him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>_NATIVE OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>A _NATIVE OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor Birtles was born in Mersey-street, and when very young attended a dame's school. Later he went to the National School and as a day scholar to the Blue Coat School, but, like many of our old public men, his education proper really commenced after he had left the day school. He belonged to a very old Cheshire family, who settled in Warrington over 200 years ago, and he was proud of the fact that his grandfather—Edward Birtles—was in the Battle of Trafalgar, where.. he- was slightly wounded by a cannon ball, the- marks of which ' were visible until; his .death. Two other brothers of his grandfather served in the American War of Independence, and one was , shot and the other was killed in action, whilst yet a. fourth brother. fought in the Peninsular- War, but he returned home without a scratch, and received a medal and pension.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>'SELF-MADE MAN.</head>
        <p>A 'SELF-MADE MAN.</p>
        <p>On the death of Mr. Birtles'- father, at the early age of 32, in 1848, the deceased councillor had to commence work, when twelve years of age, to help to support his mother, who was left with four children. His first engagement was with Mr. Robert Garnett, the founder of the firm of Garnett and Son, cabinet makers. He joined the Mechanics' Institute, and having thoroughly devoted himself to his work, he made rapid strides. On the formation of a School cf Design he early became a student, under the headmastership of Mr. R. Collinson, of Man- chester. He set out to be a designer and to obtain a strong groundwork he laboured at the school on every possible occasion which pre- sented itself, and he was ultimately successful in gaining a scholarship and became a pupil teacher under Mr. CoHinson. This gentleman soon afterwards removed to London, and Mr. J. Christmas Thompson was appointed in his stead. Mr. Birtles was still a teacher, and worked hard, with such students as Mt. War- rington Wood, to get works sent for national competition Then there came the turning point in his career.</p>
        <p>RECOGNISED BY SOUTH KENSINGTON. .The works he had sent for competition were accepted for entrance as a master in training at South Kensington, with an allowance of salary. He went to London in 1856-7, and had three National Schools to visit each week, and give blackboard lessons in freehand and model drawing. The first school he visited, singular to say, was St. George's-in-the-East, from where the late Rev. William Quekett was appointed to Warrington. His health broke down and he returned to Warrington, where he again entered the services of Mt. Robert Garnett. - PIONEER AS PHOTOGRAPHER.</p>
        <p>Soon afterwards,he -married the eldest daugh- ter of Mr. John Longshaw, photographer, of Sankey-street, and seeing that there was a future for this art, he looked out for an opening and settled at Northwich, where, after using a temporary strueture, he built the first per- , manent photographic premises in Cheshire. About this time he was e4ficte4 a member of the Northwich Local Boardi, g. was'thi-Arst in the country to photograph a 'salt 'nine lighted by electricity. That was over 30 years ago, and since that time he had had the patronage of leading county families for miles around. Tie kept in touch with Warrington, and in 1878 opened a studio over a music shop at Market Gate.</p>
        <p>RECOLLECTIONS OF OLD WARRINGTONd</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Birtles was at the foundation stone-laying by Mr. Beamont of the Museum and Library, and also at the opening of the Bold-street Wes- leyan Chapel, and was working inside the build- ing with workmen to the last moment of people coming in. He was present at a Council Meet- ing in 1848, when the Council •Chamber was in Market-street, over the firer engine shed, and he remembered the stirring Parliamentary election of 1847. He could recollect the time when the town could only boast of three policemen, Dan Dagnall being the. chief. One of these, Jack Hayes, would clear Bridge-street on a Sunday afternoon, and he had seen Irish labourers dart up passages and make for Friar's Gate out of his way. This policeman used a truncheon right and left. Mr. Dirties had fished in the river and caught eels and dace, and drunk its waters when going to school. The packet stage was a busy scone in Mr. Dirties' early days, when he remembered the "Jack Sharp,"&amp; which conveyed fustian pieces from shops in town to Manchester. There were six cotton mills ,then in Warrington. Boats at the packet stage could be hired, and lie had had many a sail down the river as far as Walton Arches and back. Ile could recall to memory the first station at Bank Quay, which was called Whitecross Station. There was only one cab on hire in the town then, Allison's cab, which was kept in the Eagle and Child Yard, and mostly used by Mr. Bea- mont. Mr. Dirties walked in the procession of trades at the peace rejoicings in. May, 1856. `fliis was a great day for Warrington, when there were tournaments on the river, sculling matches and boat races. He witnessed the launching of the " Taylour" in October, 1853, the ill-fated vessel which was sunk in January the following year. SOME AONOURS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Birtles was elected a member of the Council of the School of Art in 1889, and had the honour of being a member of the National Society of Professional Photographers of Eng- land, and up to the time of his death he was a member of the Council. In 1895 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Photographic Society of Great Britain. In November, 1908, he was returned unopposed for the Town Hall Ward representation on the Warrington Town Council, and on the expira- tion of his term of office he was again returned unopposed. lie was unsparing in his efforts for the well-being of the town, and held a good percentage of attendances on the various com- mittees on which lie sat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>• •-- -----..._. A fitting tribute of respect to the memory of Councillor Birtles was paid at the funeral, which took place at Hill Cliffe Graveyard yes- terday afternoon, a large number of the mem- bers of the Council and Corporation officials, as well as many prominent townsmen, attending. Thei Chairman of the Electricity and Tram- ways Committee placed a car, draped in black, at the service of the members. This left Academy-street before 12 o'clock, and when it reached the Cemetery gates the occupants, headed by the Mayor, proceeded to the borough boundary at Padgate Bridge, where a detach- ment of the police, under Inspector Esp, awaited the arrival of the cortege, after which the procession was formed and returned to the tramcar, which conveyed the Members to the Sttaton Heath Swing Bridge. Here the mem- bers allowed the cortege to pass through open ranks. Preceding the hearse was,a landau con- taining many wreaths, and a carriage in which were Mr. J. E. Birtles (son), the Revs. John Yong° and T. T. James, who had conducted a service at the house, and also officiated at the graveside. The other mourners were: Mr. J. C. Bate, Mr. A. Browne (sons-in-law), Mr. Basil Bate (grandson), Mr. Warwick Brookes, Mr. Reginald Birtles (son), Mr. F. Jackson, Mr. Stanley Wright (sons-in-law), Mr. Denis Wright (grandson), Mr. W. Grimsdale, Mr. IT. Brough (sons-in-law), and Mr. It. Bate (grand- son). , Wreaths were sent by the following : V` Daughters and daughter-in-law," "his sons</p>
        <p>(Continued in next col,tonn.),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ADULT SCHOQLS AND LIFE.</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOQLS AND LIFE.</p>
        <p>Work that Widens the Outlook and Develops Character.</p>
        <p>OLD YEAR STOCKTAKING.</p>
        <p>The following reports come to band from the various Adult School centres in town:—. BEW SE Y MEN'S. The Price of Ignoble Contentment.</p>
        <p>On Sunday- morning the Bible lesson dealt with the confidence which the Psalmist (121st Psalm) had in God, and Paul's consciousness of his own imperfections and shortcomings. Concerning the Psalmist's point of view, it was felt by most members that few men reached the sense of absolute security that all was well with the world. In some moods-- moods to which most men were susceptible at some time or other—evil seemed the stronger force in life. This serene optimism of the Psalmist could only be reached through faith in God's infinite power and only in the sense that the power was synonymous with Eternal Goodness. Evil, as we knew it, belonged to things transitory. And so in Paul's conception of completed character, he, and all other men were only still attaining through defeat and sorrow and conflict. Strug- gle and conquest were the very breath of the Christian life. These thoughts partially ex- plained why the School had not made greater progress in the old year. Many members pre- ferred ease and an ignoble contentment to struggle and conflict. But the best things in the New Year would only be realised by climb- ing " the upward faith." The extent of pro- gress .in tht real things of life would be the measure of the sacrifice involved. On New Year's Eve a watchnight service was held, conducted by Mr. William Ashworth.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD. Forward " in 1914. On Sunday the members surveyed the work done during the short period the School had been open, and made plans for the coming year. The members hope to get into their new school early in the spring, when there will be more opportunity for a forward movement. During the morning the President read the "Adult School Aims" and pressed home the fact that they could only be worked into practice by each member doing his part in the work. . SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S. Children's Treat: No Such Entertainment Was Ever Before Held.</p>
        <p>• The principal event at this school during. Christmas was a free treat given to the poor children of Sankey Bridges on Boxing Day. This was made possible by the kind generosity of several gentlemen of the town, who re- sponded heartily to the appeal made by Vice- President Jos. Elwell on behalf of the children. The members of both sections of the School set to work on the preparations with a will, and at 5.15 p.m. 160 children sat down to tea in three rooms of the school. After 'tea an entertain- ment was held, the boys upstairs and the girls downstairs. No other such entertainment was ' ever held, for the children entertained them- selves right merrily. Prizes were given for the best songs and recitations. It was surprising to find what talent revealed itself in the company. One lad gave quite a respectable interpretation of Mark Anthony's speech in "Julius CEesar;" "Friends, Roniane. Countrymen." Another gave a long piece of Hamlet's philosophy. The "grown-ups" en- joyed the fun as well as the youngsters. The prize-winners amongst the girls were Eva Day, who sang "Whispering winds," and Polly Titchard, who gave a recitation. Amongst the boys, the winners were J. Beddard and E. Hirst. At the close three rousing British -cheers. were 1041110 r the friends who provided the treat: 'lf the donors had been present they would have been amply rewarded by the bright, happy ' /aces. The Making of Better Men and Women. On Sunday morning the meeting was devoted to "Stocktaking," being the last meeting of the old year. After several members had give* personal testimony as to the value of the school -,on their lives, the President gave a summary of the year's work.. The .lecturettes_ and ,the excellent short series of Bible studies had been responsible for the widening of the outlook on life. Members were learning to put aside petty prejudice and a narrow view of things, they •were learning to look beyond their own little circle of time and place, to see how events had developed over a wide area and during a great lapse of.time. They had-seen, by a very valu- able series of lecturettes on the 19th century movements, how the national, international, social, industrial and religious developments had been brought about, how every effect had , a cause. The Darwinian theory of evolution was not confined to biology, but was applied to history and nearly all the affairs of man. There was always going on a process of development. There was no such thing as chance to the evo- lution of society. The place of the Adult School in the general progress was pointed out. This lay in the direction of enabling men and women to look out upon life with intelligence, to do away with that blind, purposeless mode of living, which was only too common, and to replace it by a new point.of view, by an ideal- ism, by a vision; and in makinehetter men and women, in bringing them to realise their responsibilities as personal units and as citi- zens. This would gradually bripg shout that most desirable thing amongst mankind—true fellowship and brotherhood between man and man the world o'er.</p>
        <p>1 i giveii. i</p>
      </div>
