      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Mr. Chas. Dikes Replies to "Lab&amp;</head>
        <p>Mr. Chas. Dikes Replies to "Lab&amp;</p>
        <p>Omnia Vincit."</p>
        <p>POLFTICS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>DEFENCE AND EXPLANATION</head>
        <p>A DEFENCE AND EXPLANATION</p>
        <p>TO TIIE ED/TOR OF " THE EXAMINER." Str(—After a lapse of fifteen months, "Labor Omnia Vincit" has mustered courage to again open attack, and to conventional persons lei methods will appear somewhat noisome. lam _</p>
        <p>grateful for the absence a such qualm;; in-deed, i should have respected him more if, instead .of hinting, he had made the direct assertion. Secrecy is unnecessary, and can only lead to iuisunderstanding. I hope this is not what "L.0.V." requires. If a person is deliberately lying by creating false impressions for personal gain, then such a person merits public denun- ciation. This is quite different from an honest change in opinion and should be treated differ- ently, but facts and not fancies must be forth- coming. Now, the sum and substance a L.O.V.'s " letter is to the effect that I am deliberately exploiting a movement by organis- ing men for one purpose (industrial), and using them for another (poritical). I do not claim toe impartial and openly confess that I cannot read . . „ _</p>
        <p>any other meaning into "L.O.V.'s" letter. The meeting at which "L.0.V." gleaned Ids information that I hare changed my views, kc.,</p>
        <p>was convened for certain purpo- see. They -ho,'''t•e' not been stated, .nor has a correct impressiDn</p>
        <p>. of what was said been conveyed to your renders: I was engaged to "defend iiolitical action,":DV,- subject being chosen by the heads of the organ- isation who arranged the meeting. These people are "Syndicalists," and it may be accepted that my views were well known by them whnsf arrangements were made months before the meeting took place. Now, "L.0.V." knows-tfint T am a member of a political org,anisatioreand- have been so connected for years. During the period in which I am supposed to have opposed political action I was actually the secretary of the political body referred to. This is well known by " L.0.V." My utterances.from public platforms during recent strikes are toe well known for anyone to assume that I have evnr declared for political or industrial action us separate policies. One can only feel sorry for that type of mind so immobile as to fail to detect the limits of specified subjects. "L.0.V." assumes an entire knowledge of one's polky from a single address; if political action is sup- ported it of necessity negates industrial action, and vice-versa. This is his code of reasoning. The puerility of such method is only too obvious.</p>
        <p>If "L.0.V." desired a definite statement of policy, why did he not ask for it at the meeting, which was open for questions, and, afterwards, discussion? He chose rather to remain dumb, and deliberately concealed his flabby fission until a mean advantage could be taken. No report was taken of the meeting, and yet " L.0.V." quotes sentences and passages that I am sup- posad to have uttered. The fact is that "L.0.V." is punching his own shadow, as may be seen from his letter, where he asserts: "If strikes could benefit the workers, political action is unnecessary." That is " L.O.V.'s" opinion, not mine. I am further accused of saying that strikes have failed. This is also incorrect, and ".L.0.V.". knows that it ie incorrect. As a matter of fact, his letter is a gross perverdon of what took place. As to the supposed change of policy, the fol- lowing was stated in my first letter 15 months ago, when replying to "L.O.V.":— " Whilst one must confess that 'L.O.V.' is correct in his criticism of party politics, I, as one who believes in independent political action- and industrial organ isatiou, am compelled to— cross swords with him," tic. - • This does not contain any refutation of poli- tical action, and was made deliberately, to pre- vent that absurd notion being conveyed. There is plenty of room for honest criticism without misrepresentation, and those who desire to assist the workers in their struggle against Capitalism-can do better work than spilling ink over imaginary incongruities. lam prepared to fight industrially and politically with men f purpose, and by that I mean more than mere ulsome fetish worship. I shall not declare war with the enemy and flirt with their spies. The conditions of warfare demand variation in movements, and even the acceptance of defeat. It is a recognition of these factors that breed pessimism in minds incapable of nursing defeat until strong enough to fight again and win. Let. us let out.,"Of this purgatory of principal per- verting; there is other work to do. We want men of action—men who will try even though they fail. Let me remind "L.0.V." that he is, or once was, a Socialist; might I ask if he has changed his policy? or ? ?</p>
        <p>pef.yur- pose,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>DIRTY LINEN.</head>
        <p>DIRTY LINEN.</p>
        <p>There is one point that lam particularly anxious to inform him about; that is, my in- fluence with the thousands of men I represent. This influence is not dominating, but suggestive. I offer service, not coercion, and there is always the free choice to reject or accept anything I propose. This necessitates that I act in accord- ance with the instructions given, even though 1 disapprove. The outcome of such method has been a net gain of hundreds of pounds weekly to the general workers of Warrington, and has been carried out whilst obscure revolutionaries have sat dumb, .eagerly awaiting some oppor- tunity to find fault.</p>
        <p>When "Labor Omnia Vincit " suggests that I believe the workers gullible and ready to swal- low anything I offer,' he seems to forget the Position we are dealing with. My time has been too well occupied in pulling down heroes and combating- hero worship to make possible my own ascension by that staircase. The strength of the local Labour movement is cen- tred in the large number of active, though silent, workers within its ranks, and not any coercive and dominating individual. lam sorry " L.0.V." is not counted in this number. If Warrington sends a working man to Par- liament, so much the better for Warrington; but when " L.0.V." makes his concealed thrust, and has not the bowels openly to assert that certain men are scheming for these positions, it is well that we reflect on the interests for which. he is prostituting his pen. If "L.0.V." is troubled with a bad liver, let him seek some other avenue wherein to vent his spleen: the Labour movement wants enlivening, not croaking over. His remarks about men who part their hair in the middle and cultivate a style of speech leads me to say they choose that method of etiquette purposely to attract simpletons who cannot evade these obtrusions. It is also better to have a copy of Marx up Your jacket than to have your back scratched by men of avowed opposition to the workers' movement; it may he a little more expensive to carry, but it will at least enable you to stand erect.</p>
        <p>I am pleased he thinks me such an important person; his pseudo eulogy seems to have ob- scured his vision to his own importance. As the person who corrects and criticises the great he speaks of, he is certainly an indispensable quantity. Let him take his dirty linen to a competent laundry man and not parade it where some honest attempt is being made to illum- inate the obscure. The master crass are well served by the purchased service of their- hire- lings, but even they have the honesty to take .sides openly. If " L.0.V." desires to oppose .either industrial ,or political action by the workers, I am prepared to defend it through the , columns of the Press.</p>
        <p>CHAS. DUKES. 26, Dundonald-avenue, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Brick-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>The annual tea party and concert was held on Thursday, and after tea a satisfactory re- port of the year's working was given. Mr. W. Barton, senior president of the church, pre- sided over the concert, at which the following were the artistes: Miss Elsie Hewitt, Mrs. Sephton, Messrs. J. Sephton, Ashworth and W. Barton, jun. Mr. Duneley introduced his liv- ing waxworks.</p>
        <p>CLOTHS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suitinv, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to A.M. the lung, of unim- peachable Quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill. Laxey, Isle of Man; or G. 110LYBOYD &amp; SON 110, Station-street, Huddersfield.</p>
