      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>:7- • 1. DISTRICT /YEWS)</head>
        <p>:7- • 1. DISTRICT /YEWS)</p>
        <p>L Y M M.</p>
        <p>SKATING.</p>
        <p>During the festive season both the Lymm Cricket and Hockey Clubs have held successful whist drives and dances. Lieutenant-Colonel Legh is holding a three days' "shoot"; and on Thursday skating was in progress on various shallow waters in the district.</p>
        <p>WAXWORKS AND PICTURES AT THE WESLEYAN SCHOOLS. Apparently the scholars of the Wesleyan Sunday School specialise in living pictures and w_axworks, for the exhibition held in the School- room on Wednesday night was as excellent as its predecessors. The first part of the pro- gramme was devoted to .pictures given by the Primary Department, and after the opening chorus, "Gather, come, gather," consisted of Little Jack Horner, Little 13°-peep, Humpty Dumpty, Red Riding Hood, See-saw, Little Dolly Flinders, Father Christmas, and Children from Other Lands. Accompanying solos and recita- tions were contributed by Elsie Mairs, Esther Soanes, Ena Armstrong, Mildred Inglefield, and Ethel Lindley. The eider scholars and teachers provided the second portion of the programme, presenting the tableaux, " Faith, Hope and Charity," "Dream of Fair Women," "Two Strings to Her Bow," "Knave of Hearts," "In 1915," "The Passing Cloud," "Rosary," "The Waits," and "Britannia," Miss Winstanley and Mr. J. 11. Brazendale were the soloists. There were numerous gramophone selections. Mr. H. S. Brown was an efficient accompanist, a.nrl Mr. C. Moss cleverly manipulated the lime- light THE BIRTH OF 1914. Though many of the old inhabitants were content quietly to let in the NEM Year at their homes, the younger genera- tion celebrated the advent of A.D. 1914 in a more boisterous manner, with the result that the village and the road between there and Booth's Hill was for several hours a centre of gaiety. At 11.90 p.m. the Lymm Subscription Band played a well-known hymn tune at Booth's Hill' after which they headed a small torchlight pre- cession, which marched to the Cross, where they gave a musical programme, dance music being included, and Lymm's historic monument was soon looking down on a whirling party of merry_ makers. In the meanwhile the local campano_ logists had been busy in the Parish Church belfry, though their efforts at 11.55 p.m. were confined to ringing out the old, year with the single bell. However, as the last stroke of twelve was sounded they heralded the birth of A.D. 1914 with a full and joyous peal, in which 'they were supported by factory, boat, and engine whistles. The Free Church Council solemnised the nas- sing of the old and the advent of the New Year by holding a united watchnight service in the Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AT THE PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening a carol service was ield at the Primitive Methodist Church, when he choir rendered a programme of seasonable music. The items, whilst varying in style, were pleasantly sung, and included such pieces as "Ring, all ye harps of glory," "When Jesus Christ our Lord was born," "Hark ! What mean those Holy Voices?" and "Hail ! the morn." Appropriate Scriptural and other oadings were supplied by Mr. A. Brown, who ~.40 preached an inspiring sermon on "The s,...ipreme value of man," as shown by his text, L Cor. vi. 39, 20, and the birth, life and death of Christ. Mr. J. Edwards was the conductor and Miss Shore the organist. The annual Boxing Day tea and entertain- ment held in connection with the Primitive Methodist Sunday School were again a decided ..uccess. The good attendance at the tea tables .:as considerably augmented at the concert -.v hich followed. Mr. G. L. Welford presided, o.nd was supported by the Rev. R. W. Fergu- son and Mr. H. Champ. The choir commenced the programme with a carol (unaccompanied) "Brightest and best," and at a later stage they sang "The Star of Bethlehem." Recitations of both a humorous and topical nature were</p>
        <p>given by Frances Mottershead, Edith Sharpies, I May Vost, Emily Warham, Stanley Knowles, Alfred and Norman Priestner. The pianoforte solos of Mary Broadey, Esther Warham and William Sharpies were well received, as were the younger scholars in their Christmas song and "Children go." "Merry milkmaids" and "Good-night" were two pretty action songs, the former being given by five dainty Cheshire maidens, with a refrain sung by the "Villa- gers." "The Suffragettes" were of the dis- tinctly militant type, though on this occasion their operations were confined to raising the hearty laughter of the audience. The Misses Millicent Clare and Jenny Thornicroft should make first-rate "inciters." Another deservedly popular item was the sketch "A Modern Cin- derella," the performance of the characters, the Misses M. Broadey, M. Priestner, L. Priest- ner, Tonge, C. Tonge and M. Vost, being ex- cellent, the unstinted applause as the various incidents were unfolded showing the approval of the audience. Mr. H. Champ presented a large number of handsome prizes to ' the scholars who had merited them for good at- tendance and conduct during the last twelve I months. The Chairman announced that Arthur Miller had been successful in obtaining a first- class certificate in the recent Connexional Scripture examination, an announcement [ which was received with hearty applause. Class IV. were presented with the Challenge Shield. Mrs. Priestner and Miss Welford are to be congratulated upon the splendid result of their efforts in preparing the programme. On Saturday evening the elder ,scholars were entertained to tea, after which the usual party games were indulged in. The novelty this year was "The Suffragettes and their worth," or "Sold !"</p>
        <p>The younger members of the school had their party on Monday evening, when they, too, had a jolly time. A variation from the usual mode of giving the Christmas toys was that of the magic pond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive.—A very successful whist drive was held in the High Legh Schools on Mon- day night in aid of the children's summer sports. The event had been organised by the Sports Committee, and -as a result they should benefit to a considerable extent. The prize- winners were :—Ladies: 1, Miss Ford; 2, Miss Weston. Gentlemen: 1 Mr. F. Wardell; 2, Mr. Viz. Houghton. Consolation prizes: bliss Plumb and Mr. Tbornley. Much praise is due to .the ladies who assisted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
