      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>STOeKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOeKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH AND HOME RULE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SHOULD WE BE ALLOWED _O MANAGE</head>
        <p>SHOULD WE BE ALLOWED _O MANAGE</p>
        <p>OUR OWN AFFAIRS?</p>
        <p>WORKMEN'S DWELLINGS.</p>
        <p>IS PUBLIC AID ALLOWABLE?</p>
        <p>By a Special Correspondent.</p>
        <p>. . The forthcoming meeting on Tuesday evening next of the Parish Council will certainly have plenty of business to consider, for thereat will come up for deliberation the resolutions passed at the September Parish Meeting regarding public recreation grounds and allotments, public conveniences, library and newsroom. Also the topic, which appears to be a special pet of Mr. Councillor Bottomley, regarding the question of the I'arish Council dealing directly with such matters and questions as it is empow- ered by law to do, without dealing with them indirectly, as the delegated Parochial Com- mittee of the Rural District Council. The resolutions referred to are sure to give rise to a considerable amount of discussion, and, I learn, a variety of opinion. There are those, both in the Council and outside, who hold that recreation grounds, allotments and public con- veniences are not required in the parish. Others hold that recreation grounds for the youths are urgently needed, but that there is not the same necessity for allotments and conveniences.</p>
        <p>Direct Power. And, with regard to the Parish Council dealing directly with the matters referred to, it is, I hear, strongly argued by influential persons, both on the Council and off, that inasmuch as committees of the Council would have to `submit their reports to the whole Council, nothing would be gained by the so-called reform. More- over, there.would be the loss of influence which' the larger body—the District Council—must un- doubtedly add to the achievement and enfoice- ment of the requirements of the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Is There a Scarcity of Cottages?</head>
        <p>Is There a Scarcity of Cottages?</p>
        <p>- ' At the meeting of the Parochial Committee, which will precede the Parish Council meeting, Mr. Councillor Dukes will move his resolution, in which he urges that powers should be sought ,to enable a number of workmen's dwellings to be erected. If I am not 'mistaken, the District Council, by virtue of a somewhat recent Act of Parliament, have-already the powers, indicated in the resolution referred to. I understand that it is open to question whether there is such a scarcity of cottage property in the parish as Mr. Dukes alleges. One gentleman-7a Councillor— has intimated to me that he will require to be satisfied on that point before he gives his aid to the public erection of cottages. He further said that, in his opinion, there was no such scarcity, and that he should opposo the erection of cottages at the expense of Stockton Heath ratepayers, in order to provide a refuge for the stranger from other districts.</p>
        <p>The Letter-box.—The village Post Office offi.• cials were kept very busy during the festive season. The mails have been heavy, and the thanks of the "Heathens" are due ,to the capable staff, who coped with the extraordin- arily minimise as much as possible the delays, which heavy traffic in such a manner as to prevail at such a period. Those who have been disappointed are no doubt in a minority, for, on all hands, we hear complimentary remarks as to the numerous Christmas Day deliveries.</p>
        <p>Happy Children.—The treat in connection with the St. Thomas' Band of Hope, given by Mr. F. Houghton, took place in the Parish Room. on Boxing Day,, and the chilOen enjoyed themselves immensely. Mr. Whikeo` with sleight of hand tricks, and Miss Heath, with her recite- Cops, proved popular entertainers. Miss Hughes sang and danced exceeding lY well and was loudly applauded. Mr. T. Baguley took the part of Father Christmas, and distributed the prel3ehts to the ,children. lie: was heartily thanked for his services, on the proposition of Mr. Hallows. Mr. Stannard (secretary) proposed a. -vote of thanks to the Vicar, Mr. and Mrs. Houghton, Mr. C. Hallows, the workers, and the enter- tainers. Mr. Whiteman and Mr. C. :Hallows supported, and the vote was accorded unani- mously.</p>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist School.—At the annual tea and concert of the Hill Cliffe Sunday School on Boxing Day about 160 scholars and friends were present. The tea was provided by the lady teachers and was much appreciated. At the concert Mr. R. Billington, of Stratton, presid-,..i and an excellent programme was Submitted The following were the artistes: Misses Gladys Challinor and A. Moore, pianoforte solos; Misses E. Capells and G. Pemberton, songs; and recita- tions were given by nine of the .3unday school scholars. Mr. J. T. Roughsedge gave a humor- ous sketch, entitled peggy Tubb's False Teeth," and in addition there were violin solos by Master A. H. Mayhall, banjo solos by Mr. Caleb Whitley, and four action gongs by differ- ent classes in the school, together with a further humorous sketch, entitled ," Uncle John. from Yorkshire," given by the Hill Cliffs Dialogue Party, which is composed of Mrs. J. T. Roughs- edge and Misses S. F. Eley, G. Moss, T. F. Moss and Roughsedge. , During an inter,al , Mrs. W. Strange presented 101 prizes to the scholars. Messrs. J. Moss and J. T. Roughsedee presented reports on the work of the Sunday school, the evening concluding by Mr. W. Drewitt proposing, and Mr. IL Brockleiturst seconding, a comprehensive vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>SATURDAYS -JANUARY 3, 1914.</p>
        <p>The cantata "King Winter" was capably given by teachers and scholars of .tho school, and as they posed on the platform they presented an exceedingly pretty sight. King Winter and a Fairy Godmother were seated on their throne at the back of the stage, whilst, bent with age and surrounded by the beaming faces of young maidens, sat Father Time. Mrs. Gould and Mr. Dutton deserved every praise for the efforts they had taken to train the party. During an inteuval Mrs. Spensley, of Manchester, presented about 80 prizes to the scholars for attendance, under the chairmanship of Mr. Spensley, who ' spoke of his associations with the school as a scholar and teacher. He congratulated the teachers and the scholars upon their excellent year's work, and wished them all further Suc- cess in the future. After the younger children had dispersed, the remainder indulged in merry games-L-a fitting finish to a pleasant evening. Much of the success was brought about by the untiring work of Messrs. G. Whitfield and T. Brereton, the secretaries, Lind the Sunday school superintendent, Mr. J. Asher, who were capably assisted by an energetic committee of ladies.</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>AGED FOREGATHER.</head>
        <p>THE AGED FOREGATHER.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL TREAT TO THE OLD PEOPLE OF THE VILLAGE. 90 AND 84.</p>
        <p>The crowning of the "king and queen" has evidently become an annual affair at Stockton Heath, for on New Year's night this ceremony was performed for the second time, in the Parish Room. The occasion was the second annual old folk's treat, and it has been made a custom to " crown " the two oldest people present with cap and bonnet. This year the honourable title of " king " has again fallen upon Mr. James Lawton, who celebrated his 90th birthday last October, whilst the dignity of "queen" rests upon Mrs. Bristow, of Caw- dor-street, who is 84 years of age. The event is the outcome of.,a combined effort on the part of all the denomidations in the village, and in consequence no question of creed or faith creeps</p>
        <p>in. The crowning ceremony was Mr. J. Hallows, prior to ,the evening enter- tainment. performed by Mr. Lawfon, who was warmly received, said that in spite of his age he could read well, eat well and sleep well; in fact, there was "nothing ado amiss whatever." God had spared him and given him good health. He had always been a regular attender at church. He had six children living and there were four who were dead. The Rev. J. Collins presided, and jocularly remarked that a king and queen came to War- rington some time ago on a flying visit, but the king and queen of Stockton Heath were stick- ing there, and they could not get them away. Tea was provided in the afternoon, and as there were numbers of old people too infirm to attend, part of the cheer was sent to their homes. An excellent concert was provided, when the first half was given by Mr. W. H. Rigby's party of "Merry- Dandies," who gave concerted items. The individual efforts of Mr. W. H. Rigby and the Misses E. Lea, Edith Catherall, J. Lowe and Ethel C,atherall, and Master S. Dutton were warmly received. The second part of the programme was given by the following :—Hill Cliffe Sketch Party, in a humorous piece, "Uncle John from York- Whitley, banjoist; and .Mr. Roughsedga, York- shire"; Mr. Bert Stitith, violinist; Mr. C. in a humorous sketch. The accontanists were Miss Greenhalgh and Mrs. H. Forman. The committee, who worked so hard for a successful result, was composed of Messrs, J. Hallows, chairifitaan; J. Miller, secretary ; W. Grounds, assisnlnt secretary; and G. Whitfield, A. Mounfield, John Roughsedge, James Rough- sedge, Clark, T. Moss, J. Moss, C. Hallows, T. Miller, A. Jones, A. Stannard, R. H. Smith and Brocklehurst. Valuable assistance was ren- dered by a number of ladies, amongst whom were the following: Mesdames Joseph and Thomas Moss, James and John Roughsedge, Clark, Whitfield, Smith, A. Jones, J. Houghton, Miller, Pearce, and. Hallows, and the Misses Grounds, Welsby, Sorb:al, and Hallows. At the conclusion presents in the form of tobacco and tea were given.</p>
        <p>New Year and the Chapels.—Watchnight services were held at the Wesleyan and Inde- pendent Methodist -Chapels, where the services were conducted by Mr- R. T. Moss and Messrs. A. Mounfield and Roberts respectively.</p>
        <p>The Tables Turned.—The annual social in con- neetion with the Stockton Reath Wesley Guild was held on Boxing Day, when the bachelors arranged an excellent evening's enjoyment. The Young men formed themselves into a 'concert , party, each one contributing a song or some lother item. In the refreshment departnient they quite eclipsed the ladies by the smart way in which they attended to their work; and it was Creditable that so little ,lint was required after the "cutting up" operations! They showed that mere man was capable of both pre- paring food for the table and entertaining.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Prize Choir.—All praise is due to the members of the Stockton Heath In- dependent Methodist Choir, who, on New Year's Day, succeeded in bringing home the Fischer Challenge Cup, which was placed for competi- tion at the fifth annual Manchester Eistedfodd in the Chorlton Town Hall. There was anout- burst of applause when it was announced that Stockton Heath were the winners, and satis- faction was expressed M the award of the ad- judicators. The competitions were arranged by the Manchester Booth-street East Welsh Con- gregational Church,. and for the first time dur- ing the four years the proceedings were graced " by the presence of the Lord Mayor of Manches- ter, who commended those present on spend- ing the holiday in the advancement of art, music and literature. The test piece was Elgar's "Oh, happy eyes, and the obtaining of 87 marks by the winners must give every satisfaction to Mr. Edward Roberts, the leader. The choir was composed of 25 voices. In addition to obtaining the cup they won ~e 5. The runners-up were the Chorlton-road, Man- chester, Welsh Chapel, who received 85 marks.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Sunday School Treat—lt was a merry party that assembled in the Stockton Heitth Wesleyan Schoolroom on. New Year's night, on the occasion of the annual Sunday school treat and prize distribution. . Late in the afternoon tea was provided by the teachers Ind members of the Ladies' Sunday School Class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PA DGATE.</head>
        <p>PA DGATE.</p>
        <p>Watchnight Services.—ViTatchnight services were held at the Parish Church and also at the Wesleyan Chapel, where the Rev. E. Morgan officiated.</p>
        <p>Singing for Others.—The Wesleyan carol singers, who were out last week, were fortunate in collecting a good sum, which they have decided to hand over to Dr. StePhenson's Homes. The singers gave an excellent account of them- selves last Sunday in the Wesley Hall. ~ Carols.—At Christ Church last Sunday a ser- vice of carols was held, when the Vicar took the readings. The choir acquitted thernselves. admirably in the Christmas anthem, "Emmanuel," Mr. Wilfred Fryar taking the 1 tenor solo with pleasing effect. M:hl Class Tea.—The members of the Pad- gate Wesleyan Men's Bible Class were enter- tained to tea, along with their friends, on Thursday, by the generosity of their leader, Mr. J. Jarman. A free-and-easy social followed the tea. Musical items we submitted by Miss Jarman, who also acted as accompanist to songs. contributed by, Messrs. E. Wood and Appleton. Readings were given by Messrs. H. Monks and Woods, and recitaions by Miss Tomlin and Mrs. Wood. Mr. Jarman was heartily praised for his kindness.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Sunday School.—ln connection with the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School a very enjoyable open Sunday was held last week in the Wesley Hall, when Mr. Roscoller presided. An acceptable musical programme was given, which included selections by the children's choir, trained by Mr. W. J. Bate. Misses Lily Sutton, G. Appleton and A. Smith gave appro- priate recitations, while the Misses J. Ward and M. Appleton sweetly rendered a sacred duet. The Wesleyan carol singers obliged with a couple of items. Mr. W. Cutland, of Ampthill, who was a former teacher in the school, gave an address, in 'which he asked the children to look back over-the past year and see if they could .koake any improvements in themselves. If they could not, they shduld endeavour to seize every opportunity that came their way during 1914.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BEST NEW YEAR PRESENT,</head>
        <p>THE BEST NEW YEAR PRESENT,</p>
        <p>PUBLIC-HOUSE SCENE.</p>
        <p>Ex-Barman's, Opinion of Barmen.</p>
        <p>The story of a melee in a Warrington public- house and an assault on a barman was related at the Borough Court on Saturday. when Thomas Sullivan, ii stranger to the town, was charged before Mr. R. T. Fairclough and other magistrates with being drunk and refusing to quit the Vine Tavern in Market-street, and also with assaulting Samuel Manning. P.C. Fish said that at 4.45 on the previous day he.was in company with P.C. Wyse, when they were called by the licensee of the Vine Tavern to eject the prisoner. Sullivan was taken into custody and on the way to the Police , Station he used very bad language. Later the officer saw the acting manager, Mr. Manning, who had a wound in the palm of his hand, where, he alleged, prisoner had bit him. Mr. Ma -ling said the prisoner was very drunk, r-,1 he asked the man to go out. Sulli- van struck at him, they closed, and both fell to the flo)r. A struggle ensued, during which prise-ier kicked him and bit him, and through an injury which witness then received he was at. 1-,sent unable to follow his employment. Mr. _o wen, the manager, went to his assist- ance, and the police were summoned. Prisoner remarked that he was thrown to-the floor by three men. He could not have bitten I the witness because he had no teeth. . .</p>
        <p>• Sergt. Metcalfe, who examined the prisoner's mouth, assured the Bench that the Prisoner had teeth. John Bowen, manager of the Vine Tavern, Said he was upstairs tit the time. He heard a noise, and on investigating the cause founil the last witness and prisoner in a desperate struggle on the floor. He sent for the police when he saw the condition of the man. '</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable pointed out that the pris- oner went in the house with a halfpenny in his possession, and there was no possibility of pay- ing for any drink. He was a drunken ruffian. The prisoner made a statement alleging that he had been severely handled whilst in the public-house. He had been a barman, and knew the difficulty they worked under. He was cer- tain he would not cause any bother if he could help it. Later prisoner remarked that barmen were the worst class of men •in the world, which elicited the retort from the Chief Constable: "No wonder you said you had been one." (Laughter.) Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months.</p>
        <p>in his Pr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CAR INCIDENT. Silver Brooch and ,Tle That Were</head>
        <p>CAR INCIDENT. Silver Brooch and ,Tle That Were</p>
        <p>" Lost."</p>
        <p>An incident which was enacted in a Corpora- tion tramcar came to light at the Borough Police Court :on Tuesday morning, when Mary Charnock, married woman, of 9, Worrall's-row, was charged with stealing on December 29 a lady's silver brooch and a silk tie, altogether value £2, the property of Mrs. Elizabeth Atkin- son, who resides at 118, Wilderspool-road, and has a business in Bridge-street. The Chief Constable stated that on the pre- vious day Mrs. Atkinson boarded the 4.30 p.m. tramcar from Rylands-street to Sankey; and when it arrived at the end of Milner—street she left the car. On returning to Bridge-street she missed the brooch, and inquired whether one had been found by the con- ductor. A statement was made to the effect that he had seen a woman in the car, who had a baby on her lap. The woman was endeavouring to pick up a Bilk tie, and he , picked it up and handed it to her. It was re- ported to the police, and Detective Summers made inquiries, with the result that he called at prisoner's house. Upon telling her what he wanted, she said she had the brooch in the cup- board, but did not know its value. The baby had dropped one of its socks and she was en- deavouring to get it. The conductor picked up the silk tie and placed it at the side of her. Corroborative evidence was given by Mrs. Atkinson and Detective Summers. Richard Leech, the conductor of the car, who lives at 53, Forshaw-street, said that when&amp;the car reached Thewlis-street lie saw the woman trying to pick up what he thought was a small handkerchief. She had a child on her knee. He picked it up and gave it to her. lie did not see a child's sock. If there had been one he would have seen it. De was not aware there was a brooch in the tie. The prisoner strongly denied that the con- ductor gave her the brooch. If e placed it beside her, and she gave the tie to the little girl today with. 'There was a sock on the floor of the car. The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) said the Bench were going to be lenient with her. Of course, it was clearly her duty to say the pro- perty was not hers, but under the circum- stances she would have to pay 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SAD CASE OF NEGLECT. Wife's Proportion of Husband's Income</head>
        <p>SAD CASE OF NEGLECT. Wife's Proportion of Husband's Income</p>
        <p>A pitiable tale of family neglect was- told to the Mayor and other magistrates at the Borough Court on Monday, during which it was men- tioned that the husband gave his wife only ..e1 2s. 6d. out of over £l5 he had lately drawn in wages. The prisoner was Walter Ellis, who gave an address in Collin-street, Warrington. He was charged with neglecting his children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr. A. Browne, appearing for the N.S.P.C.C., said the defendant was a wire stringer, and he had been employed by William Robinson, Ltd., at very good wages. There were five children. Inspector Wainwright had known him ever since he came to Warrington. When the baby was born the prisoner left his wife, and she and her two eldest children had had to provide money to keep the home together. She saw her husband lately in Warrington, and asked him what he was going to do. He replied that he could do nothing, because he was out of work. Prisoner had given his wife only ..61 28. 6d. out of over .215 he had lately drawn in wages. Elizabeth Ellis, prisoner's wife, who resides at 17, Longford-street, corroborated Mr. Browne's statement. She said she was a fustian cutter, but there was no work to be done. he</p>
      </div>
