      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Untidy Habits. In his timely protest against the littering of pur streets with scraps of waste paper, "A Visitor " wrote that "in Germany the police would be after offenders." The thing is true. It would hardly be an exaggeration to say that the word " Verboten " is writ large over the face of that overrated land. In glancing over some of our own modern elaborate and COM • prehensive bylaws, I have sometimes thought we were steadily tending that way. Isers,m- shy, I strongly- deprecate.a closer copy lf the German example in this re:Speet ; still, I hegin to fear that,bothing short Of compulsion will induce the multitude to cultivate habits- of lidir.pss. I have lived in. two or three towns the principal thoroughfares of -which were plentifully jre • vided with wire 'baskets for the reception of orange and banana peel, old paper, and the like; but really the results were not encourag- ing. Some people will actually go out of their Way to make of such things a nuisance and annoyance. I have been in places where the bulk of the male population seemed to sup off fried fish and chiPped pbtatoes at least on Sat- urday nights, and invariably to eat these deli- cacies on their way home. The paper appeared to be usually dropped just Where the meal hap- pened to finish; nay, worse, in cases not a few, with unconsurned contents still remaining, it would be deliberately and maliciously deposited down the cellar grate or on the door- step or in the front garden of sonic inoffensive householder, there to lie until discovered some time during the Sunday. Then there is the selfish smoker's thoughtless habit of striking his matches on the front of public buildings and on newly-painted lamp-posts, and even on shop fronts and private houses. This is intolerable ; hence our gradual approximation to the German system of rigorous repressive bylaWs, and there Will be no help for this unless the average man in the street speedily reforms. Scalped in the Mill. The painful accident to the girl Downridge while following her employment at Gockhedge Mill reminded me especially of two which have occurred during my residence in Lancashire cotton towns In both these cases—so horrible as to have left indelible impressions on my mind—the victims were sweeping beneath the machinery while it was in motion, and, their long hair being caught by whirling wheels, were literally scalped before assistance could be given. After careful treatment in the Infirmary, both recovered, but of course they had to wear artificial hair thenceforth. In connection with one of them there is a very different story, but it is too lengthy for present insertion.</p>
        <p>Canal Dangers. The canals abound in such risky spots as the one where the unfortunate man Nicholson, on whose body an inquest was held at Thelwall last week, lost his life. When I was in the Rochdale district there wa one precisely like it near the Littleborough Railway Station, at which crowds of visitors to Hollingworth Lake alight at week- ends during the summer. A solitary stranger passing along the subway might easily step into the canal, and quite a number of lives were lost in that way before the authorities could be prevailed upon to rail the water off at that point. There are any number of these death- traps about the country.</p>
        <p>The Huncian Boy. . The other night, while waiting for a train, I overheard three or four newslads comparing notes as to Christmas boxes. Two of them evi- dently belonged to the station bookstall, and one of these was not only triumphant on his . for at the n account but inclined to be somewhat sar- casticexpense of the other. " Oh," ho exclaimed the information of .the third party, he had to take a book right up to vicarage on th' hillside yonder, and he told me it would be a bob certain, but when lie sent his card in th' servant gave it him •back and Said they wasn't at home, and he eould see th' Parson's Wife through th' winder all th' time! He thought of going to th' Isfethody'i alt but, got that job, and th' minister opened his heart to me—thrippence more to this kid l"</p>
        <p>A Workhouse Incident. The account of the quantity of plum pudding consumed on Christ Mas day at the Workhouse rein inded me of an incident which I once wit- nessed on such an occasion. One of the imbe- ciles gorged himself with pudding so that the responsible officers refused his demand for more. The Chairman of the Board strolled his way, and in response to the wily felloW's Prompt appeal, said "Isidro pudding? Cer- tainly. Here, waiter, bring this man some pud- ding. Its Christmas, you know." The greedy chap emptied his plate almost immediately, and again began to cry out for more. Tken the doctor interposed with a peremptory order to . the contrary, so the insatiable•one cast about for other means of gratification. Directly oppo- site to him sat an apparently quiet and harm- less 'old inmate very deliberately enjoying his Pudding, so he reached across the table and helped himself to a lump of this. The exasper- ated sufferer revenged himself by dealing the Other a tremendous blow-on the top of the head With his big metal spoon. The pudding-lover “did not seem to mind this in the least; to all appearance, indeed, he did not feel it. Any- how, on the next favourable opportunity, he stole another piece of pudding, and received as a matter of course another whack with the spoon that would have sickened an ordinary mortal.</p>
        <p>Ye Gode Olde Tymes. Not very long ago I mentioned that 1-had temporary possession of a local directory pub- lished in 1825 calculated to do good to the eye- sight of " A.M." This came to mind as I read the account of Mr. and Mrs.. Tilling's golden wedding celebration, for in the course of the interesting interview the former told of an old- tine express canal boat service the particulars of which I had already seen in the ancient vol- time referred to. It goes to confirm the literal exactness of the old gentleman's version.</p>
        <p>Christmas Fare—Now and Then. Whence did the turkey, —which has of late Years come to be the pride of the Christmas dinner table, derive its name? I have some- where read that when Captain John Smith, fresh from his wars with the Turks, saw this bird in the Virginia forest strutting before the bens, he roared with laughter. " The bird," said he, "is exactly like a proud Turk, show- ing off before his harem." And so it, c,ame to be known as the turkey, now theGtirisimas Piece de resistance in many lands. In last Week's " Examiner " the writer on "Women's Questions" had a seasonable paragraph whicfl set me thinking of the days when the Christmas repast included not only boar's head, but swan (Chaucer alludes to a "fat swan roasted ") and even peacock; in fact, it was over the latter dish that the knights of old, raising their swords, were wont to take the oath of chivalry, the dish being borne by some visiting beauty of high birth.</p>
        <p>The Danger of Indigestion, Indigestion is a very real danger, a menace to your health and strength. It robs you of the nourishment you should Obtain from the food you eat • it loads the system with impurities that find their way Into the blood, causing headaches, languor, raid blotchy skin. Certainly you can't afford to ignore indigestion. It won't let You, in any c' s'. You will, therefore, be well advised to take an occasional dose of Mother Siegel's Syrup, the great remedy for the common ailments of the Stomach, liver, and bowels. There is nothing better 'for indigesti9n, or for biliousness, constipation, flatulence, • headaches, and pains after ea.l.- • fag. Not only does Mother Seigel's Syrcp BPeedily banish the symptoms of impaired ill- 4e3tion, but it keeps theta away altogether</p>
        <p>)I.lldi</p>
      </div>
