      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Pressing Social Problem of Long</head>
        <p>A Pressing Social Problem of Long</p>
        <p>Standing,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PAST EFF.ORTS TO EXTERMINATE THE VAGRANTS.</head>
        <p>PAST EFF.ORTS TO EXTERMINATE THE VAGRANTS.</p>
        <p>By -ARTHUR</p>
        <p>France Ilea been taking in hand thdliroblem of the vagrant,---and-- is at the present -ritOment putting into operation a rather drastic law. It .is described in an article in the "Daily Graphic." "Since November• 15 every tramp and vaga- bond found ‘'vandering in France has been taken into the police-statiOns to be Measured and photographed, and sent out with a 'document giving all particulars regarding their persons and past record. This paper ' they must produce , at any .moment for verification. All this is in accordance with a law passed in 1912 making it cOmpulsory for three classes who habitually use the roads to so register themselves. These classes are itinerant dealers and pedlars at fairs and markets and tramps and vagabonds. " Regarding the first, two chisseS, the new law is more protective than vexatious. Though they have to declare themselves, the mere pro- duction of their' papers will at once show their bona-fides, and a number which all must carry will allow- headquarters to learn at once the history of all the wandering family. VieSe two classes have to obtain official books containing proofs Of their identity, photograph, name, nationality, date and place of birth, last resi- dence, and-profesqion. • . " The measure is more severe for tramps who were -described by a Senator when the law was vagabonds without house, hoe, or eaesuio hntrsyßy N'v Nh-toi under the appearance of a problematical. prafession, wander the roads without caring either for the laws of health or deency, or the laws of the land.. From. now every individual reputed to be a tramp 'mist -procure a book of anthrOpornetric identity, which the authoritis are free to refuse. This hook, besides having all the indications corn- -111011 mon to., the other two classes, must have two. photographs, face and profile, finger-prints of both hands, and all particulars Of the person of the individual according to the Bertillon method. Heads of families must carry • aco ' l- lective book in •Whiell Particulars of each mem- ber of the family over two years 'old Mgt be recorded, even to linger-prints: Ever tramt;, on making a star of even twenty-four hours in a commune, must present. his book at the mairie to be endorsed.,</p>
        <p>to the number of tramps in France, it is impossible to give the exact figure, for registra- tion is still going on, hut 3,500 have already been measured and photographed. It is esti- mated that the total will reaCil Over 10,000." Our neighbours across the Channel have good reasons for corning into grips with the vagrant. In recent years the number of undetected crimes and robberies, committed presumably by the nomad,. has increased to an alarming extent, and it is hoped that the registration will lead to a .diminution of crime.</p>
        <p>As to its effects, WC will not offer a guess. Whether the tramps (whom Charlet Lamb declared to be the only free men in the universe) will find the strain upon their liberty too great, and whether it will lead to their migration to other shores, remains to be seen. The subject, howeVer' raises many memories to those who are versed in the past, and recalls other very drastic and unsuccessful efforts to deal with the unregistered wanderers of Europe. •</p>
        <p>TuE TRAMP AS A SURVIVAL. We need not try to find the origin of the trump by looking back. ,The earliest daWn is lilted with the spectacle not Merely of tramps.; but of " tramp " nations. Men swarmed in tribes like hives of bees, and the process has only recently come ito an end. . Possibly the triunp does but keep alive in our midst the primeval instinct. • . .</p>
        <p>Certainly we shall do no harm in treating him as a relic as well aS'a nuisance.</p>
        <p>THE MARK OF THE SPECIES.</p>
        <p>Nobody can get away from the interest of the gipsy, notwithstanding the generations of abuse and persecution that our ancestors showered upon them. Nor is the 'commoner species uninteresting. Ile bears the family likeness of the tribe. He appears in the part of a 'Hilliness individual, who wants but very, very little to make him happy. Ile bears ill-will to no single creatnre, least of all to those who. give him good advice instead of money. His capacity for receiving marks of anger is complete, and advice has less effect than a quotation from Cicero or Sophocles. The counsel of a whole Board of Guardians leaes him utterly unmoved. It is the price he pays for his lodging, and he leaves the chamber without a grudge against the men who have tried so hard to disturb his peace of mind. Possibly he is not so daring or picturesque as his ancestor centuries back. But there are reasons for that. This is a scientific age, which can see through mere mummery. " And so the faked-up wound? and manufactured illness has had to be abandoned. That the reader may study the tramp as he was in a pre-scientitic age, we will quote from a Nr.icarly.tinrarLeutahnedr Nn, Taw,. yi n fourte i.es tiiinugn dbr.oe do ky eeda iitseda gboy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ROADSTERS OF FOUR CENTURIES</head>
        <p>THE ROADSTERS OF FOUR CENTURIES</p>
        <p>AGO. • It is called "The Book of Vagabonds and Beggars," and the Reformer issued it in 1529. This is a list of sonic of the characters who adorned the lanes of Geruatiny. Beggars or those who Plainly and simply awts.'went about with wentr eaadbo gatherers, tialesi keirnsg, their wives and children with them, re in ragged garments, collecting food, &amp;c. These carried cooking utensils and never did anything but beg from the day of their birth to the day of their death. • Liberated prisoners, who said they were deprived of their character and buengjtlyguesdbecause they said tllitNeyliNiyioeio•ed.depr_ived from earning a different e Cyipples, many of when' shammed lame- ness or deformity. • 'Church mendicants'', producing false cre- dentials to show that they were collecting alms for religious purposes. Learned beggars, young scholars or students, who said they had naught on earth but the alms that people goy( them, and that they were furthering their studies for the Church. Pretended murderers, who asserted that they had taken a man's life away, and had afterwards been seized by remorse, though it was in self-defence, and that this had driven them to a wandering life. Wives of the above. Lepers, or those suffering from. loathsome diseases.. Spurious beggars, who pretended that, like the Capuchin friars, they Wore tarily' oor Pretended noblemen end kniigS, who travelled about Well dregged, 'Sting that they had Siiffered by win., lire; or captivity, or had been driven away and lost all they had. , • , • Pretended merchants, . who produced documents to show that they had been possessed of merchandise which had been , Baptised Jewesses, who had turned Christians, and been driven out by their</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>own people and sought the charity of . Christendom.</p>
        <p>Pretended. pilgrims. Begg,irs, suffering with sores.' .Strof ers, professing to country people, that they were possessed of magic power and could prevent murrian and disease. Knaves, with falling sickness, who took fits and assumed sudden illness. Invalids, alleging that they had suffered for years with incurable ailments, or whose wives or families were alleged to be so False ,begging priests. , Blind beggars. Naked beggars, whose apparel was so very scanty as to arouse universal pity. Silly or half-witted beggars, who were apparently bereft of sonic of their incptal faculties. . •</p>
        <p>llanginen,-who had given up their hateful vocatiop at the call of conscience and sought the sympathy of all humane 'folks. Women, to clothed as to lead to the belief that they were pregnant. Mendicants, who besmeared themselves with a yellow fluid to stimulate jaundice. Vagrants, professing to be doing penance. Blind harpers. Goose shearers, or- those.who put on good clothes and begged; saying that they had lairi ill a long lime, and were mechanics, who had eNpentled all their goods and were ashamed- to beg, but asked that they might _be helped to: procee4- on their journey.</p>
        <p>, . This, is only part of Luther's list, but it is enough to slMw. that "Good folks- of every kind" in Gecp,iany needed to blend discretion with, their charity; The list is interesting, as showing to what lengths the business of beg- ging_ was carried in the medieval period that was then coining to an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>1)111STIC LEGISLATION.</head>
        <p>1)111STIC LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>'Against the vagrants 'legislation has been ;directed in all ages. Very severe laws words passed in the Saxon had .Norman periods in- England. At, a later dale in the 16th century drastic nfeasures Were adopted, which might have been ekpeeted to exterminate the tramp for ever. •</p>
        <p>lie was, to be whipped when first caught,, on the 'next offence have his ears cropped, and for a third offence to suffer death as a felon and an enemy of the commonwealth. But he survived even this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PENALTY OF SLAVERY.</head>
        <p>THE PENALTY OF SLAVERY.</p>
        <p>In 1547 the law relating to vagrants was amended. On the first .conviction he was branded with a "V" on the shoulder and made a slave for two years. AS a slave he could be claimed and fed by anyone and forced to work by flogging; his food to 'Consist. of bread and water. This is pretty drastic as we view things, but it was then considered *try reasonable treatment for a social pest, and should the prisoner run away he was branded upon the face, when captured, with the letter "S," and made a slave - for life, being set to work in chains upon the road. . Yet vagrancy survived. It has survival to become- more scientific. It has dropped tre, crude tricks that only ap- pealed to an ignorant age, but in all its essen- tial elements it lives, and lives vigorously.</p>
        <p>THE TRAMP SCIENTIFIC. . What,thord.perfCo Ifinn'tlie statement made by a tramp to 4 lady, who was know to be a ttwosoithist. .. . " Why don't 1 work?" echoed this unshaven, burly man. " Madam, I am taking rest after work done in my previous incianations. This IS my seventh time 'on earth, and as I worked ' hard during the other six, it is my Sabba period, and lam ordained to rest. _ ,</p>
        <p>..... "The first time I was born I worked without pay on the Tower of Babel. The nest time I was born in Egypt, when •the Pyramids were going up, and Pharaoh v*is a hOly terror and never, kept a pay-boss. During mY third in- carnation I worked for eighteen hours a day in GreCk galley for food and, clothes, and got Precious little of either. Then I was a Boman soldier, fighting for glory, and got killed before Pay-day. , „ _ .</p>
        <p>"The fifth time I WaS a Peruvian, worked in a mine, and didn't see sunshine for years. It was iny Audi again to die before the pay-year Caine rOund. The last time I was a Ilbssian serf, and had the hardest time of all. , "No, madam, I can't chop the firewood. This is my 'existence off.' I am going round collecting the arrears that are due to rue for vork faithfully done in previous incarnations. ".Thank you, madam. Theosophy is a grand and comforting science.' Doubtless this well-read specimen had the lady's home labelled upon his chart as the abode of occult interests.'</p>
        <p>Only a churl would withhold praise from a man who could balance a situation so nicely.</p>
        <p>TRAMPS' sCHARTS. . Upon the subject of (Innis and the tramp's use of them, much might be written. Mr. DOWIICS Shaw, who made a close study of the 'tramp, has shown that the professional :is always well equipped with knowledge. A com- plete Freemasonry exists, and they pass to each other 'accurate information as to the best houses, the outstanding dangers, the most con- venient doss-houses, the good and bad roads. Those who Can be won by talking and those who respond to 'silent grief are Carefully 'narked; and dog kennels are marked as aecu- rately•as the rocks upon a seaman's chart. There are social degrees, too, among the roadsters. Thus, the gipsy will not on any account miN with any of the common species of vagrants. Has not the gipsy a „pedigree and a past? And has he not a language of his own and a very proper pride of ancestry? Of course he has. But space won't allow of a long diversion here to prove the claim. We end where we began. The tramp is a survival. lie r e loves the n md part hnetroaad ausnttlates6ailoprlot of the sea f tt chaining-ul)he befeanni family oste: elianstes aahssi enhao t‘nv yeu- ei set ynl idvfeiodi s taao. lion. lie ce thousand years. But. he doesn't mind. And why should he, if he is happier than lie people who classify him? He has handed on his spirit as a legacy to anyone who will accept it, and will doubtless do so for generations.</p>
        <p>THE MOVCHER'S LEGACY. Once the legacy broke out in verse. Upon the white-washed wall of the casual ward of the Ashford Union Workhouse an aged tramp wrote in pencil:</p>
        <p>The sailor loves his ship, The soldier loves his camp, 13ut give to me the. turnpihe road, To live and die a tramp.</p>
        <p>Farewell, ye "mouchers," my time is past, I've stuck to ,"mouching" to the last;</p>
        <p>Weep flof for 'me, or pity- take, But stick to mooching" for. my sake.</p>
        <p>There spoke the true vagrant. lie loved vagrancy for its own sake, and having wfitten his exhortation he died.</p>
        <p>Yet who can view the tramp without a touch</p>
        <p>Continued in next column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CHRISTMAS TREE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TREE.</p>
        <p>Presents for the Infirmary In-patients.</p>
        <p>MRS. HENRY GREENALL OFFICIATES.</p>
        <p>Those who were destined by circumstances to spend the festive season as in-patients at the Infirmary were in no way forgotten, and on Saturday afternoon they, as well as the nurses, received handsome presents, which had been ...subscribed for by the public. Mr. J. H. Sme- thurst (chairman of the Board of Management) has devoted a large amount of time and trouble to the collecting of the money, and his " star " turn at the theatres proved a successful one. Afternoon tea was partaken of, and the patients had been transferred to the top ward, new pavilion, by members of the St. John Ambu- lance Brigade. The matron and staff had taste- fully decorated the room, and the Christmas tree was most lavishly, yet artistically, bedecked. Messrs. Bibby were responsible for the electrical effects. Mrs. Henry Greenall distributed the presents, Mr. J. H. Smethurst presiding. Amongst those present were .the Mayor and Mayoress, Mr. Henry dreenall, Mr. and Mrs. W. P. Rylands, Alderman Smethurst and the Misses Smethuret, Mrs. Kirkland Rylands, Alderman Burton (chairman Working Men's Committee), Mr. G. Walker (vice-chairman), Dirs. J. 11. Smethurst, Captain Norton, RE., Mrs. Broadbent and the Misses Broadbent, Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Frost, Councillor Dr. G. W. Joseph, Dr. and Mrs. Sutherland, Dr. F. R. and Mrs. Segar, Dr. F. Carson (junior house surgeon), Rev. 11. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Rev. Father Huggins, Rev. Father Wilson, Rev. W. Bracecamp, the Misses White, Miss Bessie Dut- ton, Mr. 11. Collin, Mr. W. Daintith, Mrs. F. A. Murray and Miss Murray, and' Mr. J. H. J. Harapson (Infirmary secretary). Amongst the patients was Alderman Pier- who, we are glad to state, is looking much better.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John and Mrs. Yonge wrote from Southport expressing their regrets at being un- avoidably absent, and they sent their hearty and best wishes. Sir Gilbert Greenall was also unable to attend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>How the Treat was Provided.</head>
        <p>How the Treat was Provided.</p>
        <p>In his opening remarks Mr. SMETHURST an- nounced that Mrs. Greenall had added a new sixpence to the present for each patient. He thanked all those who had helped in any way; I the subscribers; the managers of the various places of amusement for their kindness in allow- ing him to go on-the stage, and their staffs; the , Ambulance men connected with Messrs. Cros- field's works, who were always ready and willing to help in the collection; the Infirmary staff for the work they had put in to make the life of those in the institutidn a pleasure at Christ- as time. He thanked them publicly for the work they had done during the past year.. He then praised the beautiful decorations, and stated that shortly the 350 out-patients of the institution would receive presents. (Applause.) Ile thought it was the duty of the townspeople to do whatever they could to make the lives of unfortunate individuals as bright and happy as possible. They accorded a most hearty wel- come to Mrs. Greenall and to the Mayor and Mayoress. He hoped that by what his Worship saw he would begin to take even greater in- terest in the institution. (Applause.) Mrs. GREENALL, then handed out the presents, and said she was glad to.be able to do that little service for those who were unavoidably pre- vented from being in their home circles. She congratulated the matron- and the nurses upon having the room so cheery and bright by their decorations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Thanking Mrs: Greanall.</head>
        <p>Thanking Mrs: Greanall.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR proposed a hearty vote of thanks - to Mrs. Greenall, and said they all knew the wok she did in alleviating distress and suffer- ing. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Hu.Asps seconded and wished all the patients a speedy and complete recovery, and a happy and prosperous New Year. (Hear, hear.) Their thanks, he said, were due to Mr. Smethurst and Mr. Hampson, and the matron and staff. Mrs. Greenall *as also deserving of their heartiest thanks. (Applause.) Mrs. GREENALL suitably responded. A number of the visitors went on a tour of inspection of the institution, and were greatly impressed by what they saw.</p>
        <p>Excellent music was supplied by Messrs. Wilkes (at the piano), Clarke and Law.</p>
        <p>CONCERNING TRAMPS—continued..</p>
        <p>,c 0 ifttit::nea useful members of the nuil of pity. Dean Swift wrote in-172-97 " Whoever could find out a fair and easy method of mak- ing wake up he might per- Rh, would deserve so well of the public as to have his statue set up for a pre- server of the nation." sewPcuple sound, Imps be surprised that after two hundred e Dean could the statue has not been set up, and that years we are still groping after that "easy method" of harnessing the tramp,.</p>
        <p>me 'chart, width was found . THE TRAMP'S CHART. It is evidently a rough plan uplonhaayetib.aelfz.e of the country in the neighbourhood of Edin- burgh, as the name of that citY appears upon the document. Its interest lies in the way in which certain houses are located and marked With signs, of which the following are copies</p>
