      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>In the district of Meseritz a woman who can prove by official papers that she was 120 years old on October 15th los been found by the</p>
        <p>"Frankfurter Zeitung." She is believed to be the only person in Germany who saw Napo- leon's army marching on Moscow.</p>
        <p>New Material for Motor Roads. Experimental tests giving satisfactory results have been made in France, with a new road- surfacing material, which has. been devised specially for automobile traffic. It consists of a mixture of what is called "iron straw," which consists of line filaments of iron in a wiry or brous mass mixed with cement and sand. It is stated that the iron is not a waste product, but it is made by machines designed for the purpose.</p>
        <p>flow to Bend a Pipe Without Buckling. In order to bend metal pipe without buckling it is necessary to fill it with some substance which can be easily removed after the bending is accomplished: Sand or resin is commonly used, but in Germany a new method 'has been originated which consists in filling the pipe with water under pressure. Not only does the water keep the pipe from buckling, but a test of water-tightness and pressure resistance is thus applied at the same time.--" Science Siftings."</p>
        <p>The True Philosopher's Stone.</p>
        <p>Professor Soddy makes the assertion boldly that it is only a question of application to change lead into gold. He says that while now. we can only -work with electricity at 100,000 volts, it is only a matter of perfecting the method to be able to work at ten times that voltage, when the baser metals can be changed into the more precious. In other words, elec- tricity, when -brought to its highest efficiency and application, is the real Philosopher's Stone for which the alchemists of the Middle Ages searched so long.</p>
        <p>• Cigars Fifteen Inches Long. • Ever smoke an Igorot cigar? asks Power. Well, these lgorots in the Philippine Islands make a cigar that in one respect is like the old-time Christmas plum pudding--it lasts a week. It's 15 inches long and three to five inches thick. Old man I. rolls the cig. and smokes it an hour; then Misses lg. and all the little Igs. take their turns. One smoke like this would last some people a lifetime.</p>
        <p>Desert No Longer.</p>
        <p>There are many young Australians to-day reaping good crops from areas which were marked on the maps -as " deserts " when these young fellows went to school in Australia; In fact, some of the most prosperous districts were not long ago shown on the school maps as "deserts," or uninhabited spaces, but as settle- ment has pushed on, and the landseeker has had to ..go further in for his selection, these blanks have been filled with prosperous settle- ments. Thriving towns are growing here. The same thing applies to Australia as a whole. Maps get out of date rapidly on account of the changes being effected throughout the country.</p>
        <p>Chinese "Ripened" Eggs. The " ripened " eggs 'of the Chinese (says "Science Siftings ") are found in commerce in two varieties—first, coated with a thickish layer of solid black earth; second, encrusted with a greyish-white substancd, evidently produced from barnyard refuse. The contents of the black covered eggs are snow-white; those of Ihe whitish-grey exterior are a glossy jet black inside. ,They are not putrid eggs, as we know putrid eggs, albeit they are thoroughly " ripe " —there's no doubt about that. They are eaten coil or warm, with tea-leaf salad, and can be preserved half a century or longer.</p>
        <p>Antisepsis Applied to Canning Eggs. Under a new system devised by the United States Bureau of Chemistry fresh eggs are now being canned under perfect. conditions. All utensils and receptacles are sterilised and the workers have to wash their hands just as a surgeon washes his before performing an operation. Each egg is candled before being broken; those that look good are broken separately into cups and only the perfect eggs go into the can. A worker who breaks a bad egg must before resuming work sterilise her hands and the instruments she has used. The work is done in cold rooms. When the cans are filled they are sealed and frozen, in which state they can be kept until the bakers need them.</p>
        <p>How Waterproofing Cloth Was Discovered. The waterproofing of cloth, so universal to- day, was unknown 25 years ago (says "Science Siftings"). In 1890, Thomas Fearnley Wiley, of Bedford, we believe, took out the first patent for the process known as " cravenette." lie got the idea from an accident in a, Yorkshire dye-house. Certain materials had been wrongly dyed and the workmen were directed to wash out the surplus logwood colour with alum. After the material had been dried the improve- ment was so Marked that the dyer ordered a repetition of the alum washing. The cloth was sent to the wetting-machine, but the workmen found that they could not wet it. It passed. through the water and came out dry. Thus was the discovery made that cloth could be water- proof and yet remain porous.</p>
        <p>North Pole Wobbles.</p>
        <p>A quarter of a century ago it began to be noticed (says "Science Siftings") that the North Pole is wobbling slightly, and soon afterwards Iwo movements were made out—an annual revolution in an ellipse about 30 feet long, and a revolution in about 428 days in a circle about 26 feet in diameter. Striking confirmation of this shifting of the earth's axis now comes from South Africa. A report of the Cape Observatory shows that on installing a new transit circle some years ago permanent longitude and lati- tude marks were made on the solid rock many feet below the earth's surface, and since then variations of these marks as compared with star observations indicate a revolution of the Pole, as has been calculated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Absent Treatment tor Plants.</head>
        <p>Absent Treatment tor Plants.</p>
        <p>A firm of foreign florists gave an interesting demonstration during the summer. It con- sisted of a huge tin pail of water standing in, the shop window on a table about four feet from the floor, flanging oV•er the edge of the pail were strips of muslin varying in width from one to three inches. These muslin strips were firmly anchored inside the pail by means of weights, while the other end rested on flower pots arranged in a circle around the table. " We are giving this demonstration," the florist told an interviewer, 'to show people how to water their plants when they shut up their houses and go away for four or five days at a time. The water soaks slowly through the muslin into the earth and keeps it at a uniform moisture. The width of the muslin strip varies with the size of the flower pots. A pail of this size will supply all these flowers for a week."</p>
        <p>- • _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS r-7'oeovoivremT CHILDREN S CORNER.L Literary Gleanings. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. t ° 0 ° 0t0,=2,01-64042,ve042,0,0</head>
        <p>WORLD OF LETTERS r-7'oeovoivremT CHILDREN S CORNER.L Literary Gleanings. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. t ° 0 ° 0t0,=2,01-64042,ve042,0,0</p>
        <p>THE NEW, YEAR.. My •dear Childreu, Since last I addressed you we have lett one year behind us and:entered upon anothed. - As you grow 'older you will realise how much these; partings of the way in tine resemble the intrtings of the way upon the high roads of the country. We leave one-long lane, whiCh may have had its pleasant .6(.1 its Ugly spots, and enter upon another, which we have yet te explore. It .is a time for making good resolu- tions; that -is; deciding in our Minds that for tht future we will do our best to have nothinig to do with the ugly spots of -life. This LS largely- in our. own hands. If you nikonly_ re- solve Co liav nothing to do with praclices that bring evil result's 'into your lives and the lives of others, but if you also determine that, . like true men and women—you are all men and women in miniature, you know—you will re-. main true to your good intention, you will be doing your share towards making the world the happy place it ought to be. Will each of you promise to make the new road, wh:ich we shall call 1914, as pleasant and agreeable as possible? If you really .promise , you.will have more than half succeeded. .. Your loving uNcLE- -JACK.</p>
        <p>• KITTY KNEW. "Seven sheep were standing By the pasture wall, Tell me," said the teacher,</p>
        <p>. . To her scholars small— . " One poor sheep was frightened, , Jumped and.ran away, One from seven—how many Woolly sheep Would stay?"_ .</p>
        <p>Up went Kitty's fingers— A farmer's daughter she, Not so bright at figures As she aught to be. • "Please, ma'am "---" Well, then Kitty, Tell us\if you know." "Please, if one jumped over, All the rest would go."</p>
        <p>• . IN THE WELL. Skip Gratifies His Curiosity. It was Saturday morning, and Roger Mason and Skip,,the little water-spaniel, were on their way to the station to meet Fred. , It had been nearly a month since the younger boy had gone to visit Uncle Henry, and now he was coming home. Roger's heart beat fast with joy and even Skip seemed to know that there was good reason for being happy. Roger crossed the new ',ridge, bright with its coat of red paint, and was soon at the station. When the train rushed in, Fred, who had come all the way in the care of a friendly conductor, stepped off. The first thing that he said was, "My! How Skip has grown!" 'Hie spaniel knew that' thZy were saying nice things about him, and:he wagged his tail with all his might, find ,barked in answer. _ Soon they -were on the road home, and, Fred was listening to Roger's stories of the adven- tures he. had had with their companion; Skip. Before the _red bridge was reached, the boys and the dog came to an old farmhOuSe .where no one lived. The roof had sagged, and nearly all the windows were broken, and the paint on the walls had long since been washed away by the rains. In front of it, under the great maples that-. shaded the- yard, was a well of ice-cold water. " Isn't,',iLhot,2aid Fred, squinting up at the *sun. 'Let's go in under those trees, and try to -',get a drinic otwater." He led the way into the yard. On'a nail in one of the trees hung a pail, with :a string tied to its handle: A moment later the boys were drinking—sit:Any, because the water was almost ice-cold. ' It was 'aideep Av'ell, walled with rough stones; and down at the bottom, in the round mirror of water, they could see their own laces, as they stood cautiously near the edge. A cool, moist. mossy smell flouted up front its. depths. skip conic panting from the squirrel thicket and stood beside them. Roger poured -half a pailful of water into a cup-like hollow in a flat rock, and the spaniel lapped .thirstilt. 'Then he looked over tne edge and saw his own furry ilead mirrored in -the pool-below. lie barked, and the dog at the bottom of the Well opened its mouth and seemed.to answer. Skip danced ex- citedly, barking louder than ever, while his two masters shouted with laughter. Suddenly, almost overhead, in one of tue maples, a bird, frightened by the noise, flew twittering from its nest. The boys turned to gaze after it. In that same instant they heard a strange yelp from the ,little Spaniel, mingled with the sound of a falling stone. 'they looked round. Skip was gone from the edge of the Well! A moment before he had been almost at their feet, and now there was only an ugly, fresh mark in the earth where one of the stones had slipped from the edge. They dreaded to look down, but a tiny splashing sound came from below, and they peered over the brink. There—very far relow, it scented—was Skip', swimming bravely in a little circle. twice he tried to climb the rough rocks that lined, the well, but each time he slipped back, after pawing vainly for a foot- hold. As lie paddled round and round, he whined in such a piteous way that the tears came to the boys' eyes. "The rope !" cried Roger. Ile had no sooner spoken than Fred began to lower it with the pail at the end; but it was a very small pail, and although Skip made for it the moment it touched the water, and tried to hold on with teeth and paws, all his efforts seemed of little use. Every time they tried to pull it up, the spaniel -dung desptFately for a moment, then fell back, and disappeared with a_splash. Again and again they tried, but all in vain. The last time Skip did not even have the strength to swam for the pail, but clung motionless to a rock, with little more than his nose and two appealing eyes showing. "We must get something else," said Fred, trying to keep the sob from his ,woice. He ran toward the farmhouse, and looked about. An old peach-basket was lying beside the rickety steps. fie seized it, and rushed back to Roger. Quickly they tied the cord to its rim, though their fingers trembled from fear that they were - too late.</p>
        <p>"here I" said Roger, as he placed a stone in the bottom. "That will make it sink so that .t7e can get it under him." Together they lowered the basket, while Skip, who was too exhausted to swim, watched it come down with eyes that showed that he real- ised that this Was his last chance. The basket sank. The boys moved it carefully under him, and began to pull it upward. " Hes in!" shouted Fred.</p>
        <p>The load was heavy_for the slender cord, and</p>
        <p>they lifted slowly, hoping that it ivould not break. A moment later they stepped bheli from the edge with their strange basket-load. _ . .</p>
        <p>- Little Skip could only wag his tail at first, but ten minute's in a sunny spot by the road with his two happy masters rubbing him down, and wiping the water from his fur, and patting him again and again, quite brought back his liveliness, and barking and frisking round Fred and Roger, he thanked them plainly in his dog language. Then the three companions started home; the sun seemed to shine brighter than. ever, and a great deal of joy. was in-their hearts.</p>
        <p>DoN'Y Long OLD -Keep the raves of time at bay . darkening the grey streaks With LOCKSER'SSOLYFILTIt RESTORT:ii,whieh deepens to a'naturaleolourin a few days. Lock ver's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bot everywhere.rost free 1/6 I.3adtor4 Laboratoryjouilea,k). _ _</p>
