      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>COFFEE AS A RESTORATIVE.</head>
        <p>COFFEE AS A RESTORATIVE.</p>
        <p>The "Lancet." sets forth an investigation into the composition of the infusion of coffee. In drawing a comparison between coffee and tea, the results show that while the alkaloid caffeine in coffee is identical with that in tea, its asso- ciations in coffee are quite differenl. In tea infusions the alkaloid occurs chiefly as tannate, which is a compound insoluble in the acid juices of the stomach. .In coffee it occurs as an easily soluble compound. Here : would appear to be the explanation of the prompt action of coffee as a restorative and stimulant, particularly when employed as an antidote to narcotic poisons. In the same way coffee pro- bably seryes to overcome to some extent the narcotic effect of alcohol, and to render a per- son Wakeful owing to its stimulating effects upon the central nervous system.</p>
        <p>THE "BLACK FOX" SPECIAL. Those who do not know of the vast fortunes that have been made, and are now being made, in the fur trade cab hardly realise the tremen- dous interest that is being taken in Prince Edward Island in what is known as the Black Fox •boom. . .</p>
        <p>As an indication of the enormous business I being done in Black Fox a novel special -train was recently run in the Prince Edward Island division of Me Gunadian Government Railways. I he train, consisting of a number of baggage ears, transported a million dollars worth of foxes to their new qUarters at a distance of about 50 miles, The railway employes gave the train and its valuable contents us much attention as if it were a royal train. The owners of the aniintlls, some of which are said to be. worth 25,000 dollars, take as much care of them as the average mother does of her first baby, Most of the black foxes are now held by joint stock companies, proved breeders being capital- ised at from 20 to 30 thousand dollars a pair, and most of the pups at from 10 to 15 thousand the pair, though sonic range higher. Of the companies which 116.ve been in operation foi over a year, all, with the exception of several smaller ones, have paid dividends this autumn ranging from 20 per cent. upward. One com- pany this autumn distributed to its shareholdiars 500 per cent, in cash.</p>
        <p>STUTTERING. AND HOW IT MAY BE CURED. There is no cure for stuttering. This does not mean that no stutterer can be cured—far from it—but that there is no treatment Which is•sure to cure. If a stutterer be taken in child- hood when the affliction is first noticed and carefully treated he can sometimes' be cured and generally much improved. Dr. Frank A. Bryant, of New—York, writing in the "Medical Record," says the •first thing to do is to make sure that there are no obstruc- tions in throat or nose, such as enlarged tonsils or adenoids. The child must be taught to breathe through his nose, deeply 'and slowly, as a habit. He must not be allowed to speak when excited, nor when laughing or crying. or in the paroxysms of whooping cough.: 'lle must never be tickled. All causes of excite- ment must be removed. Fresh air, scrupulous cleanliness, plain, nourishing food, moderate exercise, and plenty of sleep in a, dark room are essential. Mental treatment is of great. importance. Any measures that will increase mental poise are of incomparable value. The Stutterer must be impressed with a desire to overcome what is only a bad habit. Ile must be persuaded to study the great art of speaking correkly. Thus, by careful, patient work on the part of his parents, teachers and physicians, will he gradually cure himself, or, at least, ,so improve as to make the affliction cease to be serious:</p>
        <p>Nt. THE SPEAKER AND COTTAGE BUILDING. With very great pleasure., we are able to announce that Mr. James Lowther, the Speaker of the House of Commons, has in the most practical way signified his approval of our- National Cottage Building Competition by offer- ing to build n'pair of cottages in Suffolk. This will be an invaluable help in the movement to ensure that the new cottages are worthy of the English nation and fit dwellings for the English peasantry. Apart from this, there is a peculiar pleasure about planning a pair of cottages for Suffolk. The county has been Called ." silly Suffolk " ; but " silly" in this case is only a bad pronunciation of the Gernian word "Sag," and the meaning of the phrase is "happy Suffolk." In no other county of England do the farm- houses and the cottages nestle so snugly by the woodlands and -by the wayside; nowhere do they fit in more aptly with the natural sur- roundings. It follows that nowhere would tile and brick-built boxes be more obnoxious. The character of the county absolutely demands that the buildings should accord with the land- scape. The accommodation required for a Suffolk labourer's family must be precisely what is needed for a similar one elsewhere.— Country Life."</p>
      </div>
