      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TO TAKE</head>
        <p>TO TAKE</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 3, 1914.</p>
        <p>HOLIDAY TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Old Man Found Dying in an</p>
        <p>Entry.</p>
        <p>ARREST ON SUSPICION</p>
        <p>From a poor quarter of the town comes the report of an old man being found dying" in an entry, of his death in a neighbouring house, and of the apprehension of a young married man on suspicion of having caused the death. The prisoner was Albert Beckett, 18, Ship- yard. At the Borough Police Court on Monday he was remanded for a week charged on stis- picion with having caused the death of Martin Mahon by kicking him on the body in an entry in Ship-yard on Saturday, December 27.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that an old man named Martin Mahon was found between seven and eight o'clock very ill in an entry in Ship- yard. It was alleged that prisoner's wife came out of a house and stumbled against the old man, who was on the floor. She screamed, and her husband was alleged to have rushed out of the house and to have kicked the old man three times in the ribs. A woman named Kelly ,had given a statement to the police to that effect. The old man was removed to a house near by, tlnd he subsequently died. He had been seen by the police surgeon, but up to the post-mortem was mpirnaoci examination tpireeaslel yn t nil.the result voef r hae tion would be held and the facts laid before the Bench. Sergt. Reddington went to pris- oner's lodgings on Saturday night, and before the Sergeant could say anything prisoner ex- claimed, "I know what you want me for, it is about that old man who was found in the entry."</p>
        <p>The prisoner pleaded not guilty, and wanted to call his wife as a witness.</p>
        <p>The Mayor pointed out to him that it would be better if he consented to a remand, and con- sequently he was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST. Coroner Explains the Matter in Dispute.</p>
        <p>Death from Natural Causes.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held on Tuesday afternoon, before Deputy Coroner F. A. Jones. The pris- oner, who was accommodated with a :teat in the body of the room, appeared to be in a highly- strung condition. He was not legally repre- sented. The Deputy Coroner said the deceased, Mar- tin Mahon, lived at 18, Ship-yard, and was 73 years of age. He had stayed in the house until about six o'clock, when he went out for a shave, and in order to get back to the house he had to go through the entry. There was a conflict of evidence as to what actually took place, some witnesses saying they saw Beckett kick the old man, whilst other witnesses said that the old man staggered and fell, and Beckett's wife came out of the house and stumbled against ,him. She was under the impression that some- one was trying to take dvantage of her and ,screamed. Beckett came out, and what he a really did was the matter in dispute. Patrick Cain, 25, Dolman's-lane, said he was in relative of the deceased, who had been in fail- ing health for about six years. Deceased was a native of Ireland, but had lived in England for 50 years. The Deputy Coroner asked Beckett if luk„.was legally represented, and he replied that he did ,not need to be.</p>
        <p>Death Due to Natural Causes. ! Dr. R. Smith, police surgeon, stated that he made a post-mortem the previous day. The body was very Well nourished. There were .no marks of violence or signs of internal or ex- ternal injuries. ,The heart, Weighed 18 ounces, instead of the usual eight ror ten ounces. De- ceased must have been suffering from some chronic disease, probably chronic bronchitis. ,The lungs showed signs of suffering from double pneumonia and pleurisy. In his opinion death was due to failure of the heart's action in con- sequence of the diseased lungs. The Deputy Coroner: That is natural causes? —Yes. Supposing the old man had been kicked, would it in any way have hastened or acceler- ated death?—Not in my opinion.</p>
        <p>First ." Bother" She Had Seen. Mary Kelly, a young married woman, who appeared in the witness-box with a child in her arms, said she lived at 12, Ship-yard, and acted as deputy for Mrs. Rogerson at the lodging - houses, 12 and 14, Ship-yard. At about a quar- ter to eight o'clock the previous Saturday night she heard a woman scream in a passagE. and saw Beckett rush out of the house to an old man, who was lying on the ground. She saw Beckett kick him three times in the ribs, and heard him say "Get up and fight me like a man." The deceased was taken to a house, 18, Ship-yard, and a woman shouted, "It is an old man you have kicked." Beckett was in this house at the time, and he said "Well, I have killed the old man." Later witness heard Beckett say, "I wfll be brought up for man- slaughter for this," whereupon his wife told him to hush and go home. Witness bad lived in the district foi; only three weeks, and Beckett was a stranger to her. This was the first bother 3he had seen there.</p>
        <p>"I Will Give Myself L 113." he Thomas Hennessey, 1 lived with his father, who was the lodgng- house keeper. , He heard the womaii scream and i shout "I have fallen over 14 a mhialnrd n the said Alfred Beckett ran out of the house, and into the entry, and witness heard two.thuds as if of kicks. They sounded td come from the middle f the entry.' He heard Beckett say "I will ?lye .myself up." Later he asked him if he Ship-yard, entry. l" kicked the deceased, and he replied " Yes."</p>
        <p>Doctor's Evidence Conclusive." The Deputy Coroner said that they were there to inquire into the cause of death, and the medical evidence showed that deceased died from natural causes. He Suggested that it war, unnecessary to call the other witnesses, for if the deceased had been kicked the doctor said that it would in no way have hastened death. If death had been accelerated by a blow only for one second it would have been different. flowerer, it seemed to him to be wasting the time of the jury and his own time to call the other witnesses.</p>
        <p>A verdict in accordance with the medical evidence was returned.</p>
        <p>BECKETT DISCHARGED, BUT DESCRIBED</p>
        <p>AS " A CONTEMPTIBLE COWARD."</p>
        <p>Beckett appeared before the magistrates on Thursday morning and was discharged. The Chief Constable stated the .facts' of the. case, and recalled the inquest. He thought prisoner ought to be severely admonished for his part in the affair. They were unable to proceed with. the case. After evidence had been given by Mary Kelly and Thomas Hennessey, The Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer- Young), ad- dressing .prisoner, said.: You have had a nar- row escape. The evidence clearly proves that you did commit a brutal assault on the old man. I don't know Wliat your ideas of inanli- uess are, but to ,find you can come to kick an old man lying ill the entry marks you, in my estimation, as a contemptible coward. I don't know what your fighting capabilities are with your comrades, but you are a brave man when there is an old man on the ground. I hope you will always remember the miserable inci- dent which brought you here to-day. The court look on your conduct with extreme dis- satisfaction and contempt. -Thu are dis- charged.</p>
      </div>
