      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EGG-LAYING COMPETITION</head>
        <p>EGG-LAYING COMPETITION</p>
        <p>A Contest Open to All the</p>
        <p>World.</p>
        <p>BROODY HENS.</p>
        <p>When hens. lay soft eggs it is an indication that there is a shortage of lime, and this defi- ciency may be supplied by giving the birds crushed oyster shell. Green or vegetable food is necessary for fowls to keep them in good health, and prevent many distressing complaints like crop-binding, &amp;c. Cabbage leaves are always obtainable and a turnip cut in half for .the hens to peck at will be beneficial. The vegetable food is better hung up on a string, and keeps the fowls in good exercise. Boiled turnips, mashed, mixed with hot meal make a good early morning feed. To induee birds to lay, and also keep them in .good condition, a small piece of sulphate of iron, the size of a small bean, should be placed in the drinking water, to each pint and a half. This may be given two or three times a week. CleanNi over theoutaa theflhr d d ouasne frequently,uner i and sprinkle all oh perches dry sifted ashes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EGG-LAYING COMPETITION OPEN TO WORLD.</head>
        <p>EGG-LAYING COMPETITION OPEN TO WORLD.</p>
        <p>An egg-laying competition, open to the world, has been arranged by the South Australian Government to commence in April, 1914. In previous competitions organised by the various State Governments in Australia, sonic world's records have been established. Various new features will be introduced into the tests. Sec- tion 1, open to the world, 10 pullets, of any breeding fr olnvcompetitionpen(he prizes rang- ing is limited txci on ntsot i ult each The to 30 entries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>BROODY LIENS.</head>
        <p>BROODY LIENS.</p>
        <p>The device adopted for dealing with broody liens is the familiar sparred coop, the bird being liept in full sight f the other fowls. The result is that • three days' time is generally enough to cure the most inveterate sitter. A variation of the, sparred Coop- treatment is to suspend the coop on a line, and If the ordinary plan is not found to answer, this is almost certain to be effective. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>FEEDING.</head>
        <p>FEEDING.</p>
        <p>_ The morning food is a soft mash, given about 7.30; this is composed of different meals, varied according to breed and age and other circum- stances. Some of the birds are given a little ineat meal at dinner time at this season to make up for the absence of Insect life. In the after- noon, about 3.30 or 4, all the old birds have hard grain, oats, wheat, a little maize, and one ot two mixtures. The growing stock have soft food in the evening as a third meal. The manure is all disposed of by sale, hut for in- dustrial purposes—leather tanning—and not for use cttiNeNna trolcynocftohtnehi.eef riinvraenl duLet 00 ft teauihree manure, are not suffi- ciently Curiously, the farmers, if any trouble to get it. The brooder-house is heated by hot water. The breeders are littered so as to bring the young chicks close to the PiPos, and runs are provided for them, so that they can have exer- cise at will. There is accommodation here for 900 birds, which, as a rule, occupy the place for about a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>FOWLS WITHOUT FEATHERS FROM NOVA</head>
        <p>FOWLS WITHOUT FEATHERS FROM NOVA</p>
        <p>SCOTIA. Have you ever eaten a "Digby Chicken?" One of Canada's trade commissioners on this side has been making suggestions regarding this alleged fowl in his report to the Trade and Commerce Department at Ottawa. A bulletin issued by the Department contains the follow- ing paragraph Sent from Glasgow by Mr. J. E. Boy:</p>
        <p>"Some years ago an extensive trade in ' Digby Chickens'. was transacted with Nova Scotia, but apparently it has for sonic reason eeitsed. Inquiries made aillong Poultry dealers . eTvuar ill debde reveal Idesire to receive quotations from Cana- dian exporters If offers arcthf office in time for CliristMas before probable purchasers. Turkeys are de- sired.chielly." . _ tpol placed</p>
