      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CANADIAN DON'TS FOR INVESTORS AND</head>
        <p>CANADIAN DON'TS FOR INVESTORS AND</p>
        <p>SETTLERS.</p>
        <p>, Some useful hints to those contemplating in- vestments or settlement in Canada were given , at a luncheon held recently in connection with the Royal Colonial Institute in London by the 1.19 A. W. E. Scott, Deputy Minister of Agrieul- tiTre for British Columbia :.--- "Do not," he said, "put money into any- thing without careful investigation of its merits."</p>
        <p>"Do not expect 100 time without risks." per cent. profit in a short "Do not buy agri'cu lua t I lands without first r Y seeing them and satisfying yourself that they are as represented. If you want to buy land and cannot inspect it for competent and value." judge to report as to its capabilities farming and f t the best results from ledge of this if you have no know- aswing ourself, employ a " Do not expect to ge to profits. There are good P business." " Do not believe all the statements you hear circulated but there have been a. number of statements which rofits to be made, "Do net Put Etre net justifiable."all your money into land. Enough should be retained to provide for stock- ing, implements, and living expenses until the returns begin to come in." Mr. Scott added some " don'ts " for parents who are thinking ef sending their sons over- sea:—</p>
        <p>Do not send your boy out just because you do not know what to do with him at home," he said. " SatisfY liking for outdooYourself that he has a natural for it. r life, and is physically adapted " knowing that h e has only "Do not send him out with plenty of money, get it. Throw him to apply for more to on his own resources. "Do not pay a premium. If the boy, has any- thing in him at all lie is worth his board." "Do not send a boy to an agricultural college in this country. Conditions of farming here and in Canada are altogether different."</p>
