      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN,</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN,</p>
        <p>FORWARD CROPS.</p>
        <p>The wheat crop is very forward, thanks to the very open weather we have had. This is not a source of much anxiety, but still farmers would welcome on its account a spell of frost, for too forward a condition at this stage is not favourable ,to .the production of a good crop, and there are other reasons why a hardening of the ground would be helpful. Carting is thereby rendered more easy, and less damage is done to the roads and fields. It is not, by the -way, the corn alone which is forward, and a correspondent calls attention to the fact that there is a possibility of serious injury to the vetches owing to the free growth that has taken place, should a fall of snow be recorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BRACKEN AS LITTER.</head>
        <p>BRACKEN AS LITTER.</p>
        <p>Dairying is the staple industry in this part of Cornwall, there being very little grain grown for the market; what is grown is largely for the consumption of stock. Butter-making is the chief form of dairying, so that it is necessary to keep a fairly large number of pigs to consume the milk and whey. This is where a fairly large amount of litter is demanded; the straw is fed'inostly to young stock, with the addition of cal:6' and roots, so that we have 'to find a substitute, which in this locality is usually "bracken." On a farm of fifty acres there may be twenty acres of hill, heath or moor land; this is where the bracken comes from. Its manurial constituents are not quite so high as straw, but its power of retaining moisture is 'slightly higher. It is harvested at three differ- ent stages of ripeness, viz. : (I) Cut when it has ceased growing, but per- fectly green, treated similarly to hay, and stacked. It must be remembered that it cannot be cut with a mower, but with a scythe, a patch here and there, a little around this furze bush, kind a little on that bank; in short, it is a slow process. This is very often done between hay and corn harvest, and is really the best stage at which to cut; but it has its disadvantages, for if cut regularly at this stage they die out. A striking example of this may be seen in pas- tures; in Some districts, where they have been cut regularly for years, they have died out. There was one field in particular on my own farm that was practically covered, that has been cleared Brough repeated cuttings. I remem- ber having saved a rick once in this state; it smelt almost as sweet as hay, and when put under cattle they ate all around as far as their ,chains would allow.</p>
        <p>(2) Some farni-e-rprefer to cut at the half- ripe stage, just when it is turning from green to brown; this is usually done in the latter part of September, and through October. It is saved as above, but it is not so lasting, being more brittle.</p>
        <p>(3) Sonic eut when it is perfectly ripe and stack at the same time; this is not to be recom- mended, as the bracken gets gradually changed in composition, having a higher percentage of lignirl or woody fibre. Also its power of retain- !hg moisture has diminished, for the bracken gets "weathered," and loses its leaves, while there is little left but sticks. This is done as late in the season as the third week in Novem- ber, when the weather is fine. Farmers in this part stack approximately twenty waggon-loads of bracken, and this is suMeient to last through late spring and early summer, when the stack- yards are empty.—Penderleath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Slugs do much damage at this time of the year—among rock or dwarf growing plants. they he concealed during the day under the foliage, and if the soil beneath the plants is dusted occasionally with dry wood-ashes, mixed with fresh soot, their ravages4vill be dimin- ished if not entirely eradicated. The potato is the best crop to plant on a foul or weedy ground. It clears the land better than anything else. Grass-grown land turned over and cropped with this vegetable saves a lot of time in clearing the land. There seems to be no limit to the hybridist. New forms and superior qualities in plants are annually introduced—the result of hybridising and selecting. From the tiny wild heartsease the whole race of pansies and violas have been proaced. The various dahlias are discarded from one species, and the beautiful sweet peas owe their origin to the humble lathyrus odora- WS.</p>
        <p>The long-flowering growths of the winter yel- low jasmine should be loosely tied !n, as some protection against strong winds. " -</p>
        <p>Frames containing cuttings should have a free circulation of air to prevent damping off. Pick off all dead leaves and keep the soil free of weeds. Campanula alba makes an excellent hanging plant for a window. When in flower it is one mass of white star-like blooms. House ferns should never be allowed to get dry enotigh for the leaves to show signs of decay at any period of the year, and more espe- cially during the growing season. Provided with good drainage, they may be given a good soaking from May until September, when they do not require so much moisture. The soil should not be allowed to become hard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>" SPORTS " IN FLOWERS.</head>
        <p>" SPORTS " IN FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>The remarkable tendency of some plants to "sport "—to produce a growth which gives rise to flowers that are of a different colour, is one of the mysteries of horticulture. It is more common with the chrysanthemum than any other flowers. A similar thing happens to phmts raised from seeds, which is under- standable, the flowers being crossed by insects. But how is the following remarkable curious instance of reversion to be .accounted for. At the last exhibition of the perpetual flowering carnation a lovely new variety, named Queen Alexandria, rich salmon pink, was described by the introducer 6s a "sport" from Scarlet Glow, which has a rich crimson flower, near enough to be called scarlet. Yet plants raised from zuttings taken from the parent come true to colour. What would happen to seedlings raised from it, would be interesting to know.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BEST DAHLIAS.</head>
        <p>THE BEST DAHLIAS.</p>
        <p>_ During the past season an important trial Of dahlias was conducted by the Boyal Horticul- tural Society and the National Dahlia Society, near Cardiff. Blooms were shown from all over Europe, one thousand useful garden varieties acing included. The following is a list of the popular cactus class which received the ap- proval of the expert committee, who examined 250 varieties: Amos Perry, velvety crimson, very free-flowering, 311. ; Arthur Pickard, pale pinkish-salmon, fine habit, 51t. ; Bridal Crown, yellow shaded pink, 411, ; Conquest, deep crim- son-maroon, very erect, 411. ; Duchess of Marl- borough, delicate silvery pink, fine habit, 411. ; :lith Carter, yellow, heavily suffused rosy car- mine, 411. ; Effective, amber with rose centre, t.;lf Floradora, rich crimson, long wiry stems. 31t. ; Glow, s yellow tipped apricot, 31t. ; lion. Greville, orange-yellow, suffused salmon, lift.; Jeanne Hardy (Cayeux and Le Clere); \label Needs, orange scarlet, shaded plum, lft ; Mrs. J. Barker, salmon-pink, 211 t. ; Mrs. J. S. Brunton,•deep yellow, 41 t. ; Mrs. D. Flem- ing, fine white, 3ilt. ; Mrs. Chas. Foster, rich rose-pink, 3ift. ; Mrs. J. Goddard, glowing crim- son, 3111. ; Mrs. Landale, yellow, suffused and edged bright rose, 41t. ; Mrs. Macmillan, centre white, deepening to mauve, 411; Mrs. F. Paton, brilliant crimson-scarlet, 41 t. ; Nimrod, bright pink, tinted lemon, 2111; Rene Cayeux (Cayeux and Le Clere); Bev. M. Limon, rich bronzy- yellow, 311. ; Snowdon, pure white, 51t. ; Sportsman, fine scarlet, 51 t. ; Stability, rose- pink, 31t. ; Star, bright bronze-yellow, 41t. ; Sweet Briar, clear pink, 411. ; Sweetheart, sal- mon-pink, 411. ; Ttios. Parkin, bright orange, 31t. ; Victory, rich coral ; Vivid, orange-scarlet ; West Hall Scarlet, bright scarlet, 311. ; W. 11. Treseder, pale yellow, shading to pink at end of petals.</p>
      </div>
