      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DANCING, NEW AND OLD.</head>
        <p>DANCING, NEW AND OLD.</p>
        <p>Kings and Courts have banned the Tango, and the medical Press has issued a warning that it cannot be indulged in with impunity by every- body. For those getting on in years it may put a strain on the heart, and arterial system, that may be dangerous—an objection that would seem to apply to all forms of dancing. None of these vetoes, however, has prevented the Tango from filling a large place at Christmas parties and entertainments. For the young and the elderly who go in for Swedish gymnastics, for the sake of their health or figure, it is but a mild form of exercise. The "new dancing" is certainly having a merry life in society, although it is pretty sure to be a short one. It has nothing in it to compare with the inspiriting music and poetry of motion there is in the waltz, which for generations was the principal item on ballroom programmes, and will no doubt, sooner or later, come into its own again.</p>
        <p>The Cothurn.</p>
        <p>A new dancing shoe, suggested by the Tango, is much worn at afternoon and other dances. It made its first appearance in Paris, 'and was speedily adopted by leaders of fashions It is. classic in origin, and is said to be an almost exfict copy of the Greek sandal. It is cut down very low in front, almost to the toes in fact; is fastened by crossed ribbon, and has very high heels, which, needless to say, are not classic. There are several ways of threading the ribbon; occasionally it is crossed as far as the knee, or it is simply passed under the instep, crossed over and fastened round the ankle; again, it may be threaded through silver or gold eye-let holes, embroidered in the shoe. When the ribbon goes to the knee it has to be pulled uncomfortably tight in order to be kept in place, and the best method, which has the advantage of also being the simplest, is to fasten it under the instep. The Demi-toilette.</p>
        <p>_ The demi-toilette, that for years has been an - -</p>
        <p>indispensable feature of the Frenchwoman's wardrobe, is gradually being recognised as a useful garment for afternoon functions. In London this is more especially the case, for since the advent of telephones and other modern Lime-saving inventions, invitations tend to be- come much less formal than formerly. To be prepared to appear suitably dressed at the theatre or principal meal of the day on a short notice—often given after some afternoon func- tion—is a matter for complacency and congra- tulation. The Zouave Jacket.</p>
        <p>Among revivals of many old and long-for- gotten faShions, that of the Zouave jacket may be noted. It bids fair, in its new guise, to rival the blouse. It is long at the back and loose in front, opens under a vest, often of fur, and has a turned-down collar, edged with the same. Tunics may be said entirely to dominate gowns at the present moment. They are made in an astonishing variety of styles. Lace Rebus and very large jabots are used with fur stoles and cellarettes, for lace has a soften- ing effect, and is, moreover, a favourite com- bination with any and every kind of peltry. Dyed Furs.</p>
        <p>When furs dyed in blues and greens were first exhibited in Paris they met with much adverse criticism. Despite this fact they are steadily making . their way, and are much in evidence on evening wraps, more especially in a mauve shade (rather deeply tinged with red) in pink and in blue. Caracul and fox take any shade of red remarkably well, and are dyed the exact shade of the prevailing colour of the lovely brocades, or embossed velvets with which they are used. The stole and muff of various fabrics •become every day more popular. A particularly pretty set was, of soft black satin, veiled in tulle and trimmed with ermine. A hat to match is frequently added, and is a practical and pretty idea.</p>
        <p>The Vogue of the Earrings. Earrings have for sonic time been steadily growing in favour, and now they are almost universally worn. They have the charm of antiquity and were worn by Egyptians, Greeks, and Bomans, and they certainly improve a woman's appearance. Brooches, bracelets, and rings are undoubtedly beautiful as works of art, but they do not make that particular or marked improvement in a 'Woman's good looks that well-chosen earrings may be depended upon to do. An authority on wornan's dress asserts that there are only two jewelled orna- tnents which definitely increase the beauty of the wearer, and these are the 'tiara, when rather high, and a pair of becoming earrings. Both help to frame the face, and to bring out the best points in hair, complexion and features. Long earrings are preferred just now to the small stud, although the latter is still favoured by some women, for they suit piquant faces with irregular features, whilst the long orna- ments are more becoming to handsome women. The most charming earrings were aisplayed among the gifts for Christmas and the New Year; they were built of all kinds of semi-pre- cious stones, and these were by no means ex- pensive, when their attractiveness was taken into consideration.</p>
        <p>Transformations for the Elderly. The perfection attained in his trade by the furrier is equalled by that of the artist in hair, and this doubtless accounts for the astonishing increase in the number of elderly men and women who have recourse to false locks when their own become scanty. Oldt women are be- ginning to realise that coloured hair does not suit them as well as white, and the demand for the latter has raised the price to as much as six guineas the ounce. Europe sends half a mil- lion pounds of hair to this country every year. The finest white and coloured hair comes from</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL ACTRITIE</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL ACTRITIE</p>
        <p>Over 900 Scholars at Workingmen's Mission.</p>
        <p>The annual Christmas gathering of the Sunday School scholars associated with the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, was held on Monday evening. After tea, to which ample justice was done, Mr. T. Dale ,(senior superin- tendent) presided over a good muster of scholars. and parents.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he was proud to be asso- ciated with such a live Institution, the influence of which was being felt throughout the district. He pointed out the advantages to be derived by scholars retaining their membership with the Sabbath school; and appealed for an earnest consecration of the best of their powers to the highest good. The Secretary (Mr. A. Edwards), in presenting his annual report, stated that the total number of teachers and scholars on the books was 964. This was a slight decrease on last year, although the average .weekly attendance had been higher. Financially, the school was on the rinb4- income elf</p>
        <p>_laving been received, £165 going to meet expenses. Regular and punctual attendance were special features of the school, and for these 227 prizes were presented. Associated with the Warrington Sunday School Union, the school had secured four prizes and eleven certificates at the Scripture examination held in March last, which was open to the scholars attending the Free Churches in the town. During the year many notable events had taken place, from which the Sunday School had derived much benefit. The various organisa- tions associated with the school were in a flour- ishing condition; and to these had been linked up a Boys' Life Brigade, which also formed a unit in the Warrington Free Churches Boys' Brigade. In concluding his report, the Secretary expressed the thanks of the officials to Mrs. John Crosfield, of Colwyn Bay, for the generous sup- port and interest which she continued to give to the school.</p>
        <p>l'an'</p>
        <p>During the evening action songs, pianoforte solo, and songs were rendered by the children. while Mr. H. Edwards amused the audience with a humorous recitation. Games were afterwards indulged in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>CHEST BURNING AND ITCHING.</head>
        <p>CHEST BURNING AND ITCHING.</p>
        <p>11, Queen's-road, Cayersham, Reading, Eng.— "The neck of my baby's dress was too stiff and rubbed the skin open. The dress turned it to a running wet sore. It spread under her arms, behind her ears, and down to the very bottom of her chest. It was very painful causing her to cry at the least touch. It worried me dread- fully. Her chest was all of a burning and itch- ing. Whenever I took her clothes off I had to get some one to hold her hands, because she used to scratch it something awful. She brought the blood lots of times, the rate she used to tear at it.</p>
        <p>".Then one day I was lucky enough to see the advertisement of a free sample of Cuticura Soap and Ointment. The Cuticura Soap and Oint- ment started drying the sores up. I bought three tablets of Cuticura Soap and three tine of Cuticura Ointment and it was quite healed." (signed) Mrs. A. Wharton, July 22, 1913. Cuti- cura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free. Address: F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse-square, London, or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, U.S.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FATALITIES AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>FATALITIES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Three eases of sudden death are reported from St. Helens during the week-end. John Rimmer (54), a joiner, of 31, Oxford-street, and Charles H. Gleave (35), a vanman, of 85, Park-road, were found dead in their bedrooms. Edward Robert Traverse (67), of 31, Napier-street, died in the St. Helens Hospital, having for two years been a pensioner -from the St. Helens Brewery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TRAPPED IN A DOOR.</head>
        <p>TRAPPED IN A DOOR.</p>
        <p>NCTO S (Re. ed. Tradc .114.7.1:J COFFEE ESSENCE</p>
        <p>The Coffee Maid, of Symington's, says: "Sing a song of Symington's, the kettle's on the sine, A little drop of Essence, makes Coffee for a 'IIQS kine." UYII,UNGTON CO., Edinburgh, and London.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court, on Saturday. Patrick Kelly, Powell-street, was fined £2 10s. or two months for riotous conduct on the licensed premises of the Locomotive Inn, Sut- ton, and assaulting Mr. Hayes, the landlord. Mr. Fox, who prosecuted, said that while the police were being conveyed to the place in a motor car the landlord managed to trap Kelly's head in a door and held him there so that he could not do any more violence until the police arrived.</p>
        <p>Iho</p>
