      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>J. Brown, the Reserve Full-back, Meets With an Accident. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BEN JOLLEY AN ABSENTEE.</head>
        <p>BEN JOLLEY AN ABSENTEE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S HALF-SEASON</p>
        <p>RECORD.</p>
        <p>-- First Team Designs on a Position. in the First, Four.</p>
        <p>"A' TEAM MAY BE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>Cll tIIPIONS.</p>
        <p>A LITTLE FRIENOLY CRITICISM. . Except for purposes of comparison,, a half, ,yearly profit and loss account is not alWaYs sat- isfactory, i.e., so far as football is COliairiTe'd.: So much may happen to affect the game and the receipts. If one may judge, however, 'by the half- season's work, the Warrington Committee have much to show for their arduouSr labours. They' have certainly had much to do, and have spent a considerable amount of time in looking after the interests of the club. Both teams have right royally- supported them, and their respective Aiositions in , the League tables speak for themselves. The first team has only just missed getting into ''the " first four," and the "A" team has ce"great chance of coming out champions of .tlie Com- bination. They have both experienced "bard lines," but with better luek in the future they may each accomplish their object. We .sin 7 cerely hope they may. If we may offer a little friendly criticism it is not in a carping spirit—we do sok with the object of giving a spectator's view of the game. It is often said that an ordinary spectator has a better view of the game than the referee or officials of the club. We are not prepared un- duly to press that statement. But we have certain observations to- record, and give them frankly.</p>
        <p>Forwards Overworked, But do More than Well. The forwards, speaking of the first team,- have done more than well—they have :excelled them- selves on more than one occasion. They are speedy, make good use of their feet, and have a fairly good idea of stopping a Man from scor- ing. With a little more initiative in the art of passing they would :be great. They have • just one fault. They must not always want to keep the ball, although, in fairness to them, it should be stated that the backs have not taken advantage of the openings Made for them. Too much has been expected of them, owing to the weakness of the threequarters. They have had Much to do and have done it well. Half-backs:, Need Cohesion. The half-backs, we are speaking now of the t:e'toue yelshmen, somehow or ,other have not not th dowp to their proper game,. There is Would at iinderstal'iding between them that one uch likn • • 8 —. to see, and that one expects•froin .4ecomplisned. players. The ." scrum'! nalf . has - - b een slow and selfish. The " stand— off " has had plenty' of confidence. , but . has thrown the ball about wildly at times. „They must get together and think °lA.:their game. They must know what to do, •Ihen:4lnd I'm" do it, if they are going to be a success. Surely two men ought to know how to combine When Playing regularly together. We hope to see a Marked improvement in their display during :the New year, for we believe they have the ability. - •</p>
        <p>- Weakest Spot in the .Side. . The threeqnarters, as a line, are not a sue- cess• They lack speed, and the art of combina- tion has been lost completely. This is the weakest section ,of the side, and new blood will have to be introduced if the line is to be a success.</p>
        <p>• Jcilley All Right. The full-back is all right. He catches the ball well—though sometimes we think he ought not to wait for the ball to bounce,—and he kicks a good length. Heknows how to get out of a tight corner, and his tackling is safe and sure. The .Combination team is full of triers, and one or two are ready for a trial in the first. NO'RTHERN UNION HONOURS.</p>
        <p>•-- Ve hope that the New Year will be a suc- cessful one for the club, and that the Selection ' Committee of the Northern Union will not overlook the services of several- of, the players in their search for the members who will com- pose the 'contingent to fight the "Northern Union battles overseas. Good health and good luck to all Who have the welfare of the' club at heart! • . - •</p>
        <p>.------•-•-'-'"'---</p>
        <p>By</p>
        <p>The Second Half.</p>
        <p>Fearnley.</p>
        <p>scored against them of any team in the League. Warrington ,had to face a strong wind in the first .half, and with only 12 men, did remarkably well in crossing over with only seven points scored against them. They were early on aggressive, but play was soon • transferred to the railway goal, where a penalty kick for obstruction was awarded Salford, and Willie Thomas put them two points ahead.</p>
        <p>Tranter to the Fore. The. Warrington forwards raided the Salford quarters in dashing style, but a free .Rick re- lieved, and then Loveluck and Mesley raised the hopes of. the Salford section of the croWd by a nice rum,. Still.keeping up the attack, Salford were awarded k-pepalty,- hut --even,. with the assistiince of the Wind Mesley found the range too long. Tranter was next in the picture witlr a good run,—and...Rradshaw. joining issue, the latter Scored 'a Magnificent' try. .George Thcanaa made a good effort to put the ball over the cross- bar, but the wind was too strong, and carried the ball back into the field of play. Another Goal to Salford. • . The Weaste men now took up the running, and play, hovered :on the Warrington line for some time. Launce, the visiting custodian, made a mark, and Mesley put Salford further ahead with a fine kick. 'This put Warrington on their mettle, and a fine passing movement by Loveluck. Thomas and Callender was broken up by -Ruddick. Mesley made another attempt at goal, but was not successful. The visitors e itub e crossing scor d e over,ow however, continued to 'press, but the Warring ton tackling was very safe, the players to a ma down.. midaukyi,nigiuwgie•evautr:ewffours uestssutcoksefupl but Mesley failed to convert. • ,</p>
        <p>With the advantage of the strong wind, War- rington commenced the second half in great style, 4nd had Salford penned in on their own line for some considerable time. The " Reds" were penalised for off-side tactics, and Nicholas was called upon to take the kick. This player fine goal, and thus was s duceldiectlemsesfvuilsitionrsl'alnealida.g Warrington arrington still kept up tde pressure, and Salford made frequent marks to save time situation.</p>
        <p>Keen and Vigorous Play. play was now very keen, and too vigorous to be attractive. After both sides had attacked in turn, Callender got posse”ion, and, beating all opposition, scored a fine try, and Salford were thus leading by five points. Cox and Nicholas Save the Situation.</p>
        <p>Warrington were now putting all in they knew and ,they made a final rally. They worked the ball right on to the Salford line, and, amidst great excitement, COx 'wag succe"ssTul in ground- ing the ball over the line. 'Nicholas was again upon to take the kick, and he -waA Cessful in -landing the goal, amidst great enthn siasm, the game thus ending in a draw, of ten .points each.</p>
        <p>Forwards" Again Prominent. - • The Warrington team did not show that 'fine .form which was exhibited against Wigan, but after so strenuous a week of football one could not expect them to keep that form up. The forwards again showed up well, and considering they were a man short for the major ptirtion of the game, quite held their own with the opposing sextet. Warrington no doubt would have won had they played their full team. As it was, the teafns were well matched and a draw was a fit- ting conclusion to a rather scrappy game. Young Not in His Element. . .</p>
        <p>- Young was ni ot n his element in the mud, but he was always a trier: Dickenson and Tranter again showed fine defensive tactics, but Brad- shaw was 'once more only moderate, though ev.ery'credit is due to him for the way he sup- ported Tranter when he put on the finishing touches to the latter's fine breakaway. - . Ruddick Rather Unfortunate. - Ruddick had not many chances to shine, but what little he did wi's done well. He was rather unfortunate in receiving a nasty knock, and this no doubt was the reason why he went full-back in the second half. Salford's Speedy Threequarters.</p>
        <p>_ The Salford threequarters were much speedier han the home quartet, Callender and Mesley very prominent, but the heavy nature of the ground and the greasy state of the sphere mitigated against any 'good passing being in- dulged in by either, side May played a good game at half-back,' and Laum(e was a good full- back.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" LOSE AT SALFORD. The Combination team journeyed to Weaste, but at the close of hostilities had to acknow- ledge defeat by one try (three points) to nil. Bramhall once more came into the team, taking the place of Brown at full-back. Owing to the wretched state of the ground very little decent football was seen, although from a smart .round of passing by the Warrington backs it looked odds on them breaking down. the Sal- ford defence, Waywell being brought down by Rarrison only inches short of the line. Bevan ought to have put Salford two po,ints ahead from a penalty kirk, but from an easy position in front .of the pests he sent wide. Both de- fences held out during the first half,' amt the teams changed( ends without a point having been registered.</p>
        <p>Tn the second half both sides made commend- able efforts efforts to break through, and at last the "Reds" were successful. Wood scoring a good, try. The goal kick failed, and thiS was all the scoring in an uninteresting genie—,</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S GREAT MATCH. Warrington V. Huddersfield. To-day Huddersfield • visit Wilderspool, the crack team of the N.U., which it is perhaps the chief ambition of Warrington to beat. That Huddersfield are not -quite invincible just now is provided by the fact that on New Year's Day they were-only able to make a draw with St. Helens. Warrington, somewhat easily dis- posed of St. Helens in the Dickenson benefit match by 22 points to 11. But it should be borne in mind that the Warrington success was at home, and that Huddersfield's non-success- ful effort was at St. Helens; also that holiday form cannot always be relied upon. The teams will be selected from the following: Warrington. Jolley. Bradshaw, Daintith, Tranter, Ruddick. Nicholas, Young. I Chesters, Cox, Skelhorne, Thomas, Clare,</p>
        <p>Swindell, Sherwood. ,Clark, Brearley, Higson, Lee, Longstaff, Rogers, Davies, Wildman. Rosenfeld (captain), Gleeson, Moorhouse, Todd Holland. Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>SALFORD NIATCIL</head>
        <p>THE SALFORD NIATCIL</p>
        <p>Considering the strenuous programme the Warrington team have been called upon to per- form during the Christmas holidays, and that they played a man short for nearly the whole of the game, they did remarkably well to run Sal- ford to a draw of 10 Points each on Saturday; , The weather conditions were anything but favourable towards good football, but the game , was -full of excitement; the result of -constantly changing fortunes of 'both sides. The ground was im'a very heavy state, and a , strong wind was blowing from goal to goal. When the teams lined out it was seen that Jolley was absent from his nSual -place at full-back, inquiries eliciting le fact that he was on the injured list; follow- ing on the gruelling he received in the Oldham match.. This was indeed unfortunate for the I,ll.orriesters, and caused keen disappointment nf?ng their spectators. . . . . Brown Unfortunate. .. ._ J• Brown, who has done th Combination , sie de:band team such good service with was brought into the self a him worthy UP to the time of his act dent proved understudy to Jolley. The . game had pot been 'in progress long, however, when Bcoil7n, after a keen tackle, was laid hors .. de e ombat. The ambulance men were soon ren- dering assistance, a doctor also being requisi- , k, tioned, and it was found that Brown had brOken his Collar bone. This was indeed hard lila on ' the fall-back, and much sympathy for him was expressed. I trust he will make a speedy re- covery, as Warrington can ill-afford to lose such : a useful and consistent player- as Brown has . proved during the piesent season.</p>
        <p>Nicholas and Ruddick Play Full-back. This meant a re-arrangement of the team, and Nicholas played in Brown's place for the re- mainder of the first half. Ruddick,was trans- ferred to half-back, and Clare took up the Posi- tion on the left wing. During the second halt, however, Nicholas again partnered Young, and guddicic,filled the role of</p>
        <p>Saliord's Early Lead. Tope ran high that Warrington would avenge last year's defeat, when the " Reds" defeated the home team by 15 points to two. The Salford lads have usually failed at Wilderspool; but on to the present time this season they have proved Itn exceptionally smart side, arid up to Satur- day's game had the smallest number of points</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CROSS CutiN.RY.</head>
        <p>CROSS CutiN.RY.</p>
        <p>- -Warrington A.C. li-ad a fair :sized pack run- ning over about 6i miles in-the neighbourhood of Walton. Vose; Shadv.-ell and GregS'on, of the seniors, and george:and Price, of the junior division,•shoviied good form. Transon, nf a nice pace, led Hayes out on the ,home trail until meeting the pack, when all came through in promising style and finished, in close order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TO-DAY'S FIXTURE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S FIXTURE.</p>
        <p>• . . • The meet with Salford Harriers has been postponed, and all except F. Baker, G. Benett, J. 'George; J. Hayes, A. Inman and F. Price, are particularly reouested ,to .meet at head- quarters at three o'clock prompt. Those men- tioned, represent the, club in the :re-run novice team race of the West Lancashire Association. from the Seamen's Institute. at Runcorn, to- day, and, despite the reported infallibility of Wirral, Runcorn, Northwich, &amp;c., these lads are very hopaful of being able to maintain the local reputation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>POINTS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>POINTS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The competitions arranged to be run in con- junction with the Salford TTarriers inter-club race will be run off on Saturday next from headquarters, if the Salford Club caTmot run from Heat-ley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington • Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Associa ion.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>THE WELCOME FROST. • There was' not a decent bag of fish taken dur- ing the holidays.- The best specimen taken was Mr. Trigger's pike of about silb.. Frost is what anglers were craving fcr during this hack .end, and now we have had a few days of it, let us hope it will make things stir up a bit all roun . It will certainly improve matters' at the Citbviy. 10 the first place it will kill off all weeds., and then the fish will have to take shelter in the deeper holes, :and this is where the winter, angler has the advantage, over the Members who wait until after close season. If this frost continues until the Week-end, we may expect some fair eatehes;' many been keeping back their baits waiting for better conditions.</p>
        <p>GENERAL MEETING. The general meeting will be held in the Old Music Hall, Market-place, on Monday, January sth; chair to be taken at 7.30. Anglers may bring along friends. , BAY HORSE PRIZE LIST. Bream.-1, E. Hill. • Chub.-1 and 2, H. Moores. Trout.-1, T. Adcroft; 2, J. Graham. Perch,-1, J. Sanson;2e W. Sansoii. Roach.-1, IL Moores; E. Hill.. Dace.-1, A. Bradley. Gudgeon.-1, T. Singleton; 2, T. Dixon. Pike.-1, Sansoit; 2, J. Sanson. Special Prize-winners.—W. Sanson,• winner of special for pike (close season); J. Graham, eel (cloSe season);, J. Lewis flounder (close season); F. Jolley, roach (30 mile radius); A. Bradley, dace; J. Green, roach (Norton); E. Hill, .bream; IL Moores, chub; T. Adcroft, trout (Gowy);* T. Dixon, gudgeon, (Norton); J. Samson, perch.</p>
        <p>• ti NOTICE. . Members who have 'not handed in their specials will assist the secretaries if they hand them in without delay. The annual meetings must be fixed as early as possible after the general meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>' HOCKEY. '</head>
        <p>' HOCKEY. '</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD'S CLEVER WIN.</p>
        <p>The first eleven defeated Timperley first by 2-1, this being about one of their best per- formances of the season. . . .</p>
        <p>The game opened with a brisk attack on the Timperley ,goal, Pearson .anq Millar&amp; having good shots cleaied. Knight also made a fine solo rim into the circle, but Was unlucky in shooting just outside the post. 'Timperley at- tacked after- this, and Syers scored off a centre 'from the left. The pressure by the Timperley forwards was acute at this stage, and only some careful defence by Fletcher and Killington, and three splendid " kick" clearances by 13ruhl prevented a further score. The second half was very even, and both defences were hard pressed. Latchford made a spurt, and after some good work on the right Pearson equalised. Timperley made several 'threatening attacks, but no score came, and in the last five minutes the Latchford forwards and halves made a strong rally, and Pearson, with a grand shot, scored the winning goal, that gave the goalkeeper no chaike. For TimPerlq, Hennings, Syers, Tattersall, and Cragg were the best. For Latchford, Knight was in great form at left half, and gave the Timperley right wing a gruelling time of it by his resourceful play. Wilkinson and G. Woodcock, the other halves: also did well, and the latter should make a fine player. He showed very neat stickwork and stuck to Cragg tenaciously. The full-backs and goalkeeper were sound. In the forward line Pearson was very Prominent, and had wretched luck with three shots, but as he got both goals he can rightly claim a big share in the victory. Isher- wood and Furness made a good right wing in the second half.</p>
        <p>Cancelled. The second eleven match bad to be cancelled, owing to the Timperley ground being under</p>
        <p>water. The first eleven match for to-day has been cancelled by -Sale, owing to the ground being engaged for a county-match. Second Team for To-day. The second eleven are at home to Sale, with the following team: G. Mynekyme; P. Allan- son and W. T. Eccles; J. Albinson, W. Tinsley and T. Banks; J. J. McCauley, W. S. Furness, V. 11. Fletcher, W. H. Minards and C. New- bold. Umpire, Mr. H. Eccles. Bully-0ff'2.30.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. SWINTON PARK. Stockton Heath had a weak side out on Satur- day. and after a poor game won by five goals against one. The teams did not meet ;last season. Bradshaw ktwo), Richardson (two), and Partridge scored for Stockton Heath, Tomson scoring for-the visitors. . Stockton Heath are at home to Formby to-day. Bully-off 2.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 3, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>MIEDIE IME. CP IVJE IVJE 3/M 3D,1" Mo IBS 31ERPir Weilt Fir IF{ M •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>score fifteen. minutes from the start. The home team continued to have the best of the game, and were soon attacking - again, when Mortimer, one of the home full-backs, scored with a grand shot thirty yards from goal. The visitors made repeated efforts to get through, but they could not get past the home half-back line. The interval arrived in favour of the Heathens by two goals to none. On resuming, the home forwards played with energy and forced the game towards the visit- ors' goal. The backs cleared with ease, and with the aid of the-wind the forwards set up an attack on the home goal, but they failed to beat down the defence. Ball got possession and transferred to Vernon on the wing, and he made a fine centre, when Ball rushed up and sent the leather into the net, scoring the third goal. After a spell of uninteresting play, the home team made a final rally, and Richardson being in a good position had not much difficulty in registering a fourth goal. Result: Stockton Heath Reserve, four goals; Farnworth Insti- tute, none.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. • Lymm v. Latchford. These teams met in their first League fixture on Boxing Day at Lymm. The visitors won Like toss and decided to play against the wind. The home team were the first to become aggres- sive, and gained an unproductive corner. Robinson cleared, and the visitors transferred play to the home half, and they gained a corner, which was kicked behind. From the goal kick the home team again -pressed, and they were awarded a penalty kick'sigainst liaspell. Owen took the kick and placed the 'ball beyond the reach of. Kite. From the centre kick the home team continued to have the best of the ex- changes, but Robinson and Haspell' defended finely, and no further score was made; the in- terval arriving with tate goal to, none in favour of the home team.</p>
        <p>. On resuming, the visitors had the wind be- hind, them, arid. at once -made tracks to the home end, Haddon being called upon several times to clear. The home team got away on the left did Owen tested Kite with a beauty, which was tipped over the bar. The ensuing corner was 'got away and the visitors carried ' the play into the home half... AMU], on the Visitors' right, centring welt into joal,-Siddall had not much difficulty in equalising. From midfield the home forwards made a strong effort to regain the lead, but the visitors' backs proved too good for them, and again the home team had to defend their citadel. Webb .sent in a good shot, which Haddon caught inhjs hands, and while in possession of the ball was charged by Siddall, for which the referee siwarded a free kick, when the crowd rushed on the field and caused a temporary stoppage. The remainder. of the game was not very interest- ing, and no further score was made. The game elided in a draw of one goal each. ' Latchford v. Lymm. • The return League fixture between these clubs Was played on New Year's Day, on the Thelwall- lane ground, before a good number of spectators. For the second of the home matches during the holidays the appointed official has ,failed to turn up, and on this occasion Mr. H. Smith, of Latchford, acted as knight Of the whistle. Neither team were fully represented, Hind and Clare being notable absentees on the visitors' side, while E. Webb, Lees, Fogg and Haspell were absent from the home team, Bob Grace, I an old favourite at Latchford, turning out for the first time this season. The home team won the toss and the visitors defended the lane goal. The Lymm forwards were the first away. Denny relieved. Smith got away on the home right. Offside sending them .back, the home forwards again returned to the attack. Smith received the ball from Denny and made a fine centre, and the referee awarded a penalty. Robilison, being entrusted with the kick, made no mistake - and registered the first goal. This reverse 'roused the visithrs, but offside spoiled• many Well-intended efforts, while Robinson and Grace Were defending finely. .Rite had no shots t 0 stop. Play in Midfield was well'contestedon-a t!eacherous surface, and Siddall:.missed a fine opportunity by dallying too long,. Directly after the visitors' backs stood appealing for off- side. Haddon brought off a magnificent save with a shot by I'. Webb, and the ball being quickly- transferred to the other end, Owen missed a fine chance of equalising. The same player compelled Kite to concede a corner. This 'Proved of no advantage and pray was transferred to the visitors' end, where the home inside-left rattled the crossbar with a beautiful shot. Wilson:the visitors' right back, at this,juncture made some fine returns. A few minutes from the interval Haddon brought off a brilliant save in goal, throwing himself full-length at the ball. Half-time arrived with the score one goal to none in favour of the home team. On resuming, the visitors were the first to become aggressive, and for a while they had the best of the play, but so- well did Robinson and Grace defend no serious danger was threat- ened. A couple Of corners to the visitors Veing got away, the leather was taken to the opposite end, when Haddon, rushing out to clear; was Penalised for foul play inside the area. He made amends by saving the penalty kick in fine style. A spell of Even play in midfield ensued, until P. Webb got through . on.,his own and sPoilt a .grand opening at the critical moment by shooting too wide. Several Opportunities near the home goal were not made the best -use of, and many times off-side sent the visitors back. Gradually the home team got the upper hand and confined the closing stages to the visitors' half, and again their , backs stood appealing for offside without any response from the referee. The home insidelleft played on and scored a second goal. Directly after time was called, the result being: Latchford, two goals; Lymm, nil.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Orford St. John's Double Win on New Year's</p>
        <p>- . Day. Orford St. John's played and won two matches on New Year's Day—one in the morn- ing and one in the afternoon—no bad start for the year. In the Morning- the opposing side were the Warrington Co-operative. A. rattling shot .by Atkinson -gave the home 'team an early .lead. Good combination ended in Meachin scoring a Second goal. ' Atkinson scored a third' early in , the second half. A fine .centre by Smith en- abled Tickle to add another, and time arrived: St. John's, four goals; Co-operative, nil. New Springs (Wigan) were the afternoon visitors. The first half was evenly contested, and no goals were scored; but in the second half, although Stretford, the home centre-half, had to be carried off injured. St. John's scored twice through Tickle and Millward. Result: St. John's, two goals; Wigan New Springs, nil. Mr. Essenhigh refereed in both matches.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. Warrington and District League. First Division. Dives v. Barracks; B. Howell. Great Sankey v. Weston; J. Smith. Daresbury v. Crosfield's; R. Rule. Stockton Heath v. Weaver Refining Corn. pany; S. C. Essenhigh. -••- -</p>
        <p>. - Secand Division. Emmanuel 0.8. v. Orford St. John's; T. Pittam. Bank Quay Recs. v. Excelsior; W. Dennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>--- • Stockton Heath (v. Weaver Refining Co., at West Avenue).—Harrison; Mortimer and Jeffer- son; Glover, Denny and Gifford; Woodhead, Jones, Welsby, Smith and Vernon. Reserves: Lockhart and Woodward.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Reserve (v. Runcorn Old Boys, at Runcorn).—Holmes; Broadhurst • and Houghton; Magill, Marsh and West; Sankey, Isherwood, P. Richardson, Rigby and C. Pit- tam.</p>
        <p>Orford Albions (v. Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's, at Grappenhall).—Oriffiths; A. Humphries and J. Humphries; T. Holbrook W. Higham and T. Woods; W. Yates, Smart, Taylor, F. Robin- son and E. Lager. - ' _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Altrincham Amateur Cup—Preliminary Round. At Thelwall-lane. The following players will represent Latchford: Kite; Haspell and Robin- son; Downs, E. Webb and Gaiter; Smith, E. Webb, Lees, Fogg and Siddall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS.</p>
        <p>I wish to convey to all .our readers my best wishes for a happy and prosperous New rear. The holiday programme in the Warrington League has not been a very heavy one. Owing to accidents the Weaver Refining Co. were unable to continue their journey to meet Great Sankey. The Weaver lads had a strong team out, and they were sorely disappointed at not being able to play the match. The outing had been looked upon as a holiday for them.</p>
        <p>The players and supporters at Sankey were disappointed, too. Fairfield United could only muster three players for their match with Orford St. JohWe on Christmas Day. . . .</p>
        <p>The John's weie out at full strength. The Adult School team are to be congratu- lated on their performance in defeating the Heathens on the West Avenue ground. _ _</p>
        <p>The victory is more creditable to the Adalts, as they had only ten players three-parts of the game.</p>
        <p>Secretary Williams is to be sympathised wjth in his misfortune; he had to retire owing to injury early in the game; Jones, the Heathens' centre, missed a glorious chance just before half-time. • Muckley got a similar chance in the second half—but he scored with his,. and that made the difference.</p>
        <p>Hunter was a great force to the Adults' team. It was the Heathens' first League defeat of</p>
        <p>the season. The Barracks team were very well pleased in gaining a point at thevexpense of the Adults. The Soldiers had only a.scratch team out. The Adults were minus the services of Hunter. It is now half-time. of the football season. _ . -• You scarcely realise the fact, bet it's true all the same, • Great Sankey, Adult School and the EV'et- sior are the only teams who have not yet tasted • defeat in the League. Daresbury, Divex and Wilderspool Brewery are the only teams who have not a victory to their credit. In the Warrington and District League, First Division, there have been 187 goals scored. In the Second Division there have been 144 goals scored. In view of some fine goalkeeping by several custodians, this is a fine aggregate for four months' football. The chief scorers in the First Division are as follows: . J. Ditchfield, 9; Datlington, 9; Banner, 9; Rowe, 8; Trafford, 8; Roberts, 8; Poulton, 7; Jones, 6; Lockhart, 6; Forsbaw, 5; Dykes, 5; Ditchfield, 5; T.. Smith, 5; Allcock, .5; Bell- field, 4; Gardiner, 4. The chief scorers in the Second Division are as follows:</p>
        <p>Shuttleworth, 10; Bradshaw, 10; Machin, 9; Leah, 8; Walker, 7; Eceleston, 7; Hankinson," 6; Millward, 6; Isherwood, 6; Debbington, 4; Caldwell, 3; Sudell, 3; O'Neil, 3; Deakin, 3; 'Green, 3. Great Sankey team have pierced their oppo- nent's defence 44 times. Divex have had their defence pierced 46 times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>A Moderate Game at Latchford. . liecreation in their first League match at Thel- wall-lane, which did. not attract as many spec- t was generally anticipated. The eO a°l t:s a i' Christmase• nay Latchford met Crosfield's conditions were not very -suitable, and neither !team was at full strength. The first half .Was evenly contested, :each .side scoring bace, In tile second •portioil, Kite, the home custodian, had no difficult shots to stop, while ! at the visitors' end, cleino, who waS lame; had and .his goal had. several narrow s,t-th(elo,ball hitting the cross-bar - and the LI:;91/ce:I.:Ptg.Yelits., At the finish of a moderate game lloWlanda stood out. as the hest of the visitors' three goals to one. tfohieltilidse; t•aenadill Ww7rrificYl played Well at centi-e half. (la .1 Thutrticularly- e backs -kicked Well under pressing conditions. of the home team, no player stood above' his fellows, although Brandiall and Wtifson, of Grappenhall, and Starkey were • making their first appearance this season for the home team. _</p>
        <p>A Win and a Draw for the Adult School. very interesting match took place at Stock- ton Heath on Boxing Day between the Heath- ens and the Adult School, This was the first meeting of these two clubs and both had an un, defeated League record. Neither team was fully represented. The game to a great extent was spoilt with a strong wind blowing down the field. For three-parts of the game the School lads had only ten men playing, and at half- time neither team had scored. The second por- tion was evenly contested. The School team played better against the Wind than their oppo- i [lents, and were successful in scoring, Muckley adding the point. The home team failed to utilise their chances. It.was during the second half the viSitors had only ten players. • They are to he. congratulated on their victory, which was Well earned by their goOd all-round die-</p>
        <p>play. . • . . On Saturday last the Adult School played their return match with Orford Barracks at Vt ' Owing to the holidays the yiiscit°ol;ia s were not up to their usual playing 4trength, while Hunter was a notable absentee from the'home team owirg to injuries. The Barracks team, aided • by, the wind and treach- drous ground, were leading at he interval by three goals to one, and in the seeond portion; *ith the wind against diem, they were able to stave off defeat. ' The' home players did not adapt themselves to the conditions as was ex- pected of them, and it was only a couple of minutes from time when S. Leigh snapped up : a centre from Bowe and equalised. A draw was a fitting conclusion to the exhibition given. 'lt was in great contrast to the display of the Tome team on the previous day. --. .. I m Plum Pudding Before Football. The Orford St. John's team were given a very, disagreeable surprise hy their neighbours, the rairfield United Club, whose playerg\ failed to turn up for the match on Christmas bay, It oivfasthao vehio.yme plo e m unsportsmanlikeaction3ers.Thematter a will be on the part placed very strongly before the League Com- mittee at their next eeting,.</p>
        <p>,t 3 Oil their new ground at Hood-lane, An Accident Leads to Disappointment. In the North Cheshire League, •Crosfield's Recreation should have met Altrincham St. Abruitnetle elilt latter were unable to raise an eleven, and the match had to be cancelled, much to the disappointment of the Rees- supporters.' In the Warrington and District League, much disappointment was caused to the Great Sankey team and their supporters t,Y the non- appearance of the Weaver Refilling Co.'s team . in the return fixture on the Penketn Recreation when they had travelled Ground. • From information to hand, I understand the oalillottil:eirr two aycomto - It was a disappointment a mile and a half an unfortunate .accident befel IW;areytayev: tetilrliveleellif.merl Players had lleael::d.dt to startedoba 1 them in the breakdown of their conveyance. to the Weaver team, as they had been able to get their best possible eleven together, and they were expecting to have a good game and an etening'S enjoyment in thertown afterwards.</p>
        <p>: unpleasalikn: started withouttthliiretyoffimciiiail_ °id:: tslocaatl In the North Cheshire League, Li . liiford paid a visit to Lymm on Boxing. Da__ , . Y 1 play their first League fixture with their rival .leighbours. LkYnmigmh t. o f the charge official put in an appear- fought, and The game had to be The gathe was strenuonsl whistlertietfeesr,eNel,,hieal aiiiidduciaiel aiNN)i ance At the call was not free from unpleasant incidents from e of lemgeameieui::: the supporters of the of time the score was one goal each, a result which Was not very satisfactory to the visitors. They made a confident appeal for a penalty kick, but it was not allowed by the referee.</p>
        <p>By "Linesman." a fine centre. Richardson, however, shot wide. Owing to a strong wind it was very difficult to control the ball. From a long kick S. Welsby had to concede 'a corner. The visitors failed to improve on it. The School were keeping the ball in the home half, while Denny set Wood- head going with a neat pass... From his •centre Rigby gave Welsby a warm handful, which he got away. From'this relief the visitors, aided by the wind, transferred play to the home area. ..Woodward played well at back and kept the 'visitors out. From a bout of good passing Lockhart tested Welsby with a shot he easily Saved. The leather was again transferred to the opposite end, where the visitors gained a 'couple of corners in quick succession, but they failed to improve upon them, Harrison bringing off a timely save. 'Fi'om the goal kick the home forwards made a raid into the visitors' half, • where Jones got through on his own, but failed. at the critical moment with an open goal in front of him. The interval arrived without any Score being made. On resuming the home team ak once gat into .their opponents' goal area, Lockhart's final -shot going wide. The visitors; now play- ing against the wind, were severely handi- capped owing to Williams having to- retire through injury just before the, expiry of the first half. They played up very 'pluckily under their disadvantages, Hunter being very promi- nent both in play and in giving- instructions to -*his colleagues. They played a better game against the wind than their opponents did. The. home , defence was sorely taxed ~on several occasion's, Harrison doing well between the up- rights. We -home forwards again got. to close quarters with the visitors' defenders, and R. Welsby brought off a grand save' from a good attempt Waodhead. , The game was now ' keenly - contested, the visitors' backs kicking very well, and from a clearance by Hunter, 1 Gosling missed the ball. Banner, fastened on it, and made a splendid attempt"to score. liar-, neon was on the alert and made a grand save • just puder, the-bar, which the,cilltwd applauded. The home. players again got away with the ball I anti gained a corner, Which was unproductive. This brought relief to the visitors' defence, and their forwards' being well fed by •• their half- backs,': Muckley- got --through and l)eathfarni- son with a low khot. From the, centre kick, and with only a few minutes to go, the home- sters tried hard to equalise, but they could not pierce the visitors' defence, sOitl time was called with the result : Adult School, one goal; Stock- ton Heath, none. Adult School 1/. Orford Barracks. These teams met in their return fixture at , Victoria Park last Saturday, and played under bad conditions, a • very strong wind blowing down the geld during, the whole -afternoon.l Neither .team. were at full. strength Owing injuries and holidays. • The following players took.part in the game: ' Adult School : Welsby;- Garnett and Barton; S. Leigh,: J. Leigh aud Dykies; Foster, Warbur- ton, Banner, Muckley,and• Rowe.-. - Orford Barracks: Tansley; Gardiner and Harris; Moodie, Trafford and Maher; Shaw, Dunn, Acton, Midgely and Dyer. •• , • • • The Soldiers won the toss and played with the wind at their backs. Play was at once carried towards the home goal and several long shots were sent wide. The home team now began to have better control 131.4 r the ball, and paid a visit to the visitors' half, where a good shot by Warburton was just wide of the goal: The home team continued to have their share of the game and from a good passing movement Rowe opened the score for the home team. Encour- aged by this success, the home players.played very well against the wind, and 'Ansley, the visitors' goalkeeper, made a very daring save and cleared his lines after his backs were beaten. Play was transferred to the opposite end!, and after Welsby- had saved a couple of long shots, Dunn equalised the score. From the kick off, play was fast and each end was visited. Trafford. sending in a long shot, Bar- ton, one of the home backs, diverted the 'ball in ifs course; and Welsby' wap just too late to pre- vent a score being made. Directly after Midg- ley added a thi.d goal, and at half-time the scores were: Barracks, three goals; Adults, one goal. On resuming, the home team now had the wind behind them and they at once forced the game. The Barracks defenders, playing steadily, were successful for g time in keeping their goal safe. Harris had to concede a cor- ner. S. Leigh took the kick and placed the leather well in the galrriouth,sJ. Leigh heading a fine goal, TansleY having no chance to save. The Adults were continually pegging away, but the visitors' half-backs and backs defended grandly, and it was only a few minutes before the close when Rowe made a low centre, and Banner nipped in and equalised. Time was called immediately after, the result being: ,Adult School, three goals; Orford Barracks, 'three goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Latchford's Holiday Successes</head>
        <p>Latchford's Holiday Successes</p>
        <p>The Latchford .team lourneYed to Altrincham bast Saturday, and met the ,st. Vincent's, and after a well-fought and interesting game, the points were again divided, bO teams scoringth</p>
        <p>soil twice, gaining twice. The Latchford Club have come out of the holiday matches very creditably, playing at If the team n nur oo tes ao nu (t 1 on ca retain their present form there are bright prospects of the championship Coming Latch- ford • way at the end of the season. of a foreignpos possible le six.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION-.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. ID• For Agt Pts Adult School ...... 11. ,1 0 Great Sankey ...... 11 4 . 1 41 5 35 32 12 17 3 Orford Barracks a 5 38 15 2237 129 Stockton Heath... 1 1 22 6 7 Weaver R'f'g Co. !. 20 2 3 21 14 9 Daresbury ............ 7 1 4 33 1 Diva ........... ....... 6 6 0 2 46 0 mOreosstfloetr(R'sußneeosr..n..). ;, 4 4 , 38 8 SECOND DIVISION. P. W' L. D. Goals. For At. Pts. Nrgton Excelsior 87 0 1 '2B 6 15 Bank Quay Rees. _ 2 2 32 14 12 Orford St. Jobn'S 5 1 1 27 6 11 Emmanuel O.R.- 3 4 1 20 15 7 Fairfield United 7 1 10 40 3 St. Darnabas' •••••• ; 4 0 13 17 6 4 0 10 22 4 lardeerlsdpo .... .. 2 3 0 4 20 0</p>
        <p>Heath V. Adult School. met on Boxing Day at West .Avenue their first League fixture, and as neither to ot iisetShterecakt play et:yn aam had lost a League match there some was much interest M the game. Neither team were e t full st reserves , the home club playing m i ththe visitors a-enn(gi , being in a similar position. The School secretary turned out to complete their eleven. , The following players faced each other : a Stockton Heath: Harrison; Goslin and Wood- ward; Glover, Denny and Webby; Woodhead, Lockhart, Jones, Rigby. and W. Richardsou.</p>
        <p>„ Adult School 11. Welshy; Garnett and Bar- ton;• 'J. Leigh, Hunter and •S. Leigh; Rowe, Dykes,' Banner, 'Muckley and R. Williams. The home captain won the toss and decided to play against the wind. Banner set the ball rolling, and after midfield play, Denny passed nicely out to the right, for Woodhead to make</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Second Division. Excelsioi•• V. Fairfield.</head>
        <p>Second Division. Excelsioi•• V. Fairfield.</p>
        <p>The above teams Met in, their return fixture on the ground of the former last :-.,:aturday in , very unfavourable weather and before a small crowd of spectators. The home team- won• the toss and had the advantage of a strong wind behind them. The opening exchanges Were very even, and following a few minutes' paid- 'field play, the home team confined the play to the visitors' half. Leah opened, the score with an easy goal. From the centre kick the home- sters continued to have most of the game, as the visitors made only occasional visits to the home goal, but they could not break down the defence. From a free kick near the penalty area S. Lloyd got possession and scored the second goal with a capital shot. The Fair- field lads made several good attempts to get through, but with the wind against them their efforts were futile, and as the interval was draw- ing near O'Neil notched a third goal. Half- time found the home team leading by three goals to none. For a greater portion of the first half the visitors had only ten players, and on resuming their eleven was made up, and now having the wind in their favour, play was more evenly dis- lributed. The home players, with better - com- bination, played well against the wind, but the , visitors' backs were able to prevent any score being pat up against them, while at the home end their defence had more work to do. Owing to the treacherous conditions of the ground the shooting of the visiting forwards left much to be desired. Boyd, Morris and Rigby distin- guished themselves very well in defence of their goal, and no score had, been made when the whistle blew, the game ending in favour of the home team. Result: Excelsior, three goals; Fairfield, none.</p>
        <p>. • WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. Stockton Heath Reserve V. Farnworth Institute. The meeting of these teams in the above com- petition on the West Avenue Ground attracted a fair number of supporters. The game was played in very blustering weather. The visit- ors set the ball rolling against the wind, and at oitee forced the play into the home area, when Mortimer cleared with a huge kick. The home forwards gained possession and Vernon with a grand run on the left transferred - the play into the visitors' half. Sankey opened the</p>
