      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>WHERE BARGAINS MAY BE OBTAINED.</head>
        <p>WHERE BARGAINS MAY BE OBTAINED.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. J. &amp; W. DUTTON'S SALE.</p>
        <p>It is only reasonable_ to announce sweeping reductions at the conclusion of a season—re- ductions which mean to the purchaser a dis- count of 10, 15, 25, even 50 per cent. No shop succeeds in disposing of all its merchandise before the end of a season, and no well-con- ducted, up-to-date house will carry over large quantities of goods. The surplus must be mini- mised, the stock must be moved, prices which will move it must be made, with the result that customers are able to make necessary pur- chases at prices often below cost. _</p>
        <p>_ This just explains the why and how of the great sale of the type and magnitude organised by the firm of Messrs. J. &amp; W. Dutton—whose advertisement appears in our columns to-day —which commences on Monday next. When it is also realised that there is no trash bought specially for sales, but that every article is bona-fide stock, it will be at once seen that this is an opportunity for the bargain- loving public, which seldom occurs. In the cos- tume department, for instance, fur coats, ready-to-wear gowns, evening and dinner gowns, gic., which have only been purchased this season, are all on offer at reduced prices, in some cases, indeed, at as much as 50 per cent. reduction.</p>
        <p>A very special attraction this January sale will be the offer of Royal Worcester corsets. Although this agency was only taken up this season—a fact which ensures that the entire stock is new and clean—the whole of the stock is reduced on the same lines as the rest of the establishment. And so, right through every department, the bargains speak for themselves. Furs are to be had from as low as 3s. 11d, to as much as 50 guineas, aft which ought to be appreciated now that the cold weather is upon us,</p>
        <p>In the linen department also, Ithough here, particidart the•lrish linens, everything is per- fectly new, the buyer having been personally ever to Ireland and selected the stock, the same advantages prevail.</p>
        <p>ATKINSON'S SALES. On our front page will be found details of the remarkable and genuine bargains that are to be had at the January sale beginning next Wed- nesday at the three establishments of Messrs. J. Atkinson and Son, 128, Bridge-street, 79, Bridge- street, Ind 18, Winwick-street. Low prices are the rule; and at the 79, Bridge-street branch the bulk of the stock, consisting of ladies' coats, .blouses, skirts and golf coats has been sorted into lots, and will be subject to ir special dis- count, in some cases as much as 50 per cent. At all three shops there are blouses in endless variety, and at prices as low as Is. nd.; ladies' tweed coats for 55.; iind paletots from es. 11d.; and children's paletots from 9s. 11d. There will be a big window display at each establishment.</p>
        <p>SALE TIME AT HODGKINSON'S. The winter sale of Messrs. W. Hodgkinson and Son, Ltd., begins on Monday, and the various departments for which this house has long been famous will offer goods at reductions that will be attractive to those in search of real bargains. The very special lines are dress materials, in which some very exceptional offers will be made; ladies' tailoring, in which there will be a sale reduction of 15 per cent.; household linens and blankets and quilts, and pure bedding; hulks' coats and costumes, where, for instance, one satin and two theatre wraps worth 31 guineas may be had for 39/6; and dresses and tunics, in which some articles will be reduced as much as 50 per cent. Children's paletots are subject to substantial reductions and expensive corsets may be had at comparatively cheap rates. Patrons arc asked not to forget the bargain table and to remember that afternoon tea is provided.</p>
        <p>BOYDELL BROS.' TAILORING SALE. It is announced in our advertising columns that Boy-dell Bros.' annual tailoring sale will take place during January, when customers will be allowed a discount of 3s. in the The ordinary course of trade will be followed,• and nothing . will be altered except the price—and that will be reduced.</p>
        <p>MADAMEpILLIE'S WINTER .SALE.</p>
        <p>Madame Lillie, 13, Rylands-street, .has begun her winter sale of up-to-date millinery, which must be cleared regardless of cost.</p>
        <p>THE MISSES WOOD.</p>
        <p>The winter sale of the Misses Wood, 10, Rylands-street, Warrington, begins to-day. All goods hare bgen greatly reduced in trice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-03_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>SERIOUS COLLISION. Four Injured In r Hansom and Taxi</head>
        <p>SERIOUS COLLISION. Four Injured In r Hansom and Taxi</p>
        <p>Smai3L-</p>
        <p>Chester-road was the scene of a serious colli- sion between a taxicab and a hansom on Box- ing night, as a result of which four men were injured. It appears that Mr. Saunders, 151, Ellesmere-road, along. with Mr. Harding, a friend of his from London, chartered Et hansom cab at Central Station to take them to Walton. They were proceeding down Chester-road, when a taxicab belonging to Mr. Lake, and driven by a man named Smith, coming from the opposite direction, collided with the horse vehicle, which belonged to Mr. Connor, and was driven by Joseph Ruane, Dolman's-lane. The hansom was overturned And completely smashed up, the occupants and the driver being thrown violently to the ground. The taxi was badly damaged in front, and both'the drivers of the vehicles wete taken to the Infirmary. Smith was suffering- from concussion of the brain, but has since recovered. _</p>
        <p>• Mr. Saunders and Mr. Yarding were cut and bruised, and were attenTed on the spot by Dr. Langdale. They were afterwards conveyed home in a cab, sent tip. by Mr. Connor. All the victims are progressing as favourably as can be expected.</p>
        <p>A remarkable feature of the accident was that the horse of the hansom escaped entirely without injury.. •</p>
      </div>
