        <p>had five children, whose ages ranged from 16 years to 10 months. Inspector Wainwright said he had known the family for four years, and had frequently cautioned the prisoner about his neglect of his family. The Mayor asked if prisoner was out of work ,entirely through his Own fault, and the Inspec- tor replied it was through drink. Sergt. Metcalfe spoke to executing the war- rant in Bridge-street. Prisoner was then in a drunken condition. He replied "I do not neg- lect my children." Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months. ROBBED A AVIDOW. ' Musician's Artless Impogture Rewarded By "Three Months' Hard." A tale of heartless fraud on a widow was told at the Borough Court on Tuesday, when William Henry liorganti, who was described as a musician, was charged with obtaining food and lodgings between December 17 and 22, to the value of 108., from Mrs. Mary Ann Marsh, 25, Allen-street. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner was before the Court a week previous on an- other charge. On December 17 prisoner went to the house of the prosecutrix in Allen-street, and 'asked if she could take him in as a lodger. Mrs. Marsh, having just lost her husband, and having one lodger, consented, thinking it would help her in the management of her home. She questioned the man, who said he was working at Messrs. Crosfield's works and also that he -wris in the band. Prisoner called at the works on the following day and asked for work. This was after prisoner had deliberately said he was working for the firm. Mrs. Marsh corroborated the Chief _con- stable's statement. - Richard Clark, a lodger at 25, Allen-street, said the prisoner was supposed to work at Messrs. Crosfield's. He followed the prisoner to the works, but lost sight of him there. He also ,met him :in the town when he was sup- posed to be working, and prisoner excused him- self by intimating that he had altered his shift. Prisoner denied that he made any statement with regard to working at Messrs. Crosfield's. A number of previous convictions were re- corded seeink him. Councillor H. Roberta, the chairman, in sen- tencing prisoner to three months' hard labour, said it was a very cruel thing to rob a widow. BOROUGH POLICE COURT TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and T. Locker, and Colonel Edelsten. THE OTHER W031.42.7.—C1ara Wilson, 13, Navi- gation-street, was charged with assaulting Sarah Ann Spencer, 189, Wellfield-street, on December 20th.—Complainant was represented by Mr. Steel, and he stated that Mrs. Spencer had been separated from her husband for 12 years. During part of that time he had been living - with- the defendant. He had not con- tributed a single penny towards his wife's maintenance. On Saturday, December 20th, Mrs: Spencer was in the market, when she met her husband in company with defendant. Wil- son made use of filthy language, and when Mrs. Spencer suggested that the language should be repeated at Market Gate, where there was a policeman, defendant rushed at her and struck her on the face. It was not a matter of serious harm, but of the indignation such a scene coca- eioned.—Defendant, who stated, she only pushed the complainant, was fined 20s. and costs, the advocate's fee being allowed.</p>
