        <p>France, Italy and Bohemia, the coarsest— nearly all black—from China. The Doll Shows. The Press is to-day the fairy godmother of poverty and suffering, as the several exhibitions of dolls in London this winter amply prove. This year the doll show in the Albert Hall eclipsed that of any previous year, and it may truthfully be said its like is seen nowhere else. When it is remembered that the work entailed is all amateur in the true sense of that word, and done for the love of charity, the amazing expenditure of time, skill, taste and industry, as well as money, is a convincing proof that the kindly spirit which has for centuries char- acterised this festive season is not dead yet. • Autumn Weather Predictions. The exceptionally fine weather for which this autumn has been remarkable, verifies an old weather prediction long believed in by ancient prophets, viz., that in whatever quarter the wind sets at Hallow 'een that will be its pre- vailing quarter for the next four months. This year the wind blew from the south-east, and southerly Winds have predominated. Accord- ing to the old prophets, a serene autumn is fol- lowed by a windy winter, and rainy spring, and the latter by a calm summer.</p>
