        <p>CH RlsTmAs. If There Hack Been No Letters NO TRAINS—NO TRAMS. How many joyous holiday-makers at Christ- mastime gave a thought to those hard-working individuals working for the benefit c f the public—who by circumstances were compelled to stick to their more than ordinarily heavy routine work? We suppose comparatively few, and even those who did most likely expressed their sympathy in a terse - comment, "Poor fellows," or "How unfortunate they are." But where would the joys of Christmas be without them? Say, for instance, if the postal strike had taken place and there had been no letters or greetings for us, Christmas would have been very sad for many. Then, again, if those toilers on the railway and in the booking offices had been off duty, there would have been many who would have missed the pleasure of visiting loved ones at home. But with a self-sacrifice which is highly commendable these men per- formed their duties, and the wants of an exact- ing public were satisfied. AN ENLARGED POSTAL STAFF COPES SUCCESSFULLY WITH RECORD WORK. If there is one set of the community whose labours are the most arduous at the festive season it is the Post Office employes. The ordinary staff has to be augmented con- siderably, for tnere are thousands more nits-, sivee and parcels to be handled. This year's traffic constitutes a record, the increase being greater all round. But so well organised were the whole arrangements by the Postmaster, Mr. J. Wyard, that complaints have been com- paratively few. The postnspn worked most assiduously, and many were the expressions of surprise that there were So many deliveries on Christmas Day. The public have been rather more considerate this year in complying with the request to "Post early," which facilitates the speedy deliveries. A special parcels office felt the rush keenly, and motor cars, as well as vans and horse vehicles, were kept busy col- lecting and delivering. At six o'clock on Christmas Eve 50 men were engaged in arrang- ing the letters for stamping, whilst on an or- dinary day there is very little, if any, outward work which has to be dealt with so early. The clerical etaff had supper at the Winmarleigh Cafe after the night mail had been despatched, and before starting on a strenuous Christmas Day the postmen partook of a substantial breakfast, for which Mr. J. E. Hannan catered. LONGER JOURNEYS IN FASHION. -, The railway companies offered special facili- ties for those who wished to spend Christmas away from the town. Although there has been no huge increase, the exodus was great. The excursion bookings were not more than last! year, and this is attributed by the L. and N.-W. Railway officials to the fact that people have I been booking for longer periods The local trams were not running on Christ- mas Day, and although as yet the comparative fortnightly returns are not out, it is surmised that the stoppage on that day will not have made any material difference in the figures. For the six days ending with December 24 the total mileage for the cars was 7.097.2, and for the 'buses 1,339. The fluctuations of trade affect theieturns if the cars, but we were informed that taken generally the figures are satisfactory.</p>
        <p>There is a novelty and picturesqueness about a children's ball which it is difficult to surpass. IThe sight of juveniles gliding gracefully to the delightful strains of a good band is in itself impressive and captivating. On Tuesday even- ing the I'arr Hall presented a gay and festive appearanee on the occasion of the annual Juvenile Ball in aid of the "Children of War- rington Cot" Fund at the Infirmary. Dainty little blondes and brunettes attired in becoming dresses of various soft colours were in the high- est state of happiness, whilst dancing with Sturdy ,youngster a arrayed 'in spotlesti- clothes and linen. There is no doubt as to the success ;of the venture for over 330 juveniles attended, and the galleries, from which the spectators viewed the animated scene, were pretty full. , The Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor and Mrs. Peacock) took a great deal of interest in the proceedings, and at the commencement of the lball received the children with a hearty welcome ;as they marched round the room: Under the able conductorship of Mr. J. C. Taylor,' Messrs. , Crosfield's Band, with a saxophone -qtlartet, ' supplied delightful dance music, including some I of tho latest compositions. The Mayoress received a handsome bouquet of white flowers from the hands of Miss Monica Withinshaw, and the Mayor received a button- hole from Miss Wilmett Fox. Three cheers for the Mayor and Mayoress, called for by Mr. J. If. Smethurst, were lustily; given, the Mayor smilingly acknowledging the cheers of the children. At interVals in the brilliant ball, Professor Sydnem, Manchester, gave two novel interludes. "Chuckles on Chalk" and "Art in a Hurry," the children being surprised at the marvellous rapidity with which the Professor sketched his pictures. As a vocal mimic he was also sue, cessful in eliciting the applause of all. Pro- fessor Weber, of Liverpool, delighted and amused with his ventriloquial performance and his exhibitions of conjuring, his tricks being clever and smart. The M.C. for dancing was Mr. Smethurst, and, with the assistance of Mesdames W. A. Proud, Powell and Sunderland, and the Misses Bolton, Tinsley and Ellison, and the undermentioned contingent of obliging stewards, the function was conducted successfully. The stewards were: Messrs. p. w. Atherton, J. Allison, jun., E. Baker, F. Braceeamp, E. E. Bradshaw, J. A. Brooks, H. Collin, 11. Dolan, w. J. Forshaw, W. Furness, jun., A. H. Fawcett, J. H. J. Hampson, J. H. J. Hampson, jun., A.- Hodgkinson, G. H. Hodgson, G. W. Houghton, J. B. Hughes, F. Raby Jolley, F. G. Kirtland, C. D. Law, J. Lightbuni, J. Lumb, P. V. L. Mathias, T. A. Murray, J. Moore Murray, E. Partridge, T. Parr, W. A. Proud, E. E. Pickton, H. Rogers, I. Sankey, F. Short, J. Smethurst, R. W. Sutherland, W. S. Tinsley, G. 0. Venn, R. J. Venn, and J. G. Withinshaw. The Ladies' Committee, who worked most assiduously for the success of the ball, oonsisted of the following The Mayoress (Mrs. Peacock), Mesdames Joseph Bennett, E. E. Bowden, Brace- camp, Turner Cooper, John Evans, E. J. Fox, H. 'Ferguson, Jackson Holmes, J. Houghton, jun., Harold Jolley, Harold Joseph, Lightburn, F. V, L; Mathias, T. A. Murray, J. Moore Murray Arnold Nield, F. Parsonage, S. A. RobsOn, J. Sankey, James Smethurst, J. H. Smethurst, F. Short, Morley Stevenson, G. F. F. White, E. A. White; Misses P. E. Clarke, Forshaw, Edith Forshaw, Hammond, H. Houghton, Monks, I'linston, D. Reid, Sankey, Smethurst, A. E. guethurst, Spinks and Ethel Spinks. The Honorary Secretary of the ball, Mr. J. IT. Smethurst, to whom great credit is due for , the manner in which he performed his duties, was fortunate in having such an able assistant secretary as Mr. James Houghton, jun. The Winmarleigh Cafes, Ltd., supplied excel- lent refreshments. The youngsters ceased their dancing at 11 o'clock, having had a most enjoyable time. Warrington Driver Exonerated. A sequel to a motor accident, in which a War- rington driver was concerned, was heard at Wid- nes on Monday, when the Deputy Coroner, Mr. F. A. Jones, held an inquest on Edward Dor- man, a chemical' worker, of Broughton Cot- tages, Hilton. A motor van belonging to Messrs. Bennett and Sons, Lovely-lane, Warring- ton, and driven by Herbert Bennett, was passing through Ditton at night, when Herman and a man named Robert Newman were knocked down. Dertaan was•badly injured about the head, and Newiliati had his -thigh broken.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned, and the driver of the van was exonerated from all blame. _</p>
